The rapidly growing cumulative number of COVID-19 in the European Union/EEA and the United Kingdom, from January 1 to March 15, 2020.
The cumulative number of COVID-19 shows similar trends in the countries of the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom, which confirms that although the phase varies based on the country, the COVID-19 pandemic is rapidly worsening in all countries.
Based on Italy’s experience, countries, hospitals and intensive care units should increase their preparedness to increase the number of COVID-19 patients who will need health care, particularly intensive care.
On December 31, 2019, a group of pneumonia cases of unknown pathogens were reported in Wuhan City, Hubei Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention reported that the causative agent, the novel coronavirus, is now referred to as the Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, the disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 infection has been named Coronavirus (COVID-19).
The evidence so far is that almost 80% of individuals with COVID-19 have a mild disease; that is, they have respiratory infections with or without symptoms, and most of them recover.
At approximately 14%, COVID-19 was exacerbated into a more severe illness that requires hospitalization, while the remaining 6% suffer from a serious illness that requires intensive care.
The mortality rate of patients staying in the hospital due to COVID-19 was about 4%.
In this study, we assess the cumulative number of COVID-19 trends in each country in the European Union/European Economic Area (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom and compare them with those from Hubei province, China.
We are also comparing the current number of COVID-19 cases in the European Union/EEA countries and the United Kingdom with the number in Italy between January 31 and March 15, 2020.
Covid-19 cases in the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom
After what happened in China, COVID-19 has increasingly spread geographically and the dynamism of the COVID-19 pandemic in the rest of the world is currently following the dynamism of this country.
On March 11, 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 as a pandemic.
In the Eurosorvillens issue issued on March 5, 2020, Spiteri and others reported the first confirmed European COVID-19 cases according to the World Health Organization’s definition of the case.
In the European Union/European Economic Area, the first three confirmed cases were reported by France on 24 January 2020 of people returning from Wuhan, Hubei Province, in China.
As of March 15, 2020, COVID-19 cases have been detected in all 30 countries of the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom (UK), as between December 31, 2019 to date, 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths have been reported, with 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths in Italy alone.
Acquisition of cumulative number and cumulative rate of COVID-19 cases
At the European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC), the number of reported COVID-19 cases in every country around the world, obtained only from official sources such as the country’s Ministry of Health, national and regional health bodies and the World Health Organization (WHO), is being updated daily at 8:00 a.m.
This data has been used to assess COVID-19 trends in the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom and compare them to those in Italy.
As a measure of the spread of active cases of COVID-19, we calculated the cumulative number of intermittent COVID-19 cases within 14 days, thus taking into account the natural course of COVID-19, in all the countries of the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom, between January 1 and March 15, 2020.
We also provided the cumulative number of cases reported from each country as of March 15, 2020 at 8:00 a.m., comparing it with the number in Italy between January 31 and March 15, 2020.
COVID-19 Trends in the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom
Trends in the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases deducted over a 14-day period in the EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom followed those of Hubei Province (China) (Figure 1).
For the European Union/EEA and the United Kingdom generally, the cumulative number of COVID-19 began to increase around 21 February, then increased sharply on 28 February 2020.
This was often driven by the rapid increase in the number of reported cases from Italy, but all EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom showed trends of a similar increase in the cumulative number of COVID-19 (complementary).
Figure 2 shows the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases in the European Union/EEA and the United Kingdom compared to that in Italy between 31 January and 15 March 2020.
It highlights that as of March 15 at 8:00 a.m., 15 other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom have already reported the total number of cases similar to those found in Italy just 3 weeks before or less.
Our results indicate that the number of reported COVID-19 cases is increasing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
Significant trends in the cumulative number of COVID-19 indicate that the pandemic is worsening at a similar speed in all countries.
This is despite the fact that countries are at different stages, despite the different national public health responses, and the possibility of differing definitions of the situation in different countries and the different protocols to select patients who must be tested for COVID-19, including follow-up testing.
In early March 2020, doctors in the affected areas of Italy described the situation where about 10% of COVID-19 patients needed intensive care and media sources reported that hospitals and intensive care units in those areas have already reached their maximum capacity.
Data on hospital admissions and/or intensive care units for COVID-19 cases are currently available at the EU/EEA level for 6% and 1% of cases, respectively (data not shown).
However, they need to be systematically collected to complement existing observational data focusing on the number of reported cases and deaths.
A 2010–11 study showed great diversity in the availability of intensive and intermediate care beds in Europe, ranging from 29.2 in Germany to 4.2 beds per 100,000 people in Portugal.
This means that countries may have more or less resources than Italy (12.5 beds for intensive care and intermediate care per 100,000 people in 2010–11).
Satisfaction modelling scenarios, with estimates by each EU/EEA country and the United Kingdom of COVID-19 hospitalized 90% risk &gt; to exceed the capacity of intensive care beds, are available in the sixth update to assess the rapid risk of COVID-19 at the European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC).
Since cases compiled so far in certain areas of the European Union/EEA and the United Kingdom, and hospitals and intensive care units typically serve specific regional populations, it is preferable to provide information about cases and intensive care beds at the level of designation of Regional Statistics Units 2 (NUTS-2).
Italy’s experience and current trends in other countries show that the COVID-19 pandemic is rapidly worsening in the European Union/EEA and the United Kingdom.
States, hospitals and intensive care units should therefore prepare themselves for the SARS-CoV-2 sustainable community transition scenario and an increase in the number of COVID-19 COVID-19 patients in need of health care, in particular intensive care, such as in affected areas of Italy.
As indicated in the ECDC’s rapid risk assessment, a rapid, proactive and holistic approach is essential for delaying the spread of SARS-CoV-2 with a shift from containment to dilution, as the expected rapid increase in the number of cases may not provide decision-makers and hospitals with sufficient time to understand, accept, and adapt their response accordingly if they are not implemented in advance.
The rapid risk assessment also lists public health measures to mitigate the impact of the pandemic.
There is a limited opportunity during which countries can increase their surveillance efforts to slow the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce the pressure on health care.
If this fails, health care systems in other EU/EEA countries are likely to face an increase in the number of patients requiring intensive care in the coming days or weeks.
The 2019 coronavirus outbreak (COVID-19), caused by SARS-CoV-2, has killed more than 3,000 people and infected more than 80,000 people in China and elsewhere in the world, causing a humanitarian disaster.
The SARS-CoV-2 virus, similar to the similar virus, SARS-CoV, which infected thousands of people with SARS disease in 2003, can also travel from bats and causes similar symptoms through a similar mechanism.
However, COVID-19 has a lower risk and mortality rate than SARS but is more transmissive and influential among the elderly than the young and among men than among women.
In response to the rapidly growing number of publications related to the emerging disease, this article seeks to provide a comprehensive and timely review of a rapidly evolving research topic.
We will address the basics of epidemiology of the disease, its causes, virology, its diagnosis, treatment, predictions and prevention.
While many questions still need to be answered, we hope that this review will help understand and eliminate a threatening disease.
The Spring Festival on January 25, 2020, has become an unforgettable and unforgettable memory for all Chinese who have been urged to stay indoors throughout the holiday and for many weeks later due to an outbreak of an emerging viral disease.
The virus is very similar to Coronavirus (COV), which caused an outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003; hence, the World Health Organization (WHO) named SARS-CoV-2 on February 11, 2020, and was named COVID-19.
The epidemic began in Wuhan, China, and quickly spread throughout the country, as well as nearly 50 other countries around the world.
As of March 2, 2020, the virus has resulted in 80,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19, with more than 40,000 patients discharged from hospitals and the death of more than 3,000 patients.
The World Health Organization (WHO) warns that COVID-19 is the ‘No. 1 public enemy’ and is likely to be stronger than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), in less than two months, more than 200 COVID-19 papers have been published that have included virology of the disease, its epidemiology, its epidemiology, its etiologies, diagnosis and treatment, since the release of the first report on January 7, 2020, which determined the sequence of the isolated virus through several patients.
This review aims to summarize the research progress in the new subject area which is evolving rapidly.
Whenever possible, we’ll try to compare COVID-19 with SARS and another disease caused by Cove, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS, an outbreak in 2012).
We’ll also discuss what we’ve learned so far in terms of disease prevention and prediction as well as some pressing questions that remain.
Coronaviruses are typically considered non-lethal pathogens in humans and have mainly caused about 15% of the common 4 colds.
However, in this century, we have encountered coronavirus causing severe diseases in humans twice, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which caused an outbreak originally in China in 2003 and Saudi Arabia in 2012, respectively, and has quickly spread to many other countries at a terrifying prevalence and mortality rate.
Therefore, the current COVID-19 is the third outbreak of coronavirus in recorded human history.
As shown in Figure 1.1, groups infected with unidentified pneumonia were first reported by Wuhan on December 31, 2019 to the Chinese National Health Commission.
Seven days later, coronavirus sequencing was released.
On January 15, 2020, Wuhan reported the death of the first case.
In the meantime, the epidemic quickly spread to neighboring cities, provinces and countries.
On 20 January, infections were reported among health care providers, suggesting that the disease could be transmitted from person to person.
On 23 January, Wuhan was shut down with all public transportation suspended.
On 24 January, the first clinical study on the disease reported that out of 41 confirmed cases, only 21 patients were in direct contact with the Wuhan seafood market, which considered the location to initiate the infection from an unknown animal source.
On 30 January, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared an outbreak as a global health emergency.
At the time of the release of this report, the disease has already spread throughout China and about 50 other countries around the world (Figure (Figure 2).
As the situation evolves rapidly, the final scope and severity of the outbreak have yet to be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a multicentre study of 8,866 patients, including 4,021 confirmed COVID-19 patients, provided a more up-to-date explanation for the epidemic (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX_rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 infected people of all age groups, but mainly between 30 and 65 age groups.
Approximately half (47.7%) of those infected (47.7%) were 50 years old, very few were under the age of 20, and only 14 were injured under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 infected more men (0.31/100,000) than women (0.27/100,000).
COVID-19 has spread among groups mainly in and around Hubei.
COVID-19 took an average of 5 (2-9) days from the initiation of the infection to the diagnosis.
Incubation period was 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days.
The average infection time from the beginning until death was 9.5 (4.8-13) days.
The basic breeding number (basic breeding number) was 3.77 (trusty range is 95%: 3.51-4.05), and the primary reproduction number was 2.23-4.82.
The number of casualties increased exponentially before January 23, 2020, in line with the many travel operations before the Spring Festival in China.
The rate of mortality among confirmed patient cases was 1.44% (trustancy range is 95%: 1.10-1.86%), and the adjusted mortality rate for all patients was 3.06% (confidence range is 95%: 2.02-4.59%).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 were gender (male), age (≥60), and acute pneumonia.
Coronaviruses are a family of large and coated viruses that contain the genome of single-chain-positive-directional RNA.
It can be divided into four genera, namely alpha, beta, gamma and delta, and coronaviruses of sexual alpha and beta are known to infect humans.
The coated fork (S) of glycoprotein binds to its cellular receptors: angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE 2) and di-betidel peptidase 4 (DBP 4) for SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, respectively, then membrane mergation occurs.
Viral RNA genome is released into the cytoplasm; after the viral genome is cloned, genomic RNA, accompanied by diabetic envelope proteins and capsid proteins, vesicles containing verions, which merge with the plasma membrane to later release the virus.
The first SARS-CoV-2 genomic sequence was reported on January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 has been found to be a new type of beta-cove with a genetic identity exceeding 99.98% among the 10 sequence samples collected from the original site of the outbreak, the Huanan Seafood Souk.
SARS-CoV-2 is more similar to SARS-CoV than MERS-Cove.
It was revealed through an electronic microscopic examination of the transmission that SARS-CoV-2 is located in ultra-thin sections of the human airway epithelium.
Angiotensin-converting human enzyme 2 has been revealed to be a receptor for SARS-CoV-2 as well as SARS-CoV.
However, the SARS-CoV-2 S protein binds to angiotensin-converting human enzyme 2 weaker than SARS-CoV, which corresponds to the fact that SARS-CoV-2 causes less severe infections in patients than SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form an encoded short protein with orf3b and an orf8-encoded secreted protein.
orf3b in SARS-CoV-2 may play a role in viral pathogenic ability and limit IFNβ expression; however, orf8 does not have any range or pattern that has a known function.
On February 18, 2020, Zhou et al. reported the complete length structure of angiotensin-converting human enzyme 2 under the ultra-cold electronic microscope with 2.9 Å in a composite with B0AT1 amino acid conveyor.
They discovered that the compound, which had open and closed structures, had accumulated to form destruction and that ACE2-B0AT1 could bind the S protein, providing evidence to identify coronavirus and infection.
Sodium-dependent neutral amino acid vector may become a therapeutic target for drug screening to suppress infection with SARS-CoV-2.
The original host and intermediate host
It has been known that both SARS-Cove and MERS-Coff originated from bats and were transported to humans via civets and camels, respectively.
By comparing SARS-CoV-2's dynastic development with other coronaviruses, the bats were considered the original host of SARS-CoV-2, where the new virus corresponds by 96% to the coronaviruses that resemble SARS from bats and are called bat-SL-CoVZX45 and bat-SL-CoVZX21.
However, the intermediate host that helped the virus cross the factional barrier to infect humans remains unknown, and the trajectory of transmission has not yet been explained.
Gee and others proposed snakes as carriers of the virus from bats to humans, which involved recombination within the S protein.
According to a study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, have suggested that squamous antagonists – long-tongued ant-tongue mammals often used in traditional Chinese medicine – are the potential intermediate host of SARS-CoV-2 based on a 99% genetic symmetry in the coronavirus that has been discovered in squamous ant-eangents and in SARS-CoV-2.
Despite this, the difference of 1% spread across the genomine is still significant; therefore, we await conclusive results in concrete evidence (Figure 33).
SARS-CoV-2’s physicochemical properties are still largely unknown.
SARS-Cove and MERS-Cove can survive in the laboratory for 48 hours in a dry environment and up to 5 days at temperatures below 20 °C and a humidity ranging from 40% to 50%.
SARS-CoV-2 may have similar properties.
SARS-CoV-2 is said to be ultraviolet radiation and heat-sensitive at 56 °C for 30 minutes; ether, ethanol at 75% concentration, chlorine-containing disinfectants, peroxyacetic acid, chloroform and other fatty solvents, but not chlorohexidine, effectively inhibit the virus.
The whole human race generally lacks immunity to SARS-CoV-2, and is therefore more susceptible to infection with the emerging virus.
Currently, there is no detailed study of SARS-CoV-2's immune response.
Therefore, we can only refer to previous studies involving other coronaviruses, in particular SARS-CoV, and MERS-CoV (Figure (Figure 4).
In general, after a virus invasion of the hospiter, it is recognized by the natural immune system through pattern recognition receptors (PRRS), including type C receptors similar to the tin, receptors similar to tulle (TLR), receptors similar to the nuclitides (NLR), and RIG-I-like receptors.
Through different pathways, the virus induces the emergence of inflammatory agents, denitable cell maturity, and the synthesization of Interferons of Type I (IFN) that limit the spread of the virus and accelerate large macrophages of viral antigens.
However, the N protein in SARS-CoV can help the virus escape immune responses.
An adaptive immune response soon joins the fight against the virus.
T lymphocytes, including CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, play an important role in defense.
CD4+ T cells stimulate B cells to produce antibodies intended for the virus, and CD8+ T cells directly infected with the virus.
T-cell-activating cytokines produce inflammatory cytokines to help defending cells.
However, coronavirus can reduce T-cell functions by inducinging T-cell death.
Confusal immunity, including complete proteins such as C3a, C5a and antibodies, is also essential in the fight against viral infections.
For example, isolated antibodies neutralized the Merce-Cove patient recovered.
On the other hand, the excessive reaction of the immune system generates a large number of free radicals locally that can cause severe damage to the lungs and other organs and, in the worst case scenario, failure of the functions of multiple organs and death.
The SARS-CoV-2 infection, which initially appeared in groups, is more likely to affect the elderly with multiple diseases and pregnant women.
It’s common for people exposed to a large number of viruses or whose immunological functions have been damaged have a greater chance of getting infected than others.
The estimated incubation period for SARS-CoV-2 is 1–14 days, or mostly 3 to 7 days, based on a study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan.
However, a study of 1,099 cases shows that the incubation period averaged 3 days and ranged from 0 to 24 days.
As described above, a newer study showed that the incubation period was 4.8 (3.0-7.2) of the days based on the demographics of 8,866 cases.
It is very important that health authorities adjust the effective quarantine time based on the incubation period, thus preventing infected people who do not have symptoms from transmitting the virus to others.
As a common practice, people exposed to or infected with the virus are usually required to quarantine for 14 days.
Does quarantine time have to be extended to 24 days?
Fever is often the main and primary symptom of COVID-19, and may not be accompanied by any or other symptoms, such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscle pain, dizziness, headaches, sore throats, nasal thrum, chest pain, nausea and vomiting.
Some patients suffered from shortness of breath and/or hypoxia one week after the onset of illness.
In severe cases, the condition of patients developed rapidly to suffer from acute respiratory syndrome, septic trauma, metabolic acidosis, and thrombosis.
Patients with fever or respiratory symptoms and high fever should be screened, even without undergoing an imaging that detects pulmonary abnormalities, to look for the virus for early diagnosis.
A demographic study in late December 2019 showed that the percentages of symptoms were 98% for fever, 76% for dry cough, 55% for shortness of breath, and 3% for diarrhea; 8% of patients needed respiratory support.
Similar findings have been reported in two recent studies of a group of a family and a group infected by someone with no symptoms.
By comparison, a demographic study in 2012 showed that MERS-Cove patients also experienced fever (98 percent), dry cough (47 percent), and shortness of breath (55 percent) as major symptoms.
However, 80% of them needed much more respiratory support from COVID-19 patients and were consistent with MERSE’s higher mortality rate from COVID-19.
Diarrhea (26%) and sore throat (21%) were also observed in MERS patients.
In SARS patients, it was found that fever (99%-100%), dry cough (29%-75%), shortness of breath (40%-42%), diarrhea (20–25%), sore throat (13–25%) were the most important symptom, and respiratory support was required for about 14%–20% of patients.
By 14 February, the COVID-19 mortality rate had reached 2%, while confirmed cases had reached 66,576 globally.
By comparison, as of November 2002 the SARS mortality rate accounted for 10% of 8,096 confirmed cases.
According to MERS, the mortality rate stood at 37% of 2,494 confirmed cases, based on a June 2012 demographic study.
According to a previous study, SARS-CoV-2's baseline reproduction number was up to 6.47 with a confidence range of 95% from 5.71 to 7.23, while SARS-Cove's primary reproduction figure ranged from only 2 to 4.
Comparison of SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV in terms of their own symptoms, mortality rate, and basic reproduction figure shown in Table 1.1.
The figures above indicate that SARS-CoV-2 has a greater ability to propagate than that of MERS-Coff and SARS-CoV, but less lethal than the latter two.
Therefore, it is much more difficult to control the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic than the Maers-Cove and SARS-CoV epidemics.
Beginning of the disease occurs in groupings often in the same family, assembly itself, or the vehicle itself, such as a cruise ship.
Patients often have a history of traveling to or staying in Wuhan or other affected areas, or contacting people or patients within the last two weeks before the onset.
However, it has been reported that people can carry the virus without showing symptoms for longer than two weeks, and recovering patients who have been discharged from hospitals can carry the virus again, sending an alert to increase quarantine time.
Patients have a normal or low number of peripheral white blood cells (especially lymphocytes) in the early stage.
For example, lack of lymphobia was detected with the number of white &lt blood cells; 4×109/L, including the number of lymphocytes; 1×109/L, and elevated levels of aspartate amine vector and blood pretration in 1,099 patients with COVID-19.
Levels of liver, muscle and myoglobin enzymes have increased in the blood of some patients, and the increase of the C-reactive protein and the rate of erythrocytosis in the blood of most patients.
In patients with severe cases, the level of demer D, which is caused by the cracking of fibrine in the blood, has gradually decreased and the number of lymphocytes has gradually decreased.
Imbalances in radiology appear in most COVID-19 patients and are characterized by double vengient shadows or oastpressed glass in the lungs.
Patients sometimes experience atypical pneumonia, severe lung injury, and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
When acute respiratory distress syndrome occurs, out-of-control inflammation, the accumulation of fluids, and fibrosis strongly affect the exchange of gases.
The dysfunction of type 1 and type 2 pulmonary cells reduces the level of the superficial activator and increases superficial tension, thus, reduces the ability of the lungs to expand and increases the risk of lung collapse.
Therefore, the worst results of chestral radiology are often compared to the maximum severity of the disease.
On February 18, 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 showed pulmonary cytoculosis, the formation of the hyale membrane, interstitial lymphatic infiltration, and the multi-nucleous syncytial cells in the lungs of a patient who died of the disease, in line with the pathology of viral infection and acute respiratory distress syndrome and is similar to what is happening to patients with SARS and MERS.
The discovery of SARS-CoV-2 RNA through the BT-PCR has been used as a key criterion for diagnosing COVID-19.
However, due to the high rate of false negative results, which may accelerate the epidemic, clinical manifestations for diagnosis (which is no longer individually dependent on RT-PCR) began to be used in China on February 13, 2020.
A similar situation also occurred in the diagnosis of SARS.
Therefore, a combination of the history of the disease, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests, and imaging discoveries is necessary and essential for an effective diagnosis.
On February 14, 2020, Fengzhang Group described a protocol for the use of Cresper-based Sherlock technology to detect SARS-CoV-2, as it detects pieces of SARS-CoV-2's RNA at a rate of 20×10–18 mol/L to 200 × 10-18 mol/L (10–100 copies per μliter of input) using a dip in less than an hour without the need for precise devices.
We hope that the new technique will be able to significantly improve sensitivity and suitability if verified in clinical samples.
Due to a lack of expertise regarding the novel coronavirus, doctors are able to provide mainly supportive care to COVID-19 patients, while experimenting with a variety of treatments that have been used or previously proposed to treat other coronaviruses, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, and other viral diseases (Table 2).
These therapies include current and potential therapies with antiviral drugs, immunosuppressants, stimulants, plasma from recovering patients, Chinese medicine, and psychological support.
Plasma from recovering patients has been suggested to be used for treatment.
Pharmaceutical manufacturers are racing to develop antibodies and vaccines against the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 essentially attacks the lungs at first and, to a lesser extent, may also attack other organs that emit angiotensin 2, such as the gastrointestinal, intestines and kidneys.
Respiratory dysfunction, however, is the most serious threat to patients and the leading cause of death.
Respiratory support is necessary to relieve symptoms and save lives, and includes general treatment with oxygen, high oxygen flow, non-invasive respirators, and invasive respirators based on the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms should be supported by extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO), a cardiopulmonary bypass technique used in the treatment of life-threatening cardiaculosity or respiratory failure.
In addition, maintaining fluid balance, preventing and treating secondary infections and septic shock, and protecting the functions of vital organs are also essential for SARS-CoV-2 patients.
Cytokine storm is known to result from an overreactive reaction from the immune system in SARS and MERS patients.
Cytokine storm is a form of systemic inflammatory reaction characterized by the release of a series of cytokines including TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ and MCP-1.
These cytokines induce immune cells to release a huge number of free radicals and are the leading cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome and the failure of multiple organs.
Immunosuppression is necessary in the treatment of cytokine storms, especially for acute patients.
Activated hormones, tosilizumab, and a monoclonal antagonic antibody were used to treat cytokine storm.
Other immunosuppression treatments for cytokine storm include T-cell-directed immunodeficiency modification; IFN-γ, IL-1 and TNF blockades; Janus kinase inhibition; plinatumumumab; cytokine 4 signal capers; and Heston dystilization inhibitors.
Steroids and immunosuppressants have been extensively used in SARS treatment to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
However, steroids in larger doses weren’t helpful with severe lung infection in SARS and COVID-19 patients.
Instead, it can cause severe side effects, especially avascular bone necrosis, which severely affects the prediction of the course of the disease.
However, it has been recommended to use short cycles of stimulant hormones in doses ranging from small to medium with caution for critical COVID-19 patients.
At the time of writing, the effectiveness of any antiretroviral treatment has not been confirmed.
However, it has been discovered that administering remedicifer, a neoclitide analogue, was effective with an American COVID-19 patient.
Remedisivir is an emerging antiviral initially developed by Gilead to treat diseases caused by the Ebola and Marburg viruses.
Subsequently, Remedicifer also showed a possible inhibition of other single-chain RNA viruses, including MERS and SARS.
Based on these viruses, Giliad has provided China to conduct some experiments on individuals infected with SARS-CoV-2, and the results are highly anticipated.
In addition to this, parisitineps, interferon-alpha, lobinavir/ritunavir, and ribavirin have been proposed as potential treatments for patients with severe respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, liver damage, and other adverse reactions can occur after complex treatment.
The interaction of these treatments with other drugs used with patients should be carefully monitored.
Plasma from recovered patients and antibodies generation
Collecting blood from patients who have recovered from an infectious disease to treat other patients with the same disease or to protect healthy individuals has a long history.
Already, recovering patients often have a relatively high level of antibodies against the pathogen in their blood.
Antibodies are immunoglobulin (IGs) produced by B lymphocytes to combat pathogens and other foreign objects, and they recognize and directly neutralize molecules unique in pathogenic agents.
Based on this, plasma was collected from the blood of a group of patients who recovered from COVID-19 and injected into 10 patients with serious conditions.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours, coupled with reduced inflammation, viral loads and improved oxygenation in the blood.
However, validation and clarification are necessary to introduce this method for widespread use before specific treatments can be found.
In addition, due to therapeutic effects, some of the disadvantages associated with plasma have to be carefully considered.
For example, antibodies can trigger an overstimulating immune reaction and cause cytokine release syndrome, which is likely to be life-threatening toxins.
The concentration of antibodies in the blood is usually low, and the demand for plasma is great for the treatment of critical patients.
It’s difficult to develop specific antibodies and produce them quickly enough to combat a global epidemic.
Isolating B cells from recovering patients and identifying the genetic codes that encode effective antibodies, or screening for effective antibodies against the virus’s core proteins, are more decisive and practical.
In this way, we can easily increase the production of antibodies.
Traditional Chinese medicine has been used to treat a variety of diseases in China for thousands of years.
However, its effects rely heavily on a combination of multiple components in a combination that varies according to the diagnosis of the disease based on the theories of traditional Chinese medicine.
Most active ingredients remain unknown or obscure as it is difficult to extract and verify these ingredients or their optimum compositions.
Currently, due to the lack of effective and specific treatment for COVID-19, traditional Chinese medicine has become an important alternative therapies for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or those who have recovered from severe stages.
For example, Xu Feng Ji-du capsules and Lian Hua Qing Wen capsules for COVID-19 have been discovered to treat COVID-19.
The highest rates of recovery in the treatment of COVID-19 patients were observed in several provinces in China that used traditional Chinese medicine in 87% of its patients, including Ganzhou (63.7%), Ningxia (50%), and Hunan (50%), while Hubei province, which used only traditional Chinese medicine with about 30% of its COVID-19 patients, had the lowest recovery rate (13%).
However, this is quite an approximate comparison, as many other influence factors such as the number of patients and the severity of their cases should be included in the evaluation.
On February 18, 2020, Polly Zhang and his co-workers published a study to compare Western medicine therapy (WM) alone with joint treatment between Western medicine and traditional Chinese medicine.
They discovered that the durations needed to recover from hyperthermia, the elimination of symptoms, and hospital stays were much shorter in the Western medicine therapy + traditional Chinese medicine group compared to Western medicine only.
More impressively, the aggravation rate of symptoms (ranging from mild to severe) was much lower in the Western medicine treatment group + Traditional Chinese Medicine treatment group compared to the Western medicine treatment group only (7.4% vs. 46.2%), and the mortality rate was lower in the Western medicine treatment group + Traditional Chinese Medicine treatment group only (8.8% versus 39%).
Nevertheless, the efficacy and safety of traditional Chinese medicine still await more well-thought-out experiments on larger scales and in more centres.
It will also be interesting to characterize the mechanism of procedures and to clarify the active components of traditional Chinese medicine treatments or their formulations if possible.
Suspected or confirmed COVID-19 patients often feel very fearful of highly contagious and even deadly disease, and people under quarantine are bored, lonely and angry.
In addition, symptoms of infections such as fever, hypoxia, and cough, as well as the negative effects of treatments, such as insomnia caused by stimulant hormones, can lead to more anxiety and mental disorder.
In the first phase of the SARS outbreak, a range of psychiatric illnesses were reported including permanent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychomotor arousals, psychotropic symptoms, delirium, and even suicidal tendencies.
Compulsory contact and quarantine tracking, as part of public health responses to the COVID-19 outbreak, can make people more anxious and guilty about the effects of infection, quarantine, and stigmatizing their families and friends.
Therefore, it is necessary to provide mental health care for COVID-19 patients, people suspected of being infected, people in contact with them, as well as the general public who need it.
Psychological support should include the establishment of multidisciplinary mental health teams, clear contacts with regular and accurate updates about the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak and its treatment plans, and the use of specialized electronic devices and applications to avoid direct contact.
Efficient vaccines to cut the chain of transmission of the virus from infected animal reservoirs and infected humans to vulnerable hosts are necessary and often complementary to antiretroviral therapy in combating epidemics caused by emerging viruses.
Efforts have been made to develop vaccines based on protein S to generate neutral, effective, long-term antibodies and/or protective immunity against SARS-CoV.
Debilitated live vaccines have been evaluated in animal models for SARS treatment.
However, the effectiveness of these candidate vaccines in the vivo in the elderly and the lethal challenge models and their prevention of animal virus infection was not determined before the start of a clinical study.
It’s probably because SARS faded 17 years ago and no new cases have been reported since.
By contrast, sporadic cases and groups continue to emerge in the Middle East and spread to other regions due to the continued presence of animal sources in endemic areas.
Immunization strategies against MERS have been developed using inactivated virus, dehyspheric RNA plasmides, viral carriers, nanoparticles, virus-similar particles, and recombined protein subunits, some of which have been evaluated in animal models.
The development of a safe and effective vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 for non-immunized individuals is an urgent and critical task to control the ongoing epidemic.
Despite this, overcoming difficulty is a challenge due to the length of time (averaging 18 months) needed to develop a vaccine and the dynamic changes of coronaviruses.
Covid-19, as an emerging disease, has just begun to show its complete clinical course through thousands of patients.
In most cases, patients can recover gradually with impunity.
However, COVID-19, similar to SARS and MERS, is also associated with prevalence rates of the disease and high deaths in severe patients.
Therefore, building a progression model is essential for health-care agencies to prioritize their services, particularly in resource-limited areas.
Based on clinical studies reported to date, the following factors may influence or are associated with COVID-19 prognosis (Table 33):
Age: Age has been the most important factor for determining SARS circulativity prognosis, which also applies to COVID-19.
Age groups between 30 and 65 years of age were mainly infected with COVID-19, with 47.7% of these patients exceeding the age of 50 in a study of 8,866 cases as described above.
Patients who needed intensive care were more likely to develop latent concomitants and complications and were much older than those who didn't need them (at an average age of 66 versus 51), indicating age as a predictive factor for the results of COVID-19 patients.
Gender: SARS-CoV-2 infected more men than women (0.31/100,000 versus 0.27/100,000), as described above.
Concomitant diseases and complications: COVID-19 patients who need intensive care are more likely to suffer from severe heart injuries and arrhythmia.
Cardiac accidents were also the leading cause of death in SARS patients.
It has been stated that SARS-CoV-2 can also bind to positive bile cells with angiotensin-converting enzyme 2, which can lead to liver dysfunctions in COVID-19 patients.
It should be noted that the age and underlying disease are strongly related and may interfere with each other.
Abnormal laboratory results: The level of the blood-reactive protein C (CRP) reflects the severity of inflammation or tissue injury, and it has been suggested to be a predictive factor for the disease, response to treatment, and final recovery.
It has also been suggested that the correlation between the level of C-reactive protein and the severity of COVID-19 and its prognosis has also been suggested.
In addition to this, the elevated dehydrogen lactate dehydrogen (LDH), aspartate amin vector (AST), the alanine-carrying enzyme (ALT), and creatine kinase (CKK) may also help predict the outcome.
These enzymes appear strongly in many organs, particularly in the heart and liver, and are released during tissue damage.
Therefore, they are traditional signs of dysfunctions in the heart or liver.
Main Clinical Symptoms: Chest radiography and progressive progression of clinical symptoms should be considered together, along with other problems, to predict COVID-19 outcomes and complications.
Steroid use: As described above, stimulants are commonly used immunosuppressants as an adjunctive treatment for infectious diseases to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
Since the widespread use of high-dose stimulant hormones in SARS patients, many survivors have suffered avascular osteoporosis with lifelong disability and poor quality of life.
Therefore, steroids should be used at a low dose and for a short time in COVID-19 patients, if necessary.
Mental stress: As explained above, during the COVID-19 outbreak, many patients experienced exceptional stress; they often endured long periods of quarantine, severe uncertainty, and witnessed the deaths of close family members and their sick colleagues.
It’s important to provide psychological counseling and long-term support to help those patients recover from stress and return to normal life.
According to demographic studies so far, COVID-19 seems to have different epidemiological traits.
In addition to reincarnation of the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 is able to effectively replicate the upper respiratory tract and cause mild symptoms or cause no symptoms in the early stage of infection, similar to other coronaviruses that cause common colds.
Therefore, infected patients in the early stage or incubation period are able to produce a large amount of the virus during daily activities, causing difficulty in controlling the epidemic.
However, the transmission of SARS-CoV has been considered to occur when patients are severely ill, while most transmissions of the virus did not occur in the early stage.
Therefore, the current outbreak of COVID-19 is much more difficult to control than the SARS outbreak.
Strenuous efforts are currently underway in China, including the closure of Wuhan and surrounding cities and the ongoing quarantine of almost the entire population in the hope of hindering the transmission of SARS-CoV-2.
Although these measures have greatly harmed the economy and other sectors of the country, the number of new patients is declining, signaling a slowdown of the epidemic.
The most optimistic estimate is that the outbreak will end by March and the reversal phase will continue for 3 to 4 months.
However, some other experts don’t have as much optimism.
Paul Hunter and others have estimated that COVID-19, which appears to be far more contagious than SARS, will not end in 2020.
Ira Longini and others created a model for predicting the outcome of the pandemic and suggested that SARS-CoV-2 could infect two-thirds of the world's population.
SARS-CoV-2 was detected in medium-laning and throat smears for patients who recovered and left the hospital two weeks ago, indicating that the newly discovered virus may become a circulatory episode similar to the flu.
However, there have been promising signs in China based on the declining number of new cases, which suggests that current strategies may have worked.
Ebola was originally expected to infect up to one million cases with half a million deaths.
However, the disease was eventually brought under control through strict quarantine and isolation measures.
It is possible, like SARS-CoV, that SARS-CoV-2 will become weaker in terms of causing the infection and eventually disappear or become a less pathogenic virus that coexists with humans.
Below is a comparison of the COVID-19 epidemiological SARS and MERS epidemic below (Figure (Figure 55).
SARS-CoV-2 travels at a higher rate through coughing or sneezing, and can also be transmitted through direct contact with substances contaminated with the virus.
The virus has also been found in the stool, raising a new possibility of transmission from feces to mouth.
According to a recent study of 138 cases, 41% of cases may be caused by hospital infections, including 17 patients with previous diseases and 40 health care providers.
Significant precautions are needed to protect people, especially health care providers, social workers, family members, colleagues, and even bystanders who have come into contact with infected patients.
Wearing face masks is the first line of defense that can be used to reduce the risk of infection. The use of both surgical masks and N95 respiratory masks helps control the spread of viruses.
Surgical face masks prevent fluid droplets from a prospective person from moving through the air or sticking to the surfaces of the material, where they can be passed on to others.
However, only N95 masks can protect against inhalation of small virons as much as 10 to 80 nanometers, with only 5% of the virons being able to penetrate completely; SARS-CoV-2 is similar in size and is about 85 nanometers in size.
Given that particles can penetrate up to five surgical masks monolithed together, health care providers in direct contact with patients must wear N95 masks (Series No. 1860s), but not surgical masks.
In addition to masks, health care providers should wear appropriate isolation robes to reduce contact with the virus further.
Viruses can also infect a person through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor contracted SARS-CoV-2 despite wearing an N95 mask; the virus may have entered its body through its inflamed eyes.
Therefore, health care providers should also wear transparent face protectors or glasses while working with patients.
For the general public in affected or potentially affected areas, it is highly recommended that everyone wash their hands with antiseptic soap more than usual, try to stay at home for self-quarantine and limit communication with potentially infected individuals.
Three feet are considered an appropriate distance to keep people away from the patient.
These measures are effective ways to reduce the risk of infection as well as prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 came as a new virus to the human world, its extreme resemblance to SARS-CoV as reported on January 7, 2020, would have caused a state of maximum alert in China based on its deep memory of the SARS outbreak in 2003.
However, until January 19, 2020, the director of the Center for Disease Control in Wuhan did not reassure citizens by saying that the emerging virus has a low rate of infection and limited human-to-human multiplicity, and there is no problem in preventing and containing the disease.
This message greatly diminished the preparedness of the general public, especially as the whole country was preparing for the Spring Festival, and a critical time was lost to contain the disease at its slightest range in Wuhan.
China’s disease control agencies may take advantage of this harsh lesson and implement substantial improvements in the future.
For example, these agencies must be (1) more careful when issuing public statements because every word is important to citizens and can change their behavior and decisions; (2) more sensitive and interactive with unusual information from clinics rather than waiting for official reports from physicians or officials; (3) more restrictive to contain a potential epidemic in its early stages rather than trying to reassure the public; (4) more issuing meaningful and effective exercises to increase public awareness of epidemic diseases and periodically test and improve a community response system.
The outbreak of COVID-19 caused by SARS-CoV-2 began at the end of December 2019.
Spread in less than two months all over China and about 50 other countries around the world at the time of this writing.
Given that the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and the symptoms are also similar between COVID-19 and SARS, the COVID-19 outbreak has sparked a feeling of recurrenced SARS infection.
However, there are some notable differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are necessary to contain the epidemic and treat patients.
COVID-19 affects older individuals than in young people and in men than in women, and the rate of severity and mortality is also higher in the elderly than in young people.
SARS has a higher mortality rate than COVID-19 (10.91% versus 1.44%).
Patients with COVID-19 transmit the virus even when they don’t have symptoms, while SARS patients usually do this when they are severely ill, causing more difficulty in containing the spread of COVID-19 from SARS.
This partly explains why SARS-CoV-2 spreads much faster and wider than SARS-CoV.
A normal SARS-CoV-2 RNA test can be negative in some COVID-19 patients.
On the other hand, recovering patients can be infected with the virus again.
These results significantly increase the risk of spreading the virus.
With this rapid progress in research on COVID-19, there are still many critical issues to be resolved, as follows:
Where'd SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Despite the discovery of a 96% genetic symmetry between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS similar coronaviruses in bats, we still can’t conclude that SARS-CoV-2 comes from bats.
What animal was the intermediate species that transmitted the virus from the original host, such as bats, to humans?
Without knowinging the answers for numbers 1 and 2, we can’t stop the transition efficiently, and the outbreak can come back at any time.
Although molecular modeling and biochemical tests have shown that SARS-CoV-2 binds to angiotensin-converting enzyme 2, how exactly does the virus enter the airway cells and cause subsequent pathological changes?
Is the virus also linked to cells secreting the angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 in other organs?
Without clear answers to these questions, we cannot make a quick and accurate diagnosis and provide effective treatment.
How long will this epidemic last?
How does the virus genetically evolve as it is transmitted between humans?
Is it going to become a worldwide pandemic, going to fade away like SARS, or is it coming back periodically like the flu?
It’s necessary, but it may take some time to look for answers to the questions above and many other questions.
However, at any cost, we have no other choice but to stop the epidemic as soon as possible and bring our lives back to normal.
Animal origins of human coronaviruses
Mutation and adaptation have been driving the common development between coronaviruses (coronaviruses) and their hosts, including humans, for thousands of years.
Prior to 2003, it was known that two types of human coronavirus (human coronaviruses) caused mild disease, such as a cold.
An outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) has exposed the other side to reveal the extent to which human coronavirus infections can be serious and life-threatening.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 in Central China at the end of 2019 re-highlighted coronaviruses, and surprised us with the speed of its spread but with the ability to cause the disease compared to his brother SARS-CoV.
Human coronavirus is a zoonotic disease, so our understanding of the animal origins of human coronaviruses would serve us well.
Most human coronaviruses come from bats where they were unpathetic.
Intermediate repository flight attendants have also been known for some human coronaviruses.
Identification of hospites has a direct impact on the prevention of human diseases.
Investigating the interactive relationships between coronavirus and host in animals also provides an important insight into the emergence of coronavirus disease in humans.
In this review, we provide an overview of the information available about the seven human coronaviruses, with a focus on the history of their discovery as well as their animal origins and interspecial transmission.
The most important thing is that we compare and compare different human coronaviruses from the perspective of the evolution of the virus and recombination of the genome.
In this context, the disease caused by the current coronavirus pandemic 2019 (COVID-19) is being discussed.
In addition, it also highlights the requirements for a successful host switch and the impact of complications of virus development on the severity of the disease.
Coronaviruses (coronaviruses) belong to the family of coronaviruses, which includes a group of coated viruses with single-chain-positive-directional RNA.
These viruses contain the largest rheunomic genome of RNA viruses, with 26 to 32 kilograms, and have been called "coronaviruses" because their composition resembles a crown under an electronic microscope.
In terms of structural characteristics, coronaviruses contain undivided genomes that share similar regulation.
Approximately two-thirds of the genome has two large interlaced open reading molds (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are converted into pp1a and pp1ab polyproteins.
Multiproteproteins are treated additionally to generate 16 non-structural proteins, referred to as nsp1~16.
The remaining part of the genome contains open reading molds for structural proteins, including Spike (ES), envelope (E), membrane (M) and nuclear protein (EN).
Various strains of coronavirus also encode a number of additional proteins from special strains.
Based on the difference in protein sequences, coronaviruses are classified into four types (alpha coronavirus, beta coronavirus, gamma coronavirus, and delta coronavirus), including beta coronavirus, which include most human coronaviruses and are divided into four strains (a, B, C, and D).
Evidence from genetic evolution has shown that bats and rodents act as the genetic origin of most alpha coronaviruses and beta coronaviruses, while birds are the main reservoir of gamma coronaviruses and delta coronaviruses.
Over thousands of years, coronaviruses have consistently weathered species barriers and some have emerged as pathogens to serious human diseases.
To date, seven human coronaviruses are known (human coronaviruses).
Among them are human coronavirus-229E and human coronavirus-NL63 and they belong to alpha coronaviruses.
Coronaviruses include the other five beta-coronavirus-OC43, human coronavirus-HKU1, severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus (SARS-CoV), Middle East Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (MERS-CoV), and SARS-CoV-2.
Human coronavirus-229E, human coronavirus-OC43, human coronavirus-HKU1, and human coronavirus-NL63 usually cause mild symptoms, such as cold, diarrhea, or both.
By contrast, SARS-Cove, MERS-CoV, and the newly-recognized SARS-CoV-2 virus are all highly pathogenic, causing severe infection of the lower respiratory tract to a larger number of patients with a higher likelihood of acute respiratory distress syndrome (acute respiratory distress syndrome) and non-pulmonary symptoms.
Human coronavirus-229E, the first strain B814, was isolated from the nasal secretions of patients with colds in the mid-1960s.
Much of the information has since been collected through extensive studies of human coronavirus-229E and human coronavirus-OC43, the symptoms of which are spontaneous.
In fact, it was generally accepted that infection with human coronaviruses was harmless until the SARS outbreak.
The SARS outbreak in 2003 is among the deadliest epidemics in our present history, with 8,000 people with approximative deaths exceeding 10%.
Ten years later, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) has spread, triggering a stagnant epidemic in the Arabian Peninsula as it sporadically spreads to the rest of the world.
Renamed to SARS-CoV-2, the causative agent of the current 2019 coronavirus (COVID-19), has killed more than 3,120 people and infected more than 91,000 people on March 3, 2020.
The alarm bell rings and the world must prepare for the upcoming SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
All seven human coronaviruses have animal origins from bats, rats, or pets.
Multiple evidence supports the evolutionary origins of all human coronaviruses from bats, where viruses are well-adapted and non-pathogenic but exhibit significant genetic diversity.
The COVID-19 pandemic has raised enormous medical, scientific, social and ethical challenges for China and the world.
Tracking the animal origins of human coronaviruses has shown a framework for understanding natural history, the driving force and restrictive factors of interspecie transmission.
This may or may also guide or facilitate the search for warehouse, intermediate host, and amplified host animal (amplified host animals) of SARS-CoV-2, with important effects to prevent future ramifications.
In this review we provide an overview of animal origins, interspecial transmission, and human coronavirus causes diseases.
In particular, we highlight and discuss the common belief that native viruses of human coronaviruses are usually not pathogenic in their natural reservoirs but become pathogenic after being transmitted between species to a new host.
We also review an assessment of the trajectory of the human coronavirus where an increase in transmissibility is often accompanied by a decrease in the severity of disease.
In this context, we also discuss the results of the current SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.
Animal coronaviruses have been known since the late 1930s.
Prior to isolating the B814 strain of human coronavirus-229E for the first time from the nasal secretions of patients with colds, various types of coronavirus were isolated from various infected animals, including turkeys, rats, cows, pigs, cats, and dogs.
In the past decades, seven types of human coronaviruses have been identified.
Presenting a brief summary of the history of human coronavirus discovery in chronological order (Table 1) will be instructive.
The first strain of the human coronavirus-229E was isolated from the respiratory tract of patients with an upper respiratory tract infection in 1966, after which it was adapted to live in the WI-38 lung cell classes.
Patients infected with human coronavirus-229E suffer from common cold symptoms, including headaches, sneezing, malaise, sore throat, with a fever, and cough in approximately 10~20% of cases.
Later in 1967, the human coronavirus-OC43 was isolated from an organ farm and the subsequent transmission was in the brains of infant rats.
The clinical features of human coronavirus-OC43 infection appear to be similar to those caused by human coronavirus-229E, which are indistinguishable by symptoms from other respiratory viruses such as influenza A viruses and rhinoviruses.
Both human coronavirus-229E and human coronavirus-OC43 have spread globally, often inclined to travel during the winter season in the temperate climate.
In general, the incubation time of these two viruses is less than one week, followed by a feeling of illness for about two weeks.
According to a human volunteering study, the infection of healthy individuals with the human coronavirus-229E has caused a millet cold.
Only a few immunodeficiated patients have shown severe infection of the lower respiratory tract.
SARS, also known as atypical pneumonia, is the first well-documented human coronavirus-induced pandemic in human history and its causative factor was SARS-CoV, the third human coronavirus to be detected.
SARS's first case dates back to late 2002 in China's Guangdong province.
The SARS epidemic has resulted in 8,096 confirmed cases and 774 deaths, and has spread across many countries and continents.
Regardless of the hypercontinent, it has been estimated that a single case can lead to approximately two secondary cases, with an incubation period of 4 to 7 days and the peak viral load appears on the 10th day of illness.
SARS-CoV symptoms begin with muscular pain, headaches, fever, malaise, and chills, followed as delayed symptoms with difficulty breathing, coughing and respiratory distress.
Lack of lymphocytes, dysfunction of the liver, and elevated creatine kinase are common laboratory dysfunctions of SARS.
Also observed in SARS patients, diffused alveocyclic damage, the multiplicity of apparent cells, and an increase in the number of macrophagic cells.
Approximately 20-30% of patients then require intensive care and artificial respiration.
In addition to the lower respiratory tract, in severe cases, many organs, including the digestive tract, liver and kidneys, can also be affected and are usually accompanied by cytokine storm, which can be fatal, especially in immunocompromised patients.
For the first time, the virus was isolated from an open lung biopsy of a relative of the first case who traveled from Guangzhou to Hong Kong.
Since then, huge efforts have been devoted to conducting research on human coronavirus.
Human Coronavirus-NL63 was isolated from a 7-month-old child from the Netherlands in late 2004.
It was initially found common in young children, the elderly, and immunodeficiency patients with respiratory illnesses.
A cold, conjunctivitis, fever and bronchiolitis are common symptoms of the disease caused by the human coronavirus-NL63.
Another independent study has described isolating the virus itself from a sample of the nose of an 8-month-old child suffering from pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although discovered in the Netherlands, it's widespread globally.
It is estimated that the human coronavirus-NL63 is responsible for approximately 4.7% of common respiratory diseases, and the peak occurs during early summer, spring and winter.
Human coronavirus-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, also known as crocassis.
In the same year, HKU1 was isolated from a 71-year-old man who was hospitalized for pneumonia and bronchiolitis in Hong Kong.
In addition to community-acquired pneumonia and bronchiolitis, it has been reported that HKU1 is linked to the exacerbation of severe asthma attacks.
It has been found that the human coronavirus-HKU1 is widespread globally, and causes mild respiratory diseases, similar to human coronavirus-NL63, human coronavirus-229E and human coronavirus-OC43.
All of these four human coronaviruses acquired from the community have adapted well with humans. In general, the likelihood that they mutate to cause diseases characterized by their high prevalence rates are few, but the coincidence occurred for unknown reasons, as in the rare case of the most ferocious subtype of human coronavirus-NL63, which has recently been reported to cause severe infection of the lower respiratory tract in China.
In general, when these coronaviruses acquire the ability to transmit efficiently and constantly maintain themselves within humans, they also become less virulent or pathological.
Meers-Cove was first isolated in 2012 from the lungs of a 60-year-old patient with pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia.
While most laboratory-confirmed cases originate in the Middle East, in many European countries and Tunisia cases have been reported to have been brought from abroad and a rare secondary transmission of contacts in close personal contact.
Another secondary outbreak occurred in South Korea in 2015 that resulted in 186 confirmed cases.
The clinical symptoms caused by Merce are similar to those caused by SARS, and are characterized by worsening acute pneumonia.
Contrary to SARS, many of those infected with MERS have also experienced severe kidney failure, which distinguishes MERS from the rest of diseases caused by the human coronavirus.
More than 30 percent of patients experience intestinal symptoms, such as diarrhea and vomiting.
On February 14, 2020, more than 2,500 laboratory-confirmed cases were reported with a 34.4% elevated mortality rate, making MERS-Cove one of the most destructive viruses known to humans.
From mid-December to late 2019, groups of pneumonia patients that were later identified as caused by SARS-CoV-2 were detected in Wuhan, Hubei province, China.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has announced the current outbreak of lower respiratory tract infection due to SARS-CoV-2 that it is a public health emergency of international importance as it was called COVID-19.
On March 3, 2020, 90,053 cases were confirmed worldwide, and the primary mortality rate was 3.4%.
It should be noted that the percentage of deaths in China's Hubei province is 4.2 percent, while it's 1.2 percent.
SARS-CoV-2 causes severe respiratory infections such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, and appears in the form of fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Diarrhea also appears in some infected people.
Pneumonia is one of the most serious symptoms and can rapidly progress to acute respiratory distress syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very similar due to the great homogeneity in the nucleotide sequence by 82%, they belong to different branches in the breed evolution tree.
SARS-CoV-2 appears to be less severe but more contagious compared to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
It has been reported that people asymptomatic despite being infected with SARS-CoV-2 may contribute to its rapid spread around the world.
Comparison and discrepancy between SARS-CoV-2 and the other six human coronaviruses have revealed similarities and variations of great importance.
First, human coronaviruses are the same incubation period and the time period of the disease.
In this regard, SARS-CoV-2 is following the overall trend of the other six human coronaviruses.
Secondly, the severity of the symptoms of COVID-19 infection falls between SARS-CoV and the four community-acquired human coronaviruses (i.e. human coronavirus-229E, human coronavirus-OC43, human coronavirus-HKU1, and human coronavirus-NL63).
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 features that are more common in cases of human coronaviruses acquired from the community, including the appearance of unspecified or minor symptoms or even the absence of symptoms.
On the other hand, you can also see a small subset of severe cases of COVID-19 as in the case of SARS-CoV, although the percentage is slightly lower.
Third, SARS-CoV-2’s transmission demonstrates interesting patterns of both community-acquired human coronaviruses and SARS-CoV.
On the one hand, the transmissibility of SARS-CoV-2 is as high as the transmissibility of human coronaviruses acquired from society.
On the other hand, it remains to check whether SARS-CoV-2 transmissibility decreases after transmission to humans, as is the case in cases of SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV infections.
Finally, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in fecal samples as in other human coronaviruses.
More studies are still needed in the future to clarify whether SARS-CoV-2’s oral anal transition plays an important role, at least in some cases.
It’s worth paying attention to whether SARS-CoV-2 may show a seasonal spread, as is the case in cases of human coronaviruses acquired from the community.
However, SARS-CoV-2’s attributes, including the possibility of transmission, and the mechanism of pathology and sustainable diffusion after transmission to humans will have an impact on the ultimate fate of the current COVID-19 outbreak.
All of the four human coronaviruses acquired from the community that cause minor symptoms have adapted well to humans.
Another perspective, which may also be true, is that humans have adapted well to these four human coronaviruses.
In other words, they are probably both survivors of ancient human coronavirus epidemics.
Human coronaviruses that infect humans with severe diseases have been eliminated, as well as humans who have contracted acute human coronavirus diseases.
For this to happen, human coronaviruses must be copied in humans enough to allow the accumulation of adaptive mutations that face restrictive factors in the host.
Based on this logic, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak lasts and the greater the number of people infected, the greater the chance that it is fully adapted to humans.
If it's well adapted, it's difficult to stop human-to-human transmission by quarantine or other infection control measures.
The four coronaviruses acquired from society spread among human populations over many years, causing a cold in people with good immunity.
These viruses don’t need an animal reservoir.
In contrast, SARS-Cove and Mers-Cove, which cause severe impairment to humans, did not adapt well, and were unable to continue to transition between humans.
They need to stay in their animal reservoirs and multiply and then look for the right opportunity to move to the most vulnerable human goals, perhaps through one or more intermediate and amplified flight attendants.
SARS-CoV-2 has similar traits to both SARS-CoV/MERS-CoV and the four community-acquired human coronaviruses.
It's very contagious like community-acquired human coronaviruses, at least for now.
However, it causes the disease more that it is caused by human coronaviruses acquired from the community and less than SARS-CoV or MERS-Cove.
It is not yet clear whether it will fully adapt to humans and spread among them without the need for a reservoir or intermediate animal host.
Before discussing the animal origins of human coronaviruses, it will be useful for us to review the definitions and characteristics of evolutionary, natural, repository, intermediatory and inflated hosts for human coronavirus.
The animal acts as the evolutionary host of human coronavirus if the virus harbors a closely related origin and shares a great similarity in the level of nucleotide sequences.
The virus of origin is usually well-adapted and non-pathogenic to this host.
Similarly, the hospitan host shelters human coronavirus on an ongoing and long-term basis.
In both cases, flight attendants are infected naturally and are the natural hosts for human coronavirus or their father virus.
By contrast, if the human coronavirus enters the newly intermediate host before attacking humans or nearby, it doesn’t adapt well to the new host and often causes morbidity.
This intermediate host can act as an animal source of human infection and acts as an amplified host by allowing the virus to temporarily replicate and thus transmit it to humans to extend infection among humans.
Human coronavirus infection may reach an impasse if the virus fails to transmit it from within the intermediate host.
Conversely, human coronaviruses can also adapt to the intermediate host and may even be endemic for a long time.
In this case, the intermediate host becomes the normal reservoir host.
Epidemiological data later revealed that SARS's first case had a history of contact with the game.
Serogen prevalence surveys then indicated that animal traffickers have a higher IgG anti-SARS-KOV prevalence rate compared to ordinary people.
It has been identified for the first time that Paguma larvata and raccoon dogs in live animal markets carry SARS-Cove-like viruses that are almost identical to SARS-Cove.
What indirectly supported this is the fact that he did not report any other case of SARS infection after killing all covet cats in the markets.
However, it has been reported that SARS-CoV's exaggeration of masked palm cives that lives in the wild or farms without exposure to live animal markets has been largely negative, indicating that masked palm civer may only be an inflated and intermediate host, but it's not the natural reservoir of SARS-Cove.
Since 80% of the different animals in the Gonzhou market have SARS-CoV antibodies, the possibility that multiple species of young mammals also act as amplified intermediate hosts for SARS-CoV is not excluded.
It seems that all of these are flight attendants who don't carry SARS-Cove.
The subsequent search for SARS-CoV’s animal natural host led to the detection of the closely related bat coronavirus, which is called HKU3’s reddenched Chinese horseshoe bat virus (HKU3), which is found in Chinese horseshoe bat bat.
Testing of SARS-CoV antibodies in these bats and the genomic sequence of the chinesetic shoe bat virus associated with HKU3 has been positive.
This virus and other coronaviruses for bats share an 88-92% symmetry of nucleotide sequences with SARS-CoV.
These studies have laid the groundwork for the new concept that bats are hosts to emerging human pathogens.
Many several acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus-like coronaviruses (serious acute respiratory syndrome coronaviruses) have been identified from bats, but none of them could not be isolated as a live virus except WIV1.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (angiotensin-converting enzyme 2) is known as the receptor for SARS-CoV.
It has been shown that WIV1 extracted from a bat stool specimen used angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 in a bat, covet cat, and humans as receptors to enter the cell.
Interestingly, the serum of those recovering from severe acute respiratory syndrome had the ability to neutralize WIV1.
To date, WIV1 represents the virus most closely associated with SARS-CoV in bats, as they share 95% nucleotide sequences.
Despite the high similarity between these two viruses, it is generally believed that WIV1 is not the direct father of SARS-CoV and that bats are not a direct repository host for SARS-Cove.
An analysis of the evolution of MERS-CoV strains combines into the coronavirus-HKU4 group in the bats and the HKU5 coronavirus in the bats themselves.
Coronavirus-HKU4 is used in bats and the host's receptor's Mers-Cove, the host's receptor, a peptide-peptidaz4 duo (peptidil-peptidase4), to enter the virus.
MERS-Cove RNA polymerase sequences are closer in terms to the evolution of strains to their beta coronaviruses in bats identified in Europe and Africa.
It has not yet been discovered that Merce-Cove lives in wild bats.
Mers-Cove and HKU25 share the bats closest to him in the symmetry of nucleotide sequences by only 87%.
Consequently, host bats may not be the direct reservoir of MERS-Cove.
Studies in the Middle East have shown that only camel hump is positive for MERS-Cove’s equivalent antibodies, as well as camels of Middle Eastern descent in many African countries.
Mers-Cove's zombiological virus in humans has been isolated from nasal swabs of single-hump camels, indicating that camel acts as Mers-Cove's original reservoir host.
It is also noteworthy that minor symptoms generally appeared in Jamal was experimentally infected with MERS-Cove, but extensive viral scattering was observed.
Infected camels scattered viruses significantly, not only by breathing but also through the oral anal road, which is the primary route for scattering the virus from bats.
However, there are still questions because many confirmed cases of MERS do not have a history of contact with camels before the onset of symptoms, and these cases have been attributed in terms of logic to human-to-human transmission or unknown modes of transmission that include unrecognized types of animals that have not been harbored by Maers-Cove.
SARS-CoV-2 RaTG13 is involved in bats isolated from horseshoe bats in 96.2% nucleotide symmetry.
The sequencing between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is too long to determine the relationship with the father, as in the cases of SARS-Cove and MERS-Cove.
This means that bats may not be SARS-CoV-2’s direct reservoirs unless coronaviruses for bats are almost identical in the future.
SARS-CoV-2 intermediate hosts are supposed to be among the wildlife species sold and killed at the Wuhan Seafood Market, with which many of the initial cases of COVID-19 have been linked, suggesting the possibility of animal-to-human transmission.
Many recent studies based on metagenomic sequencing have suggested that a group of small endangered mammals known as manis javanica can also harbor beta coronaviruses of origin associated with SARS-CoV-2.
The genomes of the novel coronavirus of these ant eaters and SARS-CoV-2 share an 85-92% nucleotide sequence.
However, they are closely related to RaTG13 with the similarity of about 90 percent at the level of nucleotide sequences.
Two subspecirals of SARS-CoV-2-like viruses are clustered in an evolutionary tree, one of which is involved in a similar receptor binding field with SARS-CoV-2, with 97.4% matching of amino acid sequences.
In stark contrast, SARS-CoV-2 receptor correlation areas differ significantly from RATG13, although the entire genome sequence is higher.
An earlier study on a diseased squamous anate also revealed the presence of viral organisms from lung samples, similarly associated with SARS-CoV-2.
This study adopted different assembling methods and manual processing methods to generate partial genome sequences consisting of a full-lenged viral genome of approximately 86.3%.
We can’t rule out the possibility that the pangolin is one of the intermediate hospites of SARS-CoV-2.
However, there is currently no evidence to support pangolin is the direct origin of SARS-CoV-2 due to the sequencing variation between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-Cov-2-associated beta coronaviruses.
In addition, the spacing between SARS-CoV-2 and RATG13 is less than between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-Cov-2-related beta coronaviruses in pangolin.
The evolutionary trajectory of SARS-CoV-2 in bats, squamous ant eaters, and other mammals is still being assigned.
While the highest sequential symmetry has been found in receptor correlation areas between SARS-CoV-2 and squazoal eaters, beta coronaviruses associated with SARS-CoV-2, SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 share the higher symmetry of full genome sequencing.
It is the dominant speculator that the high degree of similarity between the areas of association of SARS-CoV-2 receptors in beta and SARS-CoV-2 squamous eaters is submissive by selective convergent evolution.
There's an anti-recomposition between SARS-CoV-2 in beta coronavirus squamolin and RaTG13 in the third wild animal species.
Recombination is widespread among beta coronaviruses as a driving force for evolution.
The direct animal origin of SARS-CoV-2 has not yet been confirmed.
In addition to highly pathogenic human coronaviruses, the animal origin of the human coronavirus-229E, the human coronavirus-OC43, the human coronavirus-NL63, and the human coronavirus-HKU1 have also been examined.
Evidence of the evolution of strains has indicated that both human coronavirus-NL63 and human coronavirus-229E may have originated from coronaviruses for bats, while father viruses for human coronavirus-OC43 and HKU1) have been found in rodents.
It has been reported that the coronavirus in bats called ARCoV.2 (Al-Ablash High Coronavirus) discovered in trichlored bats in North America has a close relationship with the human coronavirus-NL63.
On the other hand, it has been found that the human coronavirus-229E is genetically linked to another coronavirus in bats, called Hipposideros/GanaKwam/19/2008, which was discovered in Ghana, and it has been speculated that the camels are also their intermediate host.
To clarify, current information about the animal origins of known human coronaviruses is summarized in Figure 1 and Table 2.
Analyzing the evolution of strains has provided evidence of cases of human coronavirus transmission between species throughout history.
When the human coronavirus-OC43 crossed species to infect humans from domestic livestock around 1890, an epidemic of respiratory infection was recorded.
The date of transmission of human coronavirus-229E between species is still less pronounced.
Alpha coronaviruses in bats have been found to be closely related to human coronavirus-229E.
There is an alpha coronavirus between them in alpaca.
Many evidence supports the transmission of the virus from bats directly to humans.
First, humans, not alpaca, may have contact with bats in a shared environmental habitat.
Humans are in close contact with alpaca.
Second, the human coronavirus-229E, which belongs to alpha coronaviruses in bats, is multiple and non-pathogenic in bats, while the coronavirus alpha in albaka causes an outbreak of respiratory diseases in infected animals.
Finally, alpaca-related alpha coronaviruvirus has not been detected in wild animals.
Thus, the possibility that albaka was infected with the human coronavirus-229E belonging to the alpha coronavirus from humans cannot be ruled out.
In fact, bats are the direct source of viruses that cause diseases in humans including rabies virus, Ebola virus, Nipah virus, and Hendra virus.
So it’s no surprise that bats may transmit human coronavirus-229E directly to humans.
While alpha coronaviruses in bats act as accumulating the genes of the human coronavirus-229E, alpaca and single-hump camels may act as intermediary hosts that transmit viruses to humans, just as is the case in Maers-Cove.
Meers-Cove gives an excellent example of the transition between species as it travels from bats to only hump camels and from onlione camel hump to humans.
Meers-Cove's evolutionary origin has been known from bats from the very beginning of identification and has also been boosted by subsequent findings.
It is clear that bats provide a rich concentration of types of viruses to exchange genes within the same species and move them between species.
Bats are characterized by longevity, living in overcrowded colonies, close social interaction, and strong ability to fly are all favorable conditions to make them an ideal “virus distributor”.
Mirce-Cove, on the other hand, has moved into single-hump camels for decades.
These camels have been well adapted until you have switched from an intermediary host to a natural and stable repository host.
Meers-Cove causes a very minor illness and maintains a relatively low mutation rate in these animals.
Its intermittent transmission to humans remains coincidental and man is an unconvulsive host for Mers-Cove as its transmission cannot continue.
Contrary to the role of camels in the transfer of Maers-Cove, the role, if any, in the transmission of SARS-CoV-2 is different.
In particular, beta coronaviruses associated with squamous anthromites infect him with severe disease.
They may be unvolable hosts for SARS-CoV-2, similar to the civet cat in the case of SARS-CoV.
Futuristic studies will determine many possibilities for SARS-CoV-2 moving between species of animals to humans by either proving or excluding them.
Initially, bats may be a repository host for a virus linked to SARS-CoV-2, which is mostly identical to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans and bats may share the ecological habitat through slaughter or coal mining.
Second, squamus can be one of the recently discovered SARS-CoV-2 inflated inflaters.
Humans become infected with the virus by slaughtering and consuming game meat.
Many mammals, including pets, are likely to be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2.
It is justified to scan antibodies in pets and wild animals.
Third, as mentioned above, there may have been recombination and adaptation of SARS-CoV-2 in a third species that has contact with bats and squamous ant eaters.
The search for the animal origin of SARS-CoV-2 is still ongoing.
Apart from the different types of animal hosts, from the viral side there are three main and also important factors in facilitating coronavirus infection to overcome species barriers.
First of all, their mutation rates are relatively high to multiply RNA.
Appreciative coronavirus mutation rates, compared to other single-chain RNA viruses, can be considered "averaging" or "high" with an average substitution rate of ~10-4 substitutions each year for site 2, depending on the coronavirus adaptation phase to the new flight attendants.
Coronaviruses involve reponoclyase enzyme reponoclease externally correctively, deleting it leads to turbility and attenuation or incapacity to develop.
Interestingingly, nucleotide isotope remdesivir is known for its ability to suppress coronavirus by inhibiting external ribonoclysase and RNA-based RNA polymerase.
Remedicivir is one of the most promising factors against SARS-CoV-2, which is scheduled to be tested in clinical trials.
Coronavirus mutation rates, however, are about a million times higher than flight attendants.
In addition, the mutation rate is often high when coronaviruses do not appropriately adapted to the host.
SARS-CoV-2 mutations are remarkably low when compared to SARS-CoV with a high mutation rate, indicating a higher level of human adaptation.
It seems that he has already adapted to another hospite close to man.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2, this also applies to Meers-Cove, who has adapted well to single-hulted camels.
In theory, it’s unlikely that genetic deviation of vaccines and antiviral drugs will help resist SARS-CoV-2 by rapidly weakening its effectiveness.
Secondly, the massive RNA genome in coronaviruses actively provides an additional dehydration of mutations and recombination, thereby increasing the likelihood of interspecied evolution, which is useful for new coronaviruses when conditions become favorable.
This is powered by unique ample open reading templates and the functions of the coded protein near the end of the genome’s 3′.
Coronaviruses randomly and frequently switch templates during RNA copying through a unique “transcription selection” mechanism.
In a host that is like a blending vessel, sequencing frequently occurs during coronavirus RNA replication.
Full-length and secondary genomic nucleic acids may recombine to generate new coronaviruses.
A special guide has been discovered for the development of strains for the natural recombination of human coronavirus-HKU1 and human coronavirus-OC43, in addition to animal coronaviruses, as well as in the coronavirus similar to severe acute respiratory syndrome in bats and HKU9 coronavirus in bats.
Interaction of the virus with the transmission-related host
In addition to the three viral factors mentioned above, viral interaction with the host receiver is another major factor in interspectal transmission.
In this regard, we look at the recombination of SARS-CoV as a typical example, which has also shown evidence of positive selection during interspecie transmission incidents.
Based on the comparative analysis of isolated SARS-CoV samples in humans and civet, SARS-Cove was thought to undergo rapid adaptation in different hospores, particularly through mutations that occur in receptor binding field to the S protein.
In general, the receptor binding field of coronavirus S protein interacts with the cytoceptor and is intensely selected through the host's antibody response.
In SARS-CoV, the receptor correlation field occurs in amino acids from 318 to 510 in piece S1, which is associated with angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 as well as receptors to enter the virus.
SARS-CoV’s receptor correlative domain is able to recognize angiotensin-converting receptors in various animals, including bats, civet cats, mouse, and raccoon dog, which allows the transmission of the virus between species.
In fact, residues of only 6 amino acids that were different from insulated human and civet samples were observed in the receptor binding field, 4 of which are found in the receptor's correlation sequence pattern to interact with angiotensin-converting enzyme receptor.
K479N and S487T mutations are found in SARS-Cove receptor binding field in the civet, which can increase the likelihood that spike protein will interact in human angiotensin-converting enzyme receptor.
In other words, these two types of amino acid substitutes may be critical to viral adaptation in humans.
It is noteworthy that SARS-CoV-2 shares the same cytoceptor found in SARS-Cove.
There is a 30% difference between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV in the S1 unit of the SS protein, implying that the probability of the S protein binding in the angiotensin-converting enzyme to human angiotensin 2 has changed.
Already, an ultra-cold electrominoscope study has indicated a probability of about 10 to 20 times higher for this correlation compared to that between human angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 and SARS-CoV.
It’s also interesting to determine whether any other type of auxiliary receptor might be required for SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
It's also interesting that human coronavirus-NL63 also binds to angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 but in a different fraction of the S protein.
There are many other human coronavirus receptors, such as the HCoronavirus-229E Protein Analyzer, and 9-O Cyalic Acid (HCO43).
They may also be responsible for the successful adaptation of these coronaviruses in humans after transmission between species from their animal hosts.
In addition to cellular receptors, the result of interspecie transmission of human coronaviruses is also subject to the control of other dependabilitaties and limitations in the host.
The branching of these hosting proteins between humans and natural reservoir hosts for coronaviruses, such as bats, single-hump camels, and rodents, may be an obstacle to transmission between species.
Human coronaviruses need to control host dependencies and impase restrictions in them to carry out a successful transmission between species.
In this regard, molecular determinants in this important area of virus interaction with the host remain detectable and characterizable.
Extensive unbiased screening on the genome can be leveraged to examine SARS-CoV-2’s dependabilitative factors and host limitations using advanced CRISPR technology.
Emergence of Emerging Human Coronavirus: Returning to the starting point
The diversity of coronaviruses in bats offers abundant opportunities for the emergence of emerging human coronaviruses.
Based on this logic, coronaviruses in bats are a hereditary complex of human coronaviruses.
In addition, rapid mutation and genetic recombination are also driving the evolution of the human coronavirus, which represent two important points in the process.
For example, the acquisition or loss of emerging protein encoding genes involves the ability to significantly modify viral phenotypes.
Among SARS-Cove’s regulatory proteins, ORF8 was thought to be important in adapting to humans, as SARS-CoV associated with viruses was isolated in bats but was found to encode disparate ORF8 proteins.
It has been found that the 29 characteristics of SARS-CoV nucleotide deletion were found in isolated strains at the beginning of the human epidemic.
This deletion has split ORF8 into ORF8a and ORF8b, and is believed to be an adaptive mutation that promotes flight attendant switching.
In addition, SARS-Cove has a history of possible recombination through strains of alpha and gamma coronaviruses, where a large number of small recombination areas have been identified in RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.
Recombination sites have also been identified in nsp9, most nsp10, and parts of nsp14.
In a similar way, research has shown that the MERS-CoV epidemic has seen recombinations between different strains, which have occurred in Saudi Arabia’s single-hump camels.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, recombinations have also been observed in other human coronaviruses, where human coronaviruses have been synthesized with other animal coronaviruses in their non-constructive genes.
It should be noted that artificial selection can also help unintended changes in the viral genome, most of which are most likely caused by viruses getting rid of selective stress, such as what happens with the host’s immune system.
The loss of a full-length ORF4 protein in the human coronavirus phenotype strain-229E due to the deletion of two nucleotides is an example of these effects.
Although ORF4 can be properly observed in camel viruses and bats associated with human coronavirus-229E, the alpha coronavirus that infects alpaca shows single nucleotide stitches, which leads to an underlining framework mutation.
Last but not least, the development of new coronaviruses is also fueled by selective pressure in their reservoired flight attendants.
Asymptomatic or only mild cases have been detected when bats are infected with coronaviruses, which indicates mutual adaptation between coronavirus and bats.
It showed that bats adapted appropriately to coronavirus anatomically and physiologically.
For example, impaired activating the supportive response to infections in bats effectively reduced the pathogens provoked by coronaviruses.
In addition, natural lethal cellular activity in bats was suppressed due to an increase in NKG2/CD94 and the lower level of expression of the main histological compositylase I.
Furthermore, the elevated level of reactive oxygen compound types (reactive oxygen compound types) arising from the high metabolic activity of bats can result in both suppression of coronavirus replicating the coronavirus and influencing correction by the external ribonoclyase enzyme, thus providing selective pressure for the emergence of strains of the virus that are highly pathogenic when infecting a new host.
Additional pathogenic strains of coronavirus may be created by recombination, leading to the acquisition of new proteins or protein properties to adapt to the host.
Accordingly, it’s no coincidence that there have been three emerging human coronaviruses over the past two decades.
Coronaviruses are not pathogenic or cause mild symptoms in reservoired flight attendants such as bats and camels.
They clone vigorously without triggering a strong immune response from the host.
Here are the secrets that explain why virus carriers who don’t show symptoms and the causes of acute cases of human infection.
Acute symptoms are mainly caused by hyperactivity of the immune response and cytokine storm where the stronger the immune response, the more severe the lung damage.
Conversely, in the case of unsymptomatic carriers, the immune response separates from coronavirus replication.
A similar strategy for detachment of the immune response may have beneficial effects in SARS-CoV-2 antiposex therapy.
The interferon response is particularly strong in bats.
Thus, ingesting Interferon type I can be used at least at least in the first stage of SARS-CoV-2 infection in humans.
In addition, the activation of NLRP3 in bats is impaired.
With this mental logic, inhibition of NLRP3 inflammatory particle with MCC950 inhibitor is beneficial in the treatment of COVID-19.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 follows the general idea by which SARS-Cove and Merse-Cove originated.
At a time when it was discovered that the beta coronavirus in bat SARS-CoV participates in nucleotide homogenization by 95%, there was also the coronavirus in bats sharing 96% of nucleotide homogeneity with SARS-CoV-2.
It has been discovered that civet cats and other animals on the market harboring viruses similar to SARS-CoV did not detect direct intermediate flight attendants of SARS-CoV-2.
Coronaviruses have been detected by betaviruses in an anteulins, which are very closely harmonized with SARS-CoV-2, suggesting that pangolins may be an intermediate flight attendant or that beta coronaviruses in pangolin have contributed to genetic cutting, which has led to the final version of SARS-CoV-2.
Although the questions remain, there is no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 is man-made not intentionally or by chance.
Coronaviruses have returned to the focus due to the start of the recent outbreak of SARS-CoV-2.
The study of coronaviruses in bats and other animals has dramatically changed our idea about the importance of the origins of zoonological diseases and animal reservoirs of human coronaviruses in human transmission.
Conclusive evidence has shown that SARS-Cove, MERS-CoV, and SARS-CoV-2 all have their origin origins in the bat and transmitted to humans via intermediate flight attendants.
Based on the fact that SARS-CoV infection originated from contact between humans and civet cats in the markets, it was assumed that shutting down markets for fresh products and killing civet cats would effectively end the SARS epidemic.
With the same mental logic, pangolins should be disposed of from the markets of fresh products to prevent the transmission of zoonotic disease, in light of the discovery of multiple genealogy of beta coronaviruses in squamous anguitants, which are very close to SARS-CoV-2.
However, whether or not SARS-CoV-2 passed on to humans, as well as no matter the way they were transmitted, through pangolin and other mammals remains a subject that needs to be clarified in future research.
On the other hand, Merce-Cove has been found in single-hull camels for a long time.
These camels are an important means of transportation as well as a major source of meat, milk, skin and wool products for local citizens.
It is widespread throughout the Middle East and Africa region.
It is therefore impossible to sacrifice every camel to control MERS, as officials in China’s wildlife markets have done to prevent the spread of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2.
To stop recurring outbreaks, a holistic approach should be taken to develop efficiencied vaccines against MERS-COF, as well as other infection control measures.
Due to our inability to completely eliminate these viruses, new genetic patterns may arise and cause outbreaks.
A variety of coronaviruses associated with zoonotic diseases are found in wildlife.
In particular, coronaviruses in bats are very diverse, which have the potential to cause zoonotic diseases.
There are many opportunities for the emergence and recombination of coronaviruses associated with zoonotic diseases, which will lead to the emergence of new coronaviruses that are more transmissible and/or deadly in humans in the future.
The culture of eating wild animals in some places in China should be abandoned to minimize unnecessary contact between humans and animals.
In light of the adversity experienced by humanity due to SARS, MERS and COVID-19, an appropriate preparedness and response plan should be prepared.
The fact indicates that a lot of viruses have taken their place on the planet for a very long time.
They live in their own natural reservoirs until they have an opportunity to spread and multiply.
Although bats carry many of the characteristics that favor the spread of viruses, the chance of human contact with bats and other types of wild animals can be reduced if humans adopt a culture that obliges them to stay away from them.
Continual monitoring of mammals is a necessary measure to better understand the environment of coronavirus and their natural hosts, which proves its usefulness in preventing their transmission from animals to humans and preventing future outbreaks.
In conclusion, the best way to prevent zoonogenic viral diseases is to distance humans from the environmental positions of natural reservoirs of zoonogenic pathological viruses.
The mystery of the origin of the zoonotic disease of SARS-CoV-2 is still surrounded by many questions that require answering them.
Firstly, if bats transmit the original virus of SARS-CoV-2 to pangolin, it is important to know the conditions that have been created to share bats and pangolins with a similar environmental place.
Second, if bats play a more direct role in the transmission of the disease to humans, it is then to determine how humans come into contact with them.
Third, if a third species of mammal acts as a true intermediate host, then it should be clarified how it interacts with different species, including humans, bats, and squamous ant eaters.
Finally, since many mammals, including pets, may be suspected of carrying SARS-CoV-2, both monitoring and experimental infections should be performed.
Whether it’s a bat, pangolin or other mammals, SARS-CoV-2 or the father’s viruses from which they branch, which are almost the same as their natural hosts in the future.
Continuing research in this field will reveal the evolutionary trajectory of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, with important actions to prevent and combat COVID-19 in humans.
‘Suspiced case’ and ‘confirmed case’ criteria need to be updated upon diagnosis of COVID-19
On February 6, 2020, our team published rapid guidance on the diagnosis and treatment of the novel coronavirus 2019 (Coronavirus-2019), and this guidance provided our experience and included appropriate reference information to combat the pandemic globally.
Although Coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19) is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge are gradually increasing based on the results of ongoing research and clinical practice experiments; diagnosis and treatment strategies are then constantly updated.
In this letter, we responded to an incoming comment on our guidance and presented the latest diagnostic criteria, a “suspected case” and a “confirmed case,” in accordance with the latest COVID-19 Diagnostics and Treatment Guidelines (VII) of the People’s Republic of the People’s Republic of China.
In December 2019, the novel coronavirus (the novel coronavirus-2019), which is now officially known as the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), has been the outbreak of Coronavirus 2019 and the virus has been named Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) described COVID-19 as a pandemic.
To combat SARS-CoV-2 infection, our team forged a rapid guidance and published it online in Military Medical Research on February 06, 2020.
I've had a lot of attention since it was published.
Please note that although COVID-19 is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge are gradually increasing based on the results of ongoing research and clinical practice experiments; diagnosis and treatment strategies are also constantly updated.
For example, the National Health Commission of the People’s People’s People’s Republic of China/http://www.nhc.gov.cn COVID-19 Diagnostic and Treatment Guidelines issued between January 16, 2020 and March 3, 2020, issued seven editions in total with some contexts fundamentally changed.
Our directive has now received comment from Zhou and others, who have made a simple grading proposal based on the clinical trial.
Their efforts have added a new guide to guide us and it has also become a valuable reference for this pandemic around the world.
We support their great effort and express our appreciation.
However, their work also needs to be updated according to the latest COVID-19 diagnosing and treatment guidelines (bettuial seventh edition) and recent studies.
According to the seventh version (March 3, 2020), the confirmation of the suspected case needs to combine any element of the characteristics of epidemiological history with two elements of clinical manifestations to reach a comprehensive analysis, or it is necessary to verify three elements of clinical manifestations in the absence of a clear epidemiological history:
Epidemiological history: (1) a date that includes traveling to Wuhan and the surrounding areas, or other communities where COVID-19 cases have been reported, during the last 14 days prior to the onset of symptoms; (2) a date that includes communication with cases of SARS-CoV-2 infection (with positive DNA test results); (3) a history that includes communication with patients with fever or respiratory symptoms from Wuhan city, surroundings or other communities where COVID-19 cases have been reported during the last 14 days prior to the onset of symptoms; and (4) a date that includes communication with a cluster of confirmed cases (2 or more cases of fever and/or respiratory symptoms that occurred during 2 weeks in small areas, such as the home, office, classroom, etc.).
Clinical manifestations: (1) fever and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) with biographical screening properties for COVID-19 infection; and (3) showing the total number of white blood cells that are normal, little, or low in the number of lymphates in the early stage of infection.
Diagnosis of the confirmed condition should be based on the suspected case involving any of the evidence elements for the presence of pathogens or serum reactions according to: (1) a positive real-time PCR test result for SARS-CoV-2; (2) the high homogenity of entire viral genomic sequencing with known emerging coronavirus properties; (3) the results of the IgM and SARS-CoV-2 specific antibodie test for SARS-CoV-2 in serum; IgS-CoV-2 special antibody change from negative to positive, or the high calibration in the recovery phase is equal to or more than 4 times that level in the acute stage.
We see that real-time polymerase chain reaction test for respiratory trajectory or blood samples has been added to the second version (January 18, 2020) and III (January 22, 2020).
The discovery of pathogens in the blood sample was added to the fourth version (January 27, 2020) and the fifth (February 8, 2020); a serum reaction guide was then added to the seventh edition.
These modifications were based on the researchers’ ongoing work to reach the design of an ideal DNA detection toolkit for rapid diagnosis, as well as respiratory trajectory samples including blood samples, which led to an increase in the availability of different specimens, and supported the transfer of the positive result of the qualitative antibody to the standards of confirmed cases.
Besides, there’s growing evidence of caution when dealing with patients with unusual symptomatic symptoms and unsymptomatic conditions.
Therefore, the content of the information flow chart for Zhou and others should be updated, classifying a person free of clinical symptomatics as “low risk.”
The grading monitoring system also needs to be validated in additional practice and clinical studies.
In conclusion, we hope to find more direct evidence and we invite readers to provide us with their comments.
To diagnose the ‘suspicious case’ and ‘confirmed case’, we call for and adherence to the latest guidance from countries of origin.
Our team will also update our guidance in a timely manner to provide assistance.
Bangladesh records five new deaths from COVID-19, the highest daily rate.
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new COVID-19 deaths that day.
That's the highest number of deaths in a day due to the virus.
The Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research (IEDCR) in Bangladesh reported that, as of yesterday, the number of registered cases had reached 114 active cases and 33 recoveries who were staying at home.
There have been 17 deaths.
The director of the Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research, Dr. Mirjadi Sabrina Flora, said in an online press conference that the deaths involved four males and one female.
According to Dr. Mirjadi, there were two cases older than 60, two cases between the ages of 51 and 60, and one case between the ages of 41 and 50.
She also said that two of the deaths were from Dhaka.
The World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic on March 11.
An official at a hospital told Anadolu Agency, a local news agency, that one of the dead was Jalal Saif al-Rahman, director of the Bangladesh Anti-Corruption Commission, who was receiving care at Kuwait Maitri Hospital.
Bangladesh’s Minister of Road and Bridge Transport, Obaid al-Qadir, said in an online video statement on Saturday that public transport will be halted longer than initially planned until Saturday.
Disruption of public transport initially began on March 26 and was scheduled to end on Saturday, April 4.
Commodities are still allowed to be transported: medicinal products, fuel and food.
The first cases of COVID-19 in Bangladesh were recorded on March 8, for two people who returned from Italy, as well as the wife of one of them.
The three have already recovered by March 19.
The number of SARS-CoV-2 cases has surpassed one million worldwide.
Data released by Johns Hopkins University on Thursday indicated that the total number of cases of severe acute respiratory coronavirus (SARS-CoV-2) has surpassed one million cases around the world.
Coronavirus-induced COVID-19 has caused at least 52,000 deaths.
This dangerous development came on the same day that Malawi confirmed the first cases of coronavirus, and the first associated death was recorded in Zambia.
North Korea has claimed to be one of the few countries still free of coronavirus infections.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has reported 1,05635 confirmed cases as of yesterday, including 79332 cases in the 24 hours leading up to 10 a.m. Central European Time (0800 UTC) on 4 April.
More than 244,000 cases of coronavirus have been recorded in the United States of America, involving at least 5900 deaths.
Citing data from Johns Hopkins University, CBS News reported that more than 1000 deaths were recorded in the United States on Wednesday caused by a coronavirus infection.
Countries around the world have announced stricter measures to prevent the spread of the disease.
Mr. Sergei Sobyanin, the mayor of Moscow, announced on Thursday that the city’s closure was extended until May 1.
At the national level, President Vladimir Putin announced the continuation of the payment of salaries to Russians without going to work until April 30.
Portugal’s parliament voted on a 15-day extension of the state of national emergency, with 215 abstentions, and one against.
Saudi Arabia extended the curfew in the cities of Mecca and Medina to become throughout the day, as it was previously from 3 p.m. to only 6 a.m.
Thailand plans to impose a curfew from 10 p.m. to 4 a.m.
The Governor of Ohio, Mr. Mike D. Wayne, announced that the state has extended the stay-at-home order until May 1.
Stores in Australia reduce the limits of the purchase of toilet paper per transaction
Australia’s Wall Worthurs and Coles chains on Saturday and Sunday evening tightened restrictions on the purchase of toilets to reduce them to two rolls of Wall Worths stores and one roll of Coloss stores for each transaction in all branches nationwide.
Aldie's stores also scheduled one scroll, on Monday.
These purchase restrictions have been published as messages at payment points in store chains and on their Facebook pages.
Buyers have reportedly stored it due to COVID-19 concerns in case people need self-isolation.
Wall Worthurs on Wednesday approved restrictions on the delivery of toilet paper to the homes to become one roll per purchase order.
The changes came after previous restrictions approved by Wall Worths stores on March 4 and Cooles stores on March 5 by allowing only four rolls to be purchased per transaction.
Cooles stores reported in their media release issued on March 8 that despite the implementation of the four wrap restrictions, "many stores still sell everything they have within one hour of receiving it," describing the order as "unprecedented," as well as Aldie stores described it in a Facebook post on Tuesday as "unexpected."
Sales "significantly increased" in the past week, according to the Wall Worthuys spokesperson.
Last week’s Costco store in Canberra specified that the amount allowed to be purchased is two rolls.
To ease the shortages, Coles stores ordered larger rolls from suppliers and increased the pace of deliveries, and Wall Worths stores ordered additional inventory, while Aldi stores made their inventory available for special offers scheduled for early Wednesday.
Russell Zimmermann, executive director of the Australian Retailers Association, said retailers were trying to increase inventory, but the local board’s restrictions on the timing of truck deliveries make it more difficult.
Production costs are expected to rise, as suppliers try to meet demand, with few special offers.
On Tuesday, Aldie stores announced that as a result of early availability of inventory, some stores would not be able to provide Wednesday’s special offer.
Dr. Gary Mortimer, a retail expert at the Queensland University of Technology, said in a report for News.com.au that stores fill stores every night.
He pointed out that toilet paper is a large commodity, which leads to a decrease in the amount of inventory in terms of numbers, and when sold in full, it leaves large empty spaces on the shelves, which enhances the feeling of inferiority.
Russell Zimmerman stated, according to ABC News, that “Coll’s and Wall Worth shops have the view [that] if a lot of goods are available on the shelves, and if it’s possible [purchasing] products such as toilet rolls and sterilizers and providing them in large quantities, it may reduce panic.”
Who Gives a Crap, which produces recycled toilet paper, said on Wednesday its stock had run out.
According to a report by News.com.au, Kimberley-Clark, which produces Kleinex toilet wipes, and Solaris Pepper, which produces Sorbent wipes, confirmed that they are working 24/7 to continue providing their products.
Domain.com, a real estate website, pointed out that some real estate sellers offer free toilet paper to the first bidder at auctions in Melbourne, while fewer auctions have been held due to buyers stopping working on the long weekend of Labor Day.
Thursday’s edition of NT News, a daily publication in Darwain, included an eight-page advertisement fold made so that it could be cut and used as toilet paper.
Shoppers were initially hesitant about imposing restrictions, according to a report by broadcaster ABC Australia on March 3, with stores saying they had no plans to impose restrictions on purchases.
Russell Zemerman added that there’s also high demand for other products, including masks, sterilizers, dried goods, handwash and flour.
In the same vein, outside of Australia, it was noted on Sunday evening that a British online store called Okadu was identifying Andrés’s toilet paper purchases with two cans of 12 scrolls.
The World Health Organization declares COVID-19 a pandemic.
The World Health Organization (WHO) announced on Wednesday that the ongoing outbreak of COVID-19 – a disease caused by the coronavirus associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) has reached the level of the pandemic.
Although the word “pandemic” refers only to the extent of the spread of the disease, not the seriousness of certain cases, the World Health Organization (WHO) has noted the need to push governments to take the necessary measures:
All countries can still change the trajectory of this epidemic.
WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said: “If countries find out, examine, treat and isolate people, track their cases and mobilize them for confrontation.”
“We’re all very concerned because of the alarming levels of prevalence and severity as well as the alarming levels of inaction.”
According to Dr. Tom Frieden, former director of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the pandemic is “unprecedented.”
“The trajectory of no other respiratory virus has been tracked from the onset to the ongoing global spread, unlike influenza.”
Ghebreyesus expressed a similar view, saying: “We’ve never experienced a pandemic caused by a coronavirus.”
“We’ve never seen a pandemic that can be controlled at the same time.”
The new situation is described as a pandemic following a decision by the World Health Organization (WHO) in January announcing that the outbreak is a public health emergency of international concern.
Dr. Anthony Fauci, director of the U.S. National Allergy and Infectious Disease Institute, said, “The bottom line is that things are going to get worse.”
The Associated Press reported that there have been at least 126,000 cases of COVID-19 around the world from Thursday, and more than 4,600 deaths.
Coronavirus Pandemic 2019-20 is an ongoing pandemic for the coronavirus coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19), caused by coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
An outbreak in Wuhan, China, was identified in December 2019, and a public health emergency was declared of international concern on January 30, 2020.
As of April 10, 2020, approximately 1.61 million COVID-19 cases have been reported in 210 countries and territories, resulting in approximately 97,000 deaths.
About 364,000 people have recovered.
China's mortality rate was estimated at 4%, while globally it ranged from 13.04% in Algiers to 0.08% in New Zealand.
Common symptoms include fever, cough and shortness of breath.
Complications may include pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The period between exposure to symptoms is usually about five days, but it can be between two and fourteen days.
There's no known vaccine or specific antiretroviral treatment.
The primary treatment is supportive therapy for symptoms. Recommended preventative measures include washing hands, covering the mouth when coughing, maintaining interpersonal spacing, monitoring, and self-isolating of those who are suspected.
Authorities around the world are responding to this risk by imposing travel restrictions, quarantines, curfews, workplace risk controls and closures.
This pandemic has led to severe global social and economic turbulence, the postponement or cancellation of sporting, religious, political and cultural events, and widespread supply shortages exacerbated by buying panic.
Schools and universities were closed either nationally or locally in 193 countries, affecting about 99.4 percent of the world’s students.
There have been incidents of xenophobicism and discrimination against Chinese people, and others of East and Southeast Asian origin and appearance, and other areas where significant cases of the virus have emerged.
As a result of the reductions in travel rates and closures for heavy industries, there has been a reduction in air pollution and carbon emissions.
Health authorities in Wuhan, the capital of Hubei province in China, reported a range of pneumonia cases for an undisclosed cause on December 31, 2019, and investigation began in early January 2020.
Cases are mostly related to the Wuhan Seafood market for wholesale, so it is believed that the virus has an animal origin.
The virus that caused SARS-CoV-2 is a newly discovered virus that is closely linked to coronaviruses in bats, coronaviruses in squamous antectives, and SARS-CoV. It was later discovered that the first person with symptoms developed the disease on December 1, 2019, and this person did not have clear contact with the wet market group that was later discovered.
Of the group of early cases reported in December 2019, two-thirds were found to be market-related.
On March 13, 2020, an unverified report published in the South China Morning Journal indicated that a case dating back to November 17, 2019, aged 55 years from Hubei province, was probably the first case. On February 26, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) reported that despite the decline in new cases in China that had been reported, it suddenly rose in Italy, Iran and South Korea, where the number of new cases outside China exceeded the number of new cases within it for the first time.
There may be a significant underreporting of cases, particularly among those with mild symptoms.
By 26 February, relatively few cases had been reported among young people, with adults aged 19 and below accounting for 2.4% of cases worldwide. The UK's chief scientific adviser, Patrick Valance, estimated that 60% of Britons would need to be infected before effective herd immunity was performed.
Cases indicate the number of people who have been tested for COVID-19, and have been confirmed in accordance with official protocols.
As of March 23, no country had tested more than 3% of its population, and many countries had official non-testing policies for people with only mild symptoms, such as Italy, the Netherlands, Spain and Switzerland.
A study published on March 16 found that an estimated 86% of COVID-19 cases had not been detected in China, as of January 23, and that these unregistered infections were the source of 79% of recorded cases.
A statistical analysis published on March 30 estimated that the number of infections in Italy was much higher than those recorded.
Preliminary estimates of the basic reproduction figure (R0) for COVID-19 ranged from 1.4 to 2.4.
A study published by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention concluded that it could be 5.7.
Most people with COVID-19 are recovering.
For those who don't recover, it takes between 6 and 41 days from symptoms to death, the most common of which is 14 days.
As of April 10, 2020, COVID-19 has caused about 97,000 deaths.
Approximately 80% of deaths in China as of February 5 were concentrated in those over the age of 60, and 75% had pre-existing conditions including cardiovascular disease and diabetes. Official COVID-19 mortality statistics generally refer to those who have tested positive for COVID in accordance with official protocols.
The true number of deaths from COVID-19 may be much higher, because it may not include people who die untested – for example; at home, in nursing homes, etc.
Partial data in Italy revealed that the number of excess deaths during the pandemic exceeded the official number of Covid deaths recorded by 4-5x.
A spokeswoman for the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) acknowledged that "we know that [the number of deaths declared] reflects an under-reality estimate," a statement supported by narrative reports of inaccuracy in the United States. This kind of underestimation occurs in cases of pandemics, such as the H1N1 swine influenza pandemic in 2009. The first confirmed death occurred in Wuhan on January 9, 2020.
The first death outside mainland China occurred on February 1 in the Philippines, and the first out-of-Asia death was in France on 14 February.
By February 28, more than twell deaths were recorded outside mainland China in Iran, South Korea and Italy.
By 13 March, more than forty countries and territories reported deaths, on all continents except Antarctica. Many measures are commonly used to determine the mortality rate.
These figures vary by region and over time, and are influenced by the size of the tests, the quality of the health care system, treatment options and the time spent since the outbreak began, and the characteristics of the population such as age, gender, and public health.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, the mortality-to-injury ratio globally is 6.0% (97,039/1,617,204) as of April 10, 2020.
The number varies by region.
In China, estimates of mortality from injuries decreased from 17.3% (for those who developed symptoms from January 1 to January 10, 2020) to 0.7% (for those who developed symptoms after February 1, 2020). Other measures refer to the case mortality rate (CFR), which reflects the percentage of those who died from the disease, and the Infection Mortality Rate (EFR), which reflects the percentage of those infected (those who have tested positive and those who have not been tested) who die from a disease.
These statistics do not have a time frame and follow a specific population group from injury to knowing the fate of the condition.
A number of academics have attempted to calculate these numbers for certain population groups.
The Centre for Evidence-Based Medicine at the University of Oxford estimates that the infection mortality rate for the pandemic as a whole ranges from 0.1% to 0.39%.
The higher estimate of this range corresponds to the results of the first random test for COVID-19 in Germany, and with a statistical study that analyzes the effect of the test in the estimates of the case mortality rate.
The World Health Organization emphasizes the possibility of controlling the pandemic.
The peak of the outbreak and its maximum duration are uncertain and may vary by location.
Massage Bonnie of Pennsylvania State University stated, “Usually, outbreaks of uncontrollable infectious diseases reach their peak and then begin to decline when the disease doesn’t find available hosts.
But it’s almost impossible to reach any logical prediction now about the timing of that.”
China's chief medical adviser Zhong Nanshan said it "could be finished by June" if all countries could be incentivized to follow the World Health Organization's advice on measures to stop the spread of the virus.
On 17 March, Adam Kocharsky of the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine stated that SARS-CoV-2 "would be prevalent for perhaps a year or two".
According to a study conducted by Imperial College of London under the guidance of Neil Ferguson, we will need to rely on physical distancing and other measures "until a vaccine becomes available (possibly after 18 months or more).
“I don’t think this coronavirus is likely that this coronavirus will disappear completely – because it’s easily transmissible,” said William Schaffner of Vanderbilt University and may “turn into a seasonal disease — so that it comes back every year.”
The ferocity of return will depend on collective immunity and the extent of mutation.
Symptoms of COVID-19 may be relatively undefined and may not show symptoms in those infected.
The two most common symptoms are fever (88%) and dry cough (68%).
Less common symptoms include fatigue, respiratory phlegm secretion (phlegm), loss of sense of smell, shortness of breath, muscle and joint pain, sore throat, headaches, chills, vomiting, hemorrhagia, puffing, diarrhea, or cyanosis.
In the United States, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) confines emergency symptoms to difficulty breathing, persistent pain or pressure in the chest, sudden confusion, difficulty waking up, and cyanosis of the face or lips. Immediate medical care is recommended if these symptoms appear.
Some people who don’t have clinical symptoms, but the results of the analyzes confirm their infection, so specialists have provided advice that people who are in contact with confirmed cases should be closely screened to rule out infection.
Chinese estimates of asymptomatic cases range from a few to 44 percent.
The usual incubation period (the period between infection and the appearance of symptoms) ranges from one day to 14 days; it is often five days. As an example of confusion, the population of people with COVID-19 who lost their sense of smell was initially estimated at 30% and recently dropped to 15%.
Some details about how the disease spread are still under investigation.
It is believed that the disease spreads mainly during mingling or sprinkling spray when coughing, sneezing or talking, when mixing at a distance of 1 to 2 meters (3 to 6 feet).
Studies have shown that a cough without covering the mouth may result in a splash scattered from 4.5 meters (15 feet) to 8.2 meters (27 feet).
Some argue that the virus may also be transmitted by spray stuck in the air for a long time, which may be produced while speaking. It may also produce a respiratory spray during exhalation, including when speaking, although the virus is not generally airborne.
Spray may fall on the mouths or noses of nearby people or inhaled into the lungs.
Certain medical procedures, such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CBR), may release respiratory secretions in the form of a spray and then spread through the air.
It may also spread when one touches a contaminated surface, including the skin, and then touches one’s eyes, nose, or mouth.
Although there are concerns about the possibility of spreading from the stool to the mouth, it is believed that this risk is limited.
The Chinese government has denied the possibility that SARS-CoV-2 has been transmitted from feces to the mouth. The virus is more contagious within the first three days of symptoms, although it may spread before any symptoms appear and during later stages of the disease.
People tested positive up to three days before the onset of symptoms, indicating the possibility of transmission before severe symptoms appear.
There are few reports of laboratory-confidered asymptomatic cases, but non-symptomatic transmission has been identified by some countries during contact tracing surveys.
The European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) confirms that despite some ambiguity about how easily the disease spreads, one person can enumerate from two to three others in general. The virus remains on surfaces from hours to days.
Specifically, it was found that the virus could be detected for up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and 304 stainless steel, one-day on cardboard, and up to four hours on copper.
However, this varies depending on the humidity and temperature. Pets and other animals have been confirmed infected with COVID-19.
There is no evidence that animals can transmit the virus to humans, however, British authorities recommend washing hands after contact with animals, such as when touching other surfaces that may have been touched by an infected person.
Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is an emerging virus, first isolated from three people who were experiencing pneumonia in contact with a group of cases of acute respiratory disease in Wuhan.
The SARS-CoV-2 virus shares all its properties with coronaviruses found in nature. Outside of the human body, domestic soap kills the virus, as it dissolves its protective membrane.
It's believed to have animal origin.
The genetic analysis revealed that the coronavirus meets genetically with the gender of the beta coronavirus, and falls under the sub-sex of the coronavirus Sarbi (B strain) in addition to two strains originating bats.
It corresponds to 96% at the level of the entire genome with other samples of coronaviruses in bats (Bat-Cove Rat-13).
In February 2020, Chinese researchers discovered that there was only one difference in amino acids in certain parts of genomic sequences between viruses originating from pangolins and viruses originating in humans.
Comparing the entire genome to date has resulted in the discovery of the maximum sharing of 92% of the genetic material between the coronavirus of the squasil eater and the SARS-CoV-2 virus, which is not enough to prove that the pangolin is the intermediate host of this virus.
The virus can be temporarily diagnosed based on symptoms, although confirmation is ultimately made by a polymerase-transal reaction for reverse transcription (RRT-PCR) of infected secretions or computed tomography.
A study in Wuhan that compares polymerase chain reactions and computed tomography suggests that CT scans are more sensitive than polymerase chain reaction, although it is less accurate, with many of its imaging characteristics interfering with pneumonia cases and other pathological phases.
As of March 2020, the American College of Radiography recommends that "computed tomography not be used to detect COVID-19 or consider it a preliminary test to diagnose the disease."
The World Health Organization (WHO) has published several RNA testing protocols for SARS-CoV-2, for the first time on 17 January.
The test uses reversible polymerase chain reaction (RRT-PCR) in real time.
The test can be performed on samples of the respiratory tract or blood.
Results are generally available within a few hours to days.
This test is generally done with a nasopharyngeal swab, and can also be used from the throat. A number of laboratories and companies are developing synthesized antibodie tests that detect antibodies.
As of April 6, 2020, none have been sufficiently provined for large-scale adoption.
A serum test developed by Selex has been approved for emergency use in the United States only in accredited laboratories.
Imaging properties for radiographs and CCT scans for people with symptoms of the disease include asymmetric peripheral oasphemy, such as fogging glass blurry and blurred crystalline infiltration.
The Italian Society of Radiation collects an international online database of imaging results for confirmed cases.
Radiography without polymerase chain reaction is a limited-precision procedure for detecting COVID-19 because it interferes with other diseases such as adenovirus infection.
A large study in China compared the results of chest tomography and polymerase chain reaction and proved that although imaging is less accurate in terms of proving infection, it is faster and more sensitive, suggesting that it is considered an examination tool in endemic areas.
Artificial intelligence-based convolutional neural networks have been developed to reveal the characteristics of virus imaging in both radiation and computed tomography.
Strategies to prevent the transmission of the disease include maintaining good personal hygiene in general, washing hands, avoiding contact with the eyes, nose or mouth with unwashed hands, and using a handkerchief when coughing or sneezing and disposing of it by throwing it directly into the trash.
Those who carry the infection have already been advised to wear a medical mask in public places.
Physical distancing measures are also recommended to prevent transmission. Several governments have banned non-essential travel to and from countries and regions affected by the outbreak or advised against travel.
However, the virus has reached the stage of spreading throughout society in large parts of the world.
This means that the virus is spreading within communities, and some individuals don’t know where or how they’ve contracted it. Healthcare providers, who take care of a potentially infected person, are advised to use considerable measures, mixing precautions and eye protection.
Governments’ use of locating data via mobile phones for tracking has raised privacy concerns, with Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations issuing a statement calling for the restriction of such surveillance.
As of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen specialized groups have worked to find privacy solutions, such as using Bluetooth to record the user’s proximity to other mobile phones.
Users then receive a message if they approach someone who has tested positive for COVID-19. Misconceptions about how to prevent an infection are promoted; for example, nasal washing and gargling with mouthwash is not a viable solution.
There’s still no vaccine for COVID-19, although many organizations are working to produce a vaccine.
It is advisable to wash hands to prevent the spread of the disease.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that people wash their hands frequently with soap and water for at least twenty seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when dirt appears on the hands, before eating, and after emptying the nose, coughing or sneezing.
That’s because household soap kills the virus outside of the body, where it destroys its protective membrane.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention has also recommended the use of a hand sterilizer that contains at least 60% alcohol when soap and water are not available.
The World Health Organization (WHO) advises people to avoid touching their eyes, nose or mouth with unwashed hands.
Surfaces can be disinfected using multiple disinfectants (within one minute of applying the disinfectant to the surface of the stainless steel), including; ethanol 62–71%, isopropanol 50–100% sodium hypochlorite 0.1%, hydrogen peroxide 0.5%, and iodophidone 0.2–7.5%.
Other disinfectants, such as benzalconium chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate, are found less effectively.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that when a person is suspected of having or confirmed to be infected in a facility such as an office or care center, all places such as offices, bathrooms, shared spaces, shared electronic devices such as tablets, touch screens, keyboards, remote controls, and ATMs used by the patient should be sterilized.
Health organizations recommended that people cover their mouths and noses when coughing or sneezing either bend the elbow and placing it in front of the mouth and nose or using the paper napkin and getting rid of any napkins immediately.
It is recommended for those who may be infected using medical masks, as wearing the mask reduces the size of the respiratory spray and the distance it travels when scattered due to talking, sneezing and coughing.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has issued instructions on the timing and how to use masks.
According to Stephen Griffin, a virologist at the University of Leeds, “wearing a mask can reduce [people’s] desire to touch their faces, which is a major source of infection in the event that the hands are not properly cleaned.”
The World Health Organization (WHO) has recommended that healthy people not wear masks unless they are exposed to the possibility of high infection, such as those caring for COVID-19, although it also acknowledges that wearing masks may help people avoid touching their faces.
Several countries have begun to encourage ordinary people to wear face masks.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends wearing a non-medical face mask made of fabric. China specifically recommended that healthy people use disposable medical masks, especially in the case of direct contact with people (at a distance of 1 meter (3 feet) or less).
Hong Kong recommends wearing a medical mask when using public transportation or staying in crowded places.
Thai health officials encourage individuals to make cloth masks at home and washe them daily.
The Czech Republic and Slovakia have banned going out to public places without wearing a mask or covering the nose and mouth.
On March 16, Vietnam asked everyone to wear masks when going to public places to protect themselves and others.
The Austrian government has required anyone entering a grocery store to wear a mask.
Israel has asked all residents to wear masks in public.
Taiwan, which has produced ten million masks a day since mid-March, has asked passengers on trains and interurban buses to wear masks on April 1.
Panama has made wearing a face mask when going out necessary, while also recommending making face masks at home for those who can’t buy face masks.
Facial masks are also widely used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, and Singapore.
Social distancing (also known as physical distancing) includes infection control measures aimed at slowing the spread of the disease by minimizing the mixing of individuals.
Roads include quarantine; travel restrictions; and closure of schools, workplaces, playgrounds, theatres or shopping malls.
Individuals can apply social distancing methods by staying at home, limiting travel, avoiding crowded areas, excising greetings without contact, and being physically distant from others.
Many governments currently impose or recommend social distancing in areas affected by the outbreak.
The maximum cluster size recommended by U.S. government agencies and health organizations was quickly reduced from 250 people (if COVID-19 hasn't been detected in the region) to 50 people, and 10 later.
On 22 March 2020, Germany banned public gatherings of more than two people. The elderly and those suffering from chronic diseases such as diabetes, heart disease, respiratory diseases, high blood pressure, and weakened immune systems face an increased risk of developing serious diseases and complications. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention has advised them to stay at home as much as possible in inter-communal outbreak areas.
The use of the term ‘social distancing’ has led to suggesting that people should engage in complete social isolation, rather than encouraging them to communicate with others through alternative means. Some authorities have issued sexual health directives for use during the pandemic.
This includes recommendations to only have sex with someone you live with who is not a carrier of the virus or has symptoms.
Self-isolation at home is recommended for people with COVID-19 and those who suspect them.
Health agencies have issued detailed instructions for proper self-isolation. Several governments have imposed or recommended self-quarantine to all residents living in the affected areas.
The strongest self-quarantine instructions have been issued for those of the most vulnerable.
Those who have dealt with someone with COVID-19 and those who have recently traveled to a country or area where the infection is widely transmitted have self-quarantined for 14 days from the time of the last possible exposure.
Strategies to control outbreaks are containment or braking and limiting spread.
Containment is applied in the early stages of the outbreak and aims to track and isolate the infected, as well as introducing other infection control measures and vaccines to prevent the disease from spreading to the rest of the population.
Whas it becomes impossible to contain the spread of the disease, efforts are then directed to mitigation: measures are taken to slow the spread and mitigate its effects on the health care system and society.
A combination of both containment and mitigation measures can be carried out at the same time.
Braking requires stricter measures to reverse the trajectory of the pandemic by reducing the baseline reproduction number to less than 1. Part of the management of infectious disease outbreaks is trying to reduce the peak of the epidemic, which is known as flattening the curve of the epidemic.
This reduces the risk of straining health services and saves more time for the development of vaccines and treatments.
Non-pharmaceutical interventions that may address outbreaks include personal preventive measures, such as hand hygiene, masking and self-quarantine; community actions aimed at physical distancing such as closing schools and canceling major gathering events; community solidarity to encourage acceptance and participation in such interventions; and environmental measures such as surface cleaning.
Other countries have also taken a variety of measures aimed at curbing the spread of the virus.
South Korea has introduced mass testing and local quarantines and issued alerts about the movements of the injured.
Singapore provided financial support to self-isolated patients and imposed heavy fines on those who did not.
Taiwan has increased the production of masks and criminalized the hoarding of medical supplies. Simulations of Great Britain and the United States show that mitigating (slowing the spread of the epidemic, not stopping it) and curbing (reversing the growth of the epidemic) face significant challenges.
Optimal mitigation policies may halve peak demand for health care by 2/3 and deaths by half, but still result in hundreds of thousands of deaths and strain health systems.
Braking may be preferred but must be preserved as long as the virus spreads in humans (or until the vaccine becomes available, if this happens first), as transmission on the other hand rebounds quickly when measures are eased.
Long-term intervention to curb the pandemic causes social and economic costs.
There are no specific antiviral drugs approved for COVID-19, but development efforts are underway, including testing for existing drugs.
Taking over-the-counter cold medications, drinking fluids and resting may help relieve symptoms.
Depending on the severity, treatment with oxygen, intravenous fluids and respiratory support may be required.
The use of steroids may aggravate the results.
Many pre-approved compounds for the treatment of other viral diseases are being screened for use in the treatment of COVID-19.
The World Health Organization has also stated that some “traditional and home remedies” can provide relief for the symptoms caused by SARS COVID-19.
Increasing capacity and adapting health care centers to meet the needs of COVID-19 patients have been described by the World Health Organization (WHO) as key measures to respond to the outbreak.
The European Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDCP) and the World Health Organization (WHO) European Regional Office have issued guidelines for hospitals and primary health-care services to transfer resources at multiple levels, including focusing laboratory services on COVID-19 testing, abolishing elective procedures as much as possible, separating and isolating COVID-19 patients, increasing intensive care capacity through staff training and increasing the number of ventilators and beds available.
There are multiple theories as to where the first case originated (the so-called patient zero).
The first known case of the novel coronavirus may date back to December 1, 2019 in Wuhan, Bhobei, China.
Within a month, the number of coronavirus cases in Hubei gradually increased.
These were mostly associated with the Wuhan Seafood Wholesale Market, which also sells living animals, and one theory says that the virus came from one of these animal species; in other words, it has animal origin. A group infected with pneumonia was observed for an unknown reason on December 26 and was handled by Dr. Zhang Jishian at Hubei County Hospital, who informed the Center for Disease Control in Jianghan-Puhan on December 27.
On December 30, a group of doctors at Wuhan Central Hospital warned their colleagues about “the SARS coronavirus.”
Police alerted eight of these doctors, including Li Wen Liang, for spreading false rumours, and another doctor, Ai Fin, was reprimanded by her superiors for raising the alarm.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Commission later issued a public notice on 31 December and informed the World Health Organization.
Sufficient cases of unknown pneumonia were reported to health authorities in Wuhan, promptinging them to begin screening in early January. During the early stages of the outbreak, the number of cases doubled almost every seven and a half days.
In early and mid-January 2020, the virus spread to other Chinese provinces, aided by commutations during the Chinese New Year and because Wuhan is a transportation hub and the intersection point for major railway lines.
On January 20, China reported nearly 140 new cases in a single day, including two in Beijing and one in Shenzhen.
As of March 26, the United States has surpassed China and Italy with the highest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, more than 1.61 million cases have been reported worldwide, with more than 97,000 people dying and recovering more than 364,000.
About 200 countries and territories have registered at least one case.
Due to the pandemic in Europe, many countries in the Schengen area restricted freedom of movement and imposed border controls.
As of 2 April, nearly 300 million people, or about 90% of the population, have been subjected to a form of lockdown in the United States, more than 50 million people in the Philippines, some 59 million people, or about 90% of the population, have undergone closure in the United States.
On March 26, 1.7 billion people around the world underwent some form of lockdown, which rose to 2.6 billion two days later—about one-third of the world’s population.
The first confirmed case of COVID-19 dates back to December 1, 2019 in Wuhan; one unconfirmed report indicates that the first case was on 17 November.
Dr. Zhang Jishiyan noticed a group of pneumonia cases caused by an unknown cause on December 26, and her hospital was subsequently informed by the Center for Disease Control in Jianghan Bohan on December 27.
An initial gene test for patient samples on December 27, 2019, indicated the presence of SARS-like coronavirus.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Commission issued a public notice on December 31.
The World Health Organization (WHO) was informed on the same day.
In the presence of these notifications, doctors in Wuhan have been warned by the police about “spreading rumours” about the outbreak.
China’s National Health Commission initially claimed there was no “clear evidence” of person-to-person transmission.
In late January, the Chinese government launched a drastic campaign that was later described by Chinese Communist Party General Secretary Xi Jinping as a "popular war" to contain the spread of the virus.
Described as the "biggest quarantine in human history," a sanitary collar was announced on 23 January to stop travel to and from Wuhan, spanning a total of 15 cities in Hubei and affecting about 57 million people.
The use of private vehicles in the city has been banned.
Chinese New Year celebrations (January 25) have been canceled in several places.
The authorities also announced the construction of a temporary hospital, the Hushinshan Hospital, which was completed within 10 days.
Another hospital was then built, Lishinshan Hospital, to deal with additional patients.
In addition to the newly established hospitals, China has also converted 14 other facilities in Wuhan, such as conference centers and stadiums, into temporary hospitals. On January 26, the government took other measures to contain the COVID-19 outbreak, including providing health announcements to travelers and extending the Spring Festival holiday.
Universities and schools have also been closed across the country.
The Hong Kong and Macao regions have taken several measures, particularly with regard to schools and universities.
Removal action measures have been taken in several Chinese regions.
Restrictions were imposed on travel inside and outside Hubei.
Public transportation has been altered, and museums have been temporarily closed across China.
Public movement has been tightened in several cities, and it is estimated that about 760 million people (more than half of the population) have been subjected to some form of exit restrictions. After the outbreak became global in March, the Chinese authorities took strict measures to prevent the virus from being “brought” to them from other countries.
For example, Beijing imposed a 14-day mandatory quarantine on all returnees to the city from other countries. On March 23, one case was transmitted locally in mainland China over the past five days, through a traveler returning to Guangzhou from Istanbul.
On March 24, 2020, Chinese Premier Li Keqiang stated that the spread of locally transmitted infections and the control of outbreaks in China has receded.
Travel-related restrictions in Hubei were eased on the same day, with the exception of Wuhan, after two months the lockdown was imposed. On March 26, 2020, the Chinese Foreign Ministry announced the suspension of entry for holders of visas or residence permits from March 28 onwards, with no specific details on when this policy expired.
Those wishing to enter China will be forced to apply for visas at Chinese embassies or consulates.
The Chinese government urged companies and factories to resume work on March 30, and offered a package of monetary incentives to companies. The State Council announced a day of mourning that begins with three minutes of silence on April 4 at 10:00, coinciding with the Qingming Festival, although the central government called on families to show their respect online in order to take into account the physical distancing to avoid the outbreak of COVID-19 again.
The transmission of COVID-19 from China to South Korea was confirmed on January 20, 2020.
The National Health Agency announced a significant increase in confirmed cases on February 20, largely due to a rally in Daejo for a new religious movement known as the Shincheonji Church of Jesus.
It was suspected that the followers of Shincheonji visiting Daegu from Wuhan were the source of the outbreak.
As of 22 February, 1,261 people, or about 13% of the 9,336 church attendants, reported symptoms. South Korea announced the highest alert levels on February 23, 2020.
On February 28, more than 2,000 confirmed cases were reported in Korea, rising to 3,150 on 29 February.
All South Korean military bases were quarantined after three soldiers tested positive for the virus.
Flight schedules were also affected, and then changed. South Korea has implemented a program that has been considered the world’s largest and best regulated to screen people for the virus, isolating anyone infected, and tracking and quarantining those who came into contact.
Screening methods included mandatory self-reporting of symptoms by international newcomers via the mobile application, testing the virus on people in their cars with test results available the next day, and increasing testing capabilities to 20,000 people per day.
South Korea's program represents a success in controlling the outbreak even though quarantine has not been applied to entire cities. The South Korean community was initially divided over President Moon Jae-in's response to the crisis.
Many Koreans have signed petitions either demanding Moon’s dismissal, alleging the government’s mishandling of the outbreak, or applauding his response.
On March 23, it was announced that South Korea had the lowest total cases in a single day in four weeks.
On 29 March, it was reported that from April 1, all new arrivals from abroad would be quarantined for two weeks.
According to media reports on April 1, South Korea has received requests from 121 different countries for assistance in testing the virus.
Iran reported its first confirmed case of SARS-CoV-2 on February 19 in Qom, where two people died later the same day, according to the Ministry of Health and Medical Education.
The early measures announced by the government included the cancellation of concerts and other cultural events and sporting events, Friday prayers, the closure of universities, institutes of higher education, and schools.
Iran has allocated five trillion riyals to combat the virus.
President Hassan Rouhani stated on February 26, 2020 that there is no plan to quarantine areas affected by the outbreak, and only individuals will be isolated.
Plans were announced to limit intercity travel in March, although heavy inter-city traffic continued before the Nowruz Persian New Year.
Shiite shrines remained open to pilgrims until March 16, 2020. In February, Iran became the epicenter of the spread of the virus after China.
Amid allegations that Iran has covered up over the outbreak, more than a dozen countries have tracked their cases returning from Iran as of February 28, indicating that the extent of the outbreak may exceed the 388 cases reported by the Iranian government to date.
Iran’s parliament has been shut down, with 23 of its 290 members reportedly tested positive for the virus on March 3.
On March 12, Human Rights Watch called on Iranian prison authorities to unconditionally release human rights defenders detained because of peaceful dissent, as well as temporary release of eligible prisoners.
She stated that there is a high risk of the spread of the virus in enclosed places such as detention centers, which also lack adequate medical care.
On 15 March, the Iranian government announced 100 deaths in a single day, the highest number recorded in the country since the outbreak began.
At least 12 Iranian politicians and current and former government officials have died of the disease by March 17.
By March 23, Iran was registering 50 new infections per hour, and one death every 10 minutes due to the coronavirus.
According to an official at the World Health Organization, the number of cases in Iran may be up to five times what has been announced.
U.S. sanctions on Iran are also believed to potentially affect the country’s financial ability to respond to the outbreak.
The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR) has called for an easing of economic sanctions on the countries most affected by this pandemic, including Iran.
The outbreak in Italy was confirmed on January 31, when SARS-CoV-2 tests for Chinese tourists in Rome tested positive.
Cases began to increase sharply, prompting the Italian government to suspend all flights to and from China and declare a state of emergency.
An unrelated group of COVID-19 cases was later discovered, starting with 16 confirmed cases in Lombardy on 21 February. On 22 February, the Council of Ministers announced a decree-law to contain the outbreak, including quarantining more than 50,000 people from 11 different municipalities in northern Italy.
Prime Minister Giuseppe Conté said, “It will not be allowed to enter or exit the outbreak areas.
On 4 March, the Italian government ordered the complete closure of all schools and universities throughout the country with 100 deaths in Italy.
All major sporting events, including first division football matches, were scheduled behind closed doors until April, but on 9 March, all sporting events were completely suspended for at least one month.
On 11 March, Prime Minister Conté ordered to stop almost all commercial activities, except supermarkets and pharmacies. On 6 March, the Italian College of Anesthesia, Housing, Resuscitation and Intensive Care (SIARTI) published medical ethics recommendations regarding screening protocols that could be used.
On March 19, Italy surpassed China as the country with the highest number of coronavirus-related deaths in the world after 3,405 deaths were reported due to the pandemic.
On 22 March, Russia dispatched nine military aircraft carrying medical equipment to Italy.
As of 5 April, the number of confirmed cases amounted to 128,948, the deaths amounted to 15,887, and 21,815 recovered in Italy, most of which were in the Lombardy region.
A combination of a large number of older people and the inability to test all those infected with the virus so far in Italy may have contributed to a rise in mortality.
The UK’s response to the virus first emerged as the most relaxed among the affected countries, and as of 18 March 2020, the British government did not impose any form of social distancing or comprehensive quarantine measures on its citizens.
As a result, the government has been criticised for its perceived lack of speed and severity in its response to concerns faced by the public. On 16 March, Prime Minister Boris Johnson issued a statement advisinging to avoid non-necessarious travel or social contact, suggesting people working from home as much as possible and avoiding several places, such as bars, restaurants and theatres.
On 20 March, the government announced that it would close all leisure facilities such as bars and gyms as soon as possible, and promised to pay up to 80% of workers' wages up to a maximum of £2,500 per month to reduce unemployment at the time of the crisis. On 23 March, the Prime Minister announced stricter social distancing measures, banning gatherings of more than two people and restricting travel and activity outside the home unless absolutely necessary.
Contrary to the previous measures, these restrictions were implemented by the police by issuing fines and dispersing gatherings.
Most companies have been ordered to shut down, with the exception of “necessary” businesses, including supermarkets, pharmacies, banks, hardware stores, petrol stations, and grafts.
On January 20, the first known case of COVID-19 was confirmed in the north-west Pacific state of a man returning from Wuhan on January 15.
The White House Coronavirus Task Force was prepared on January 29.
On January 31, the Trump administration declared a public health emergency and imposed entry restrictions on travelers from China.
On January 28, 2020, the Centers for Disease Control (CDC), the U.S. government's leading public health institute, announced that it had developed its own test kit.
Despite doing so, the U.S. slowed to begin testing, obscuring the true extent of the outbreak at the time.
The test spoiled a flawed test kit produced by the federal government in February, the lack of federal government approval for non-governmental test kits (by academia, companies, and hospitals) until the end of February, and the restrictive criteria for people to qualify for the test until early March (the doctor's order was subsequently required).
By February 27, The Washington Post reported that less than 4,000 tests had been conducted in the United States.
By March 13, The Atlantic reported that fewer than 14,000 tests had been conducted.
On 22 March, the Associated Press reported: "Many people who have experienced symptoms and have a doctor's order waited hours or days for testing." After reporting the first death in the United States in Washington State on February 29, Governor Jay Insley declared a state of emergency, a measure that was soon followed by other states.
Schools in the city of Seattle canceled study on March 3, and by mid-March, schools had been closed across the country. On March 6, 2020, the United States had been informed of projections regarding the impact of the novel coronavirus on the country by epidemiologists at Imperial College London.
On the same day, President Trump signed the Supplementary Appropriations for Coronavirus Preparedness and Response Act, which provided $8.3 billion in emergency funding to federal agencies to respond to the outbreak.
Companies imposed travel restrictions on employees, canceled conferences, and urged employees to work from home.
On March 11, Trump announced travel restrictions for most of Europe, excluding the United Kingdom, for 30 days, effective March 13.
The next day, the restrictions were extended to the United Kingdom and Ireland.
On 13 March, he declared a state of national emergency, which allowed federal funds to be used to respond to the crisis.
As of March 15, many companies have shut down or reduced their working hours across the United States in an effort to limit the spread of the virus.
By March 17, the epidemic had been confirmed in all 50 states and in the District of Columbia. On March 23, there were 10,700 coronavirus cases in New York City, which is more than the total number of cases in South Korea.
On 25 March, the governor said social distancing appears to have been successful, with estimates showing a slowing doubling of cases from 2.0 to 4.7 days.
As of 28 March, there were 32,308 confirmed cases in New York City, and 672 people died from the virus. On March 26, more confirmed cases of coronavirus were reported in the United States than in any other country in the world, including China and Italy. As of April 8, 400,335 were confirmed in the United States, and 12,841 people died.
According to media reports on March 30, U.S. President Trump decided to extend social distancing directives until April 30.
On the same day, the U.S.S. Comfort, a hospital ship with about 1000 beds, docked in New York.
On 3 April, the United States recorded 884 deaths from the coronavirus within 24 hours.
In the state of New York, the number of cases exceeded 100,000 on April 3. The White House has been criticised for mocking the threat and control of news circulation by directing health officials and scientists to coordinate public statements and publications related to the virus with the Office of Vice President Mike Pence.
The Trump administration’s crisis has not been universally acclaimed as it has polarized parties.
Some U.S. officials and commentators have criticized U.S. reliance on importing essential items, including essential medical supplies, from China.
Analysing air travel patterns was used to map and predict spread patterns and was published in the Journal of Travel Medicine in mid-January 2020.
Based on information from the International Air Transport Association 2018, Bangkok, Hong Kong, Tokyo and Taipei had the largest number of passengers from Wuhan.
Dubai, Sydney and Melbourne have also been reported as popular destinations for travellers from Wuhan.
Bali was reported as the least capable of more than 20 popular destinations in terms of preparedness, while cities in Australia were considered to be the most capable. Australia released its COVID-19 emergency response plan on 7 February.
It stated that much has not yet been discovered about the COVID-19 virus, and that Australia will emphasize border control and communications in its response to the pandemic.
On March 21, a human biosecurity emergency was declared in Australia.
As public transport has been affected by effective Wuhan and Hubei quarantine, several countries have planned to evacuate their citizens and diplomatic staff from the region, primarily through charter flights from their countries of origin, with Chinese authorities providing permits.
Canada, the United States, Japan, India, Sri Lanka, Australia, France, Argentina, Germany and Thailand were among the first countries to plan to evacuate their citizens.
Pakistan has announced that it will not evacuate any of its citizens from China.
On 7 February, Brazil evacuated 34 Brazilians or family members as well as four Poles, a Chinese person, and an Indian citizen.
Polish, Chinese and Indian nationals disembarkated from the plane in Poland, where the Brazilian plane stopped before resuming its route to Brazil.
Brazilian citizens who went to Wuhan at a military base near Brasilia were deposed.
On the same day, 215 Canadians (176 from the first plane, and 39 from a second plane chartered by the U.S. government) were evacuated from Wuhan to the Canadian Forces-Trenton base to isolate them for two weeks.
On 11 February, another plane carrying 185 Canadians from Wuhan landed at the Canadian Troops-Trenton base.
Australian authorities evacuated 277 nationals on 3 and 4 February to the Christmas Island Detention Centre, which was reused as a quarantine facility, where they stayed for 14 days.
A New Zealand evacuation flight arrived in Auckland on 5 February, and its passengers (including people from Australia and the Pacific) were isolated at a naval base in Wangabarawa in northern Auckland.
On 15 February, the United States announced that it would evacuate Americans who were aboard the cruise ship Diamond Princess.
On 21 February, a plane landed in Trenton, Ontario, carrying 129 Canadian passengers evacuated from the Diamond Princess.
In early March, the Indian government began evacuating its citizens from Iran. On March 14, a South African Airlines aircraft chartered by the South African government returned 112 South African citizens.
A pre-departure check-up was carried out, and four South Africans who showed signs of coronavirus were left to mitigate the risks.
South Africans whose test results were only negative were returned.
The results of the test proved that the South Africans, including flight crews, pilots, hotel staff, police and soldiers involved in the humanitarian mission, all of whom, as a precautionary measure, under surveillance and quarantine in the resort of Ranch for 14 days, were uninjured.
On 20 March, the U.S. began partially withdrawing its troops from Iraq due to the pandemic.
On 5 February, China’s Foreign Ministry reported that 21 countries (including Belarus, Pakistan, Trinidad, Tobago, Egypt, and Iran) had sent aid to China.
Some Chinese students at U.S. universities came together to send aid to HIV-positive areas of China, and a joint group in the Greater Chicago area reportedly sent 50,000 N95 masks to hospitals in Hubei County on January 30. Direct Relief, in coordination with FedEx, sent 200,000 face masks with other personal protective equipment, including gloves and hospital staff clothing, via the Air Emergency Bridge, to Wuhan Union Hospital by January 30.
On 5 February, Bill and Melinda Gates announced a $100 million donation to the World Health Organization (WHO) to fund vaccine research and treatment efforts, along with protecting “at-risk populations in Africa and South Asia.”
The Chinese government donated 200,000 masks to the Philippines on February 6, after Senator Richard Gordon shipped 3.16 million masks to Wuhan.
On 19 February, the Singapore Red Cross announced that it would send China $2.26 million in aid.
Russia sent more than 13 tons of medical supplies to Wuhan, Malaysia donated 18 million medical gloves to China, Germany delivered several medical supplies, including 10,000 protective suits, the United States donated 17.8 tons of medical supplies, and promised another financial support of $100 million to the affected countries.
In March, China, Cuba and Russia sent medical supplies and experts to help Italy deal with the coronavirus outbreak.
Businessman Jacques Ma sent 1.1 million test kits to Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, 6 million face masks and 60,000 protective suits distributed by the African Union.
He later sent to Panama 5,000 test kits, 100,000 face masks and 5 ventilators.
The Netherlands, Spain, Turkey, Georgia, and the Czech Republic have expressed concern about masks and test kits made in China.
For example, Spain withdrew 58,000 coronavirus test kits made in China that were only 30 percent accurate, and in the meantime, the Netherlands returned 600,000 face masks made in China that were flawed.
Belgium returned 100,000 unusable face masks that were believed to be from China, but were in fact from Colombia.
Parts of Latin America and Africa, on the other hand, welcomed Chinese aid well. On 2 April, the World Bank launched emergency support operations for developing countries.
The World Health Organization (WHO) praised the efforts of the Chinese authorities in managing and containing the epidemic.
The World Health Organization (WHO) noted the discrepancy between 2002 and 2004, where Chinese authorities have been accused of obfuscating prevention and containment efforts, and the current crisis, where the central government has provided “regular updates to avoid panic before the Lunar New Year holiday.”
On 23 January, in response to the decision of the central authorities to implement a transport ban in Wuhan, WHO representative Gooden Galia noted that while this was "certainly not a recommendation made by the World Health Organization," it was "a very important indicator of the commitment to containing the epidemic where it is highly concentrated," describing it as "unprecedented in the history of public health". On January 30, after confirminging person-to-person transmission outside China, and an increase in the number of cases in other countries, the World Health Organization declared an outbreak as a public-health emergency of international concern (BHIC), the sixth case of public health emergencies of international concern since this procedure was first resorted to during the swine flu pandemic in 2009.
The Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) said that considering the outbreak as a public health emergency of international concern is due to “the risk of global spread, especially in low- and middle-income countries that do not have strong health systems.
In response to the implementation of travel restrictions, Tedros stated that “there is no reason to take measures that unnecessarily conflict with international travel and trade” and that “the WHO does not recommend limiting trade and mobility.”
On 5 February, the World Health Organization (WHO) appealed to the international community to contribute $675 million to strategic funds in low-income countries, citing the urgent need to support those countries that “do not have systems for detecting people living with the virus, even if it appears.”
Tedros also made statements in which he declared that "our strength is measured by the strength of the weakest person among us" and urged the international community to "invest today or pay a big price later". On 11 February, the World Health Organization (WHO), at a press conference, named the COVID-19.
On the same day, Tedros stated that UN Secretary-General António Guterres had agreed to offer “the potential of the entire United Nations system to respond.”
As a result, a United Nations crisis management team has been developed, which has allowed the coordination of the entire U.N. response, and the World Health Organization (WHO) will allow it to “focus on the health response, while the expertise of other agencies can be leveraged to learn about the broader social, economic, and developmental impacts of the outbreak.”
On 14 February, a joint World Health Organization (WHO) mission team with China was prepared to provide international experts and experts from the World Health Organization (WHO) on the ground in China to assist in local governance and assess "the severity and transmissibility of the disease" by hosting workshops and meetings with key national institutions, and conducting field visits to assess "the impact of response activities at the provincial and city levels, including urban and rural areas". On 25 February, the World Health Organization announced that "the world must do more to prepare for the potential coronavirus pandemic," noting that it is premature to describe it as a pandemic, but that countries should be "in preparedness."
In response to the outbreak in Iran, the World Health Organization (WHO) dispatched a joint mission team there to assess the situation. On 28 February, WHO officials said that a global coronavirus threat assessment would rise from "high" to "extremely high," the highest level for warning and risk assessment.
Mike Ryan, executive director of WHO’s health emergency program, warned in a statement: “This is a real test for every government on earth, so we have to prepare.
This virus may be on its way and you have to prepare for it," he said, urging the right response measures that could help the world avoid "worse".
Ryan also stated that the current data does not justify public health officials announcing the disease as a global pandemic, saying that such an announcement means “we fundamentally accept that everyone on this planet will be exposed to this virus.”
On 11 March, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared a coronavirus outbreak as a pandemic.
The Director-General said that the World Health Organization (WHO) is "deeply concerned about alarming levels of prevalence, severity, and alarming levels of inaction". The World Health Organization (WHO) has faced severe criticism of what is seen as inadequate treatment of the pandemic, including the delay in declaring a public health emergency and classifying the virus as a pandemic.
Violent reactions included a petition to WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom to resign, and 733,000 people signed it as of April 6.
On March 26, 2020, dozens of UN human rights experts affirmed respect for everyone’s rights during the COVID-19 pandemic.
The group of experts stated that everyone has the right to life-saving interventions and the government bears this responsibility.
The group stressed that a lack of resources or health insurance should not justify discrimination against a particular group.
The experts stressed that everyone has the right to health, including persons with disabilities, members of minorities, the elderly, internally displaced persons, homeless people, those living in very poor conditions, detainees, as well as refugees and other unspecified groups that need government support.
Intergovernmental organizations address the economic and social impacts of the COVID-19 crisis.
The OECD has launched a platform to provide comprehensive and timely information on policy responses in countries around the world, as well as perspectives and advice.
From policies to strengthen health systems and the global economy to address the effects of lockdowns and restrictions on travel, the digital axis includes a state policy tracking tool, which aims to help countries learn from each other and facilitate a coordinated global response to the coronavirus.
The Chinese government has been criticised by the United States, Britain’s Prime Minister Michael Gove, and Brazil’s President Jair Bolsonaro’s son Edward Bolsonaro for its handling of the pandemic, which began in China’s Hubei province.
A number of directors of the Chinese Communist Party (CBC) have been dismissed across several sectors for dealing with quarantine efforts in central China, in a sign of resentment over the political establishment’s response to the outbreak in those areas.
Some commentators believe the move was aimed at protecting Communist Party of Chinese Communist Party General Secretary Xi Jinping from public discontent over the coronavirus outbreak.
Some Chinese officials, for example Zhao Lijian, have previously rejected recognition that the coronavirus outbreak began in Wuhan, in favor of conspiracy theories claimed by the United States or Italy regarding COVID-19.
The U.S. Donald Trump administration described the coronavirus as a "Chinese virus," or the Wuhan virus, saying that China has "imposed an obscure that has exacerbated the virus, which has now become a global pandemic," which has been criticized by some critics for being racist and "distracting [attention] from his administration's failure to contain the disease."
The Daily Beast obtained a U.S. government cable reviewing the communications blueprint for clear sources at the National Security Council, stating the strategy that “it’s all about China.
We have been asked to pursue and disseminate these messages in any way possible, including press conferences and television appearances. "News sources such as Politico, Foreign Policy, and Bloomberg have claimed that China's efforts to send aid to countries affected by the virus are part of a propaganda drive for global impact.
EU foreign policy chief Josep Borrell has warned of a “geopolitical component that includes a struggle for influence through the practice of circumventing and policies of generosity.”
“Chinese is aggressively pushing the message that, unlike the United States, it is a responsible and reliable partner,” Borrell also said.
China has also called on the United States to lift sanctions on Syria, Venezuela and Iran, while reportedly sending aid to the latter two countries.
U.S. Sanctions banned Jack Ma donated 100,000 masks to Cuba on April 3.
U.S. authorities have also been accused of diverting aid to other countries to their countries.
Masks-related disputes have been reported among other countries, such as Germany, Austria, Switzerland, the Czech Republic and Italy.
In addition, Turkey has seized hundreds of respirators intended for Spain.
In early March, the Italian government criticised the EU’s lack of solidarity with coronavirus-affected Italy.
Italy’s ambassador to the European Union, Maurizzo Masari, said: “It is only China that has responded bilaterally.
Certainly, this is not a good sign of European solidarity.”
On 22 March, after a phone call with Italian Prime Minister Giuseppe Conté, Russian President Vladimir Putin prepared the Russian military to send military paramedics, special disinfection vehicles, and other medical equipment to Italy.
Italy’s La Stampa newspaper quoted an unnamed “high-ranking political source” as saying that 80 percent of Russia’s aid was “insignificant or useless to Italy.”
The source accused Russia of initiating a remarkable “geographical and diplomatic political” attack.
Lombardi’s president, Atelio Fontana, and Italian Foreign Minister Luigi Di Maio denied press reports and expressed their gratitude.
Russia has also sent a cargo plane carrying medical aid to the United States.
Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said that “when providing assistance to his American colleagues, [Putin] assumes that when U.S. manufacturers of medical equipment and materials are active again, they will also be able to give back if necessary.”
NATO’s 2020 cannons exercises will take place in Germany, Poland and the Baltic states, NATO’s largest military maneuver since the end of the Cold War on a small scale.
The Secretary-General of the Nuclear Disarmament Campaign Kate Hudson criticised the Cannons Training 2020, saying: "In light of the current public health crisis, the training not only endangers the lives of forces from the United States and many participating European countries, but also the population of the countries in which it will take place." The Iranian government has been severely affected by the virus, with about twenty members of parliament as well as fifteen other former or current political figures.
Iranian President Hassan Rouhani wrote a public letter to world leaders on March 14, 2020 asking for help, saying that his country was struggling to fight an outbreak due to lack of access to international markets as a result of U.S. sanctions against Iran. The outbreak sparked calls for the United States to adopt common social policies in other rich countries, including universal health care, comprehensive childcare, paid family leave, and higher levels of public health funding.
Political analysts predicted that this may negatively affect Donald Trump’s chances of being re-elected in the 2020 presidential election. Diplomatic relations between Japan and South Korea have deteriorated due to the pandemic.
South Korea has criticised Japan's "ambiguous and negative quarantine efforts" after Japan announced that anyone from South Korea would be quarantined at certain government sites for two weeks.
South Korean society was initially polarized by President Moon Jae-in’s response to the crisis.
Many Koreans have signed petitions either calling for Moon's dismissal for allegedly mishandling the outbreak or praising its response. The pandemic has allowed countries to pass emergency-related legislation under the response.
Some commentators have expressed concerns that this could allow governments to tighten their control over power.
In Hungary, parliament voted to allow Prime Minister Viktor Orbán to rule indefinitely, suspend parliament as well as elections and punish those who spread false information about the virus and the government’s handling of the crisis.
The coronavirus outbreak has been blamed, which has led to numerous supply shortages due to the increasing global use of equipment to combat outbreaks, panic purchases, and the disruption of factory operation and logistics operations.
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration has issued warnings about shortages of medicines and medical equipment due to increased consumer demand and the shutdown of suppliers.
Many areas have also seen panic purchases, leaving shelves free of basic groceries such as food, toilet paper, and water bottles, causing shortages of supplies.
The tech industry in particular has warned of delays in shipments of electronic goods.
According to WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom, the demand for personal protective equipment has doubled by 100 times.
This demand sent prices up to twenty-twenty times the normal price and also delayed medical supplies by four to six months.
It has also caused a shortage of personal protective equipment around the world, with the World Health Organization warning that this will put health workers at risk.
In Australia, the pandemic has created a new opportunity for Diego shoppers to sell Australian products in China.
Activism led to a shortage of infant's milk in some supermarkets and was later banned by the Australian government. Despite the widespread incidence of COVID-19 in northern Italy and the Wuhan region, and the subsequent rising demand for food products, the two regions survived severe food shortages.
Measures taken by China and Italy to combat the illegal storage and trafficking of vital products have managed to avert the severe food shortage expected in Europe as well as in North America.
Italy’s large agricultural output has not seen significant declines, but prices may rise according to the expectations of representatives in the industry.
Food shelves were temporarily empty, even in the city of Wuhan, while Chinese government officials released pork stocks to ensure adequate nutrition for residents.
There are similar laws in Italy that require food producers to keep stockpiles of such emergencies.
According to a media report on March 16, the Chinese economy was severely affected in the first two months of 2020 by measures taken by the government to limit the spread of the virus, and retail sales fell by 20.5%.
Since mainland China is a major economic and industrial center, it has been noted that the outbreak of the virus poses a significant destabilizing threat to the stability of the global economy.
Agatha Dimaris of the Economist Intelligence Unit predicted that the markets would remain volatile until a clearer picture of the possible results emerged.
In January 2020, some analysts estimated that the economic impact of the pandemic on global growth could outweigh those that occurred as a result of the SARS outbreak between 2002 and 2004.
An expert at Washington University in St. Louis estimated that the global supply chain has been impacted by more than $300 billion, which could last up to two years.
The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) has reportedly experienced a “disruption” after a sharp drop in oil prices due to lower demand from China.
Global stock markets fell on February 24 due to a surge in the number of COVID-19 cases outside mainland China.
On February 27, due to growing concerns about the coronavirus outbreak, several U.S. stock indices, including the Nasdaq-100, the Standard & Poor's 500, and the Dow Jones Industrial Average recorded its biggest decline since 2008, with the Dow down 1,191 points, its biggest one-day decline since the 2007–08 financial crisis.
The three indexes ended the week down more than 10%.
On 28 February, Scoop Rattings confirmed China’s sovereign credit rating, but maintained the negative outlook.
Shares fell again due to fears of the coronavirus, and the biggest decline was on March 16.
Many people believe that an economic recession is possible.
Economist Mohamed El-Erian praised the emergency measures taken by central banks and countries in a timely manner.
Central banks are responding faster than they were in the middle of the 2008 financial meltdown.
Tourism is one of the sectors hardest hit by travel bans, closures of public places including tourist attractions, and warning governments not to travel around the world.
As a result, several airlines canceled their flights due to reduced demand, including British Airways, China East Airlines and Kants, while British regional airline, Flybe, collapsed.
The impact on the cruise line industry has been at an unprecedented level.
Many train stations and ferry ports have also been closed.
The pandemic has coincided with the Spring Festival travel season known as Chunyun, the main travel season associated with the Chinese New Year holiday.
National and regional governments have canceled a number of events involving large crowds, including New Year's Eve celebrations, with private companies also independently closing their stores and attractions such as Hong Kong's Disneyland and Shanghai Disneyland.
Many Lunar New Year events and tourist attractions have been closed to preventing large gatherings, including Beijing’s Forbidden City and traditional temple fairs.
In 24 of the 31 provinces, and in municipalities and districts in China, authorities extended the New Year's holiday until February 10, instructing most workplaces not to reopen until that date.
These regions account for 80% of the country’s GDP and 90% of exports.
Hong Kong has raised the level of response to infectious diseases to the highest level and declared a state of emergency, closing schools until March and canceling New Year's celebrations.
Visits to retailers in Europe and Latin America decreased by 40%.
Retailers in North America and the Middle East saw a decline of between 50 and 60 percent.
It also led to a decrease of 33 to 43 percent in pedestrian traffic to shopping malls in March compared to February.
Shopping mall operators around the world have imposed additional measures, such as increasing hygiene, installing thermal scanners to check the temperature of shoppers, and eliminating events. According to estimates by the United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America (ECLAC), the recession caused by the pandemic can leave between 14 and 22 million people in extreme poverty in Latin America who would have experienced this situation without the pandemic.
In January and February 2020, during the peak of the epidemic in Wuhan, about 5 million people in China lost their jobs.
Many of China's about 300 million rural immigrant workers were trapped in their homes in internal counties, or in Hubei County. In March 2020, more than 10 million Americans lost their jobs and applied for government assistance.
The coronavirus outbreak could cost the loss of 47 million jobs in the United States, and the unemployment rate could reach 32%, according to estimates by the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis. The lockdown in India has left tens of millions of Indian immigrant workers (who get paid through daily wages) unemployed. A survey conducted by the Angus Reed Institute found that 44% of Canadian households suffered some kind of unemployment. Nearly 900,000 workers have lost their jobs in Spain since it was closed in mid-March 2020.
During the second half of March, 4 million French workers applied for temporary unemployment benefits and 1 million British workers applied for a comprehensive credit scheme. Nearly half a million companies in Germany sent their workers on government-subsidized short-lived action plans known as Kurzarbeit.
France and Britanny have embraced a German short-term labor compensation plan.
The performing arts and cultural heritage sectors were profoundly affected by the pandemic, affecting the operations of organizations as well as individuals—workers and independents—globally.
The institutions of the arts and culture sector have sought to support their (often government-funded mission) to provide access to the cultural heritage of the community, maintain the integrity of their employees and the public, and support artists as much as possible.
By March 2020, around the world to varying degrees, museums, libraries, exhibition venues, and other cultural institutions have been closed indefinitely with their exhibitions, events and performances cancelled or postponed.
In response, there have been intensive efforts to provide alternative services through digital platforms. Among the recent and accelerating repercussions of the disease have been the cancellation of religious services, major sporting events, and other social events, such as music festivals and concerts, technology conferences, and fashion shows.
The Vatican announced the cancellation of the celebrations of the Holy Week in Rome, which take place during the last week of Christian repentance in Lent.
Many dioceses have advised elderly Christians to stay at home instead of attending mass on Sundays, with some churches making church services available over the radio, live streaming or television, while others offering worship without leaving their cars.
With the closure of the Catholic diocese of Rome in Rome and the ecclesiastical chapel, and the evacuation of St. Peter's Square from Christian pilgrims, other religious bodies also abolished limited public services and gatherings in churches, mosques, synagogue synagogues, synagogues, synagogues, and gorduara.
Iran’s Ministry of Health announced the cancellation of Friday prayers in areas affected by the outbreak and then closed shrines, while Saudi Arabia banned the entry of foreign pilgrims as well as its residents to holy sites in Mecca and Medina.
This pandemic has caused the greatest imbalance in the world sports calendar since World War II.
Most major sporting events have been canceled or postponed, including the 2019-20 UEFA Champions League, the 2019–20 UEFA Premier League, the 2019–20 U.S. Professional Basketball League season, and the 2019–20 National Hockey League season.
The outbreak disrupted plans for the 2020 Summer Olympics, which were scheduled to begin at the end of July, and the International Olympic Committee announced on 24 March that the event would "reschedule beyond 2020 but no later than the summer of 2021". Gambling clubs and other venues around the world were closed and direct gambling tournaments were postponed or cancelled.
This has led many gamblers to switch to playing online, as many online gambling sites have reported significant increases in new subscription rates. The entertainment industry has also been affected, with various music groups commenting or canceling concert tours.
Many large theaters such as those on Broadway have suspended all shows as well.
Some artists have discovered ways to continue producing and sharing work online as an alternative to traditional live performance, such as live-stream parties or creating online “celebrations” for artists to perform, distribute and publish their work.
On the Internet, many Internet memes about the coronavirus have spread as many have turned to humor and entertainment in the shadow of uncertainty.
Since the outbreak of COVID-19, extreme xenophobia and their racism has been observed towards people of Chinese and East Asian descent, and against people from hotspots in Europe, the United States and other countries.
Incidents of fear, suspicion and hostility have been observed in many countries, particularly in Europe, East Asia, North America and the Asia-Pacific region.
Reports released in February (when the majority of cases were still confined in China) documented racist sentiments expressed by various groups around the world towards the Chinese that they deserved the virus or were receiving what was allegedly justified punishment.
Anti-Chinese sentiment has also seen a rise in some African countries.
Many residents of Wuhan and Hubei reported discrimination because of their territorial origin.
There has been support for the Chinese, both online and offline, and for those living in areas affected by the virus.
After outbreaks in new hotspots, people from Italy, Europe's first country to experience a severe outbreak of COVID-19, may be exposed to skepticism and xenophobics. Citizens in countries including Malaysia, New Zealand, Singapore, and South Korea initially signed pressure petitions to prevent the Chinese from entering their country in an effort to stop the disease.
In Japan, the hashtag ‘ChineseDontComeTocobab’ is #Twitter.
The Chinese, as well as other Asians in the United Kingdom and the United States, have reported increasing levels of racial violations, as well as attacks.
U.S. President Donald Trump has faced criticism over the reference to the coronavirus as a “Chinese virus,” a term that critics have deemed racist and anti-Chinese.
Protesters in Ukraine have attacked buses carrying Ukrainians and foreigners being evacuated from Wuhan to Novi-Sanzari.
Students coming from northeastern India, which shares a border with China, studying in major Indian cities, have been harassed by the coronavirus outbreak.
Dilippe Ghosh, head of West Bengal's Bharatiya Janata unit, stated that the Chinese had destroyed nature and "that's why God avenged them".
The Chinese Consulate in Kolkata later condemned the remarks, calling them "wrong". In China, the pandemic led to increased xenophobia and racism against non-Chinese residents, with foreigners described as "foreign garbage" and targeted for "get rid of them".
Many newspapers have removed subscribed pages for some or all of their coverage of the coronavirus.
Many scientific publishers have made available scientific papers related to the outbreak to open access.
Some scientists have chosen to quickly share their results on pre-print servers such as bioRxiv.
Emerging Infectious Diseases – Infectious Diseases of Emerging Pathogens, are often emerging in terms of the scope of their outbreak or mode of transmission.
Globalization and Sickness – Overview of Globalization and Disease Transmission
Epidemics and Pandemic List – List of death tolls due to infectious diseases
Smuggling of wild animals and zoonotic diseases – health risks associated with trade in exotic wildlife
A laboratory test for respiratory coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and associated SARS-CoV-2 virus includes methods for detecting the presence of the virus where they identify antibodies that are produced in response to the infection.
RT-PCR confirms the presence of viruses in the samples, where it detects coronavirus RNA.
This testing is accurate and designed solely to detect SARS-CoV-2 RNA.
It is used to confirm very recent or active infections.
Antibody detection (serology) is used to diagnose and monitor the population.
Antibodies tests show the number of people who developed the disease, including those whose symptoms were unreported or those who didn't.
The exact mortality rate of the disease and the level of collective immunity among the population can be determined depending on the results of this test.
Due to the limitations of tests, no country in March 2020 had reliable data on the spread of the virus among its population.
On March 23, no country examined more than 3% of its population, and there is a significant divergence in the number of tests carried out by different countries.
This discrepancy is also likely to significantly affect the mortality rates of reported cases, which in some countries is likely to be significantly overestimated.
Using the retractive polymerase reaction (RRT-PCR), the examination can be performed on respiratory samples obtained in various ways, including nasopharyngeal swab or phlegm sample.
Results are generally available within a few hours to 2 days.
RT-PCR, which is performed using throat swabs, is not relied on until the first week of the disease.
The virus may then disappear from the throat while continuing to multiply in the lungs.
For infected people who are screened in the second week, it is possible instead to take a sample of the deep airways through a suction catheter or the use of cough (phlegm).
An early BCR test was created at the Charité in Berlin in January 2020 using the reversible polymerase retracement retracement (RRT-BCR), and formed the basis for 250,000 test kits distributed by the World Health Organization (WHO).
The United Kingdom also developed a test on January 23, 2020. The South Korean company Kogenebiotech also created PowerChek Coronavirus Detection Kit for SARS-CoV-2, which is suitable for safe clinical use and relies on BCR on January 28, 2020.
In China, BGI Group was one of the first companies to obtain emergency use approval from the Chinese National Medical Products Administration for SARS-CoV-2 Detecting kit based on PCR. In the United States, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) distributes a diagnostic panel for the novel coronavirus-2019 coronavirus (the novel coronavirus-2019), which relies on real-time RT-PCR to Public Health Laboratories through the International Determination Provider.
One of the three genetic tests in older versions of the testers resulted in inconclusive results due to defective detectors, and one stage of the test was lagging behind another at CCDC in Atlanta; this resulted in an average of less than 100 samples per day being successfully completed throughout February 2020.
Testions using two components did not have confidence until February 28, 2020, and until then state and local laboratories were not allowed to start testing.
The Food and Drug Administration has approved the testing under emergency use authorization. Commercial laboratories in the United States began testing in early March 2020.
On March 5, 2020, Lapcorp announced the availability of COVID-19 testing across the country based on RT-PCR.
Similarly, Quést Diagnostics has made COVID-19 testing available across the country as of March 9, 2020.
No quantitative restrictions have been declared; sampling and processing should be carried out in accordance with CDC requirements.
In Russia, the State Center for Virology and Biotechnology Research Viktor devised and produced COVID-19 testing.
On February 11, 2020, the Federal Health Care Surveillance Service recorded the test. On March 12, 2020, it was reported that the Mayo Clinic had developed a test to detect COVID-19 infections. On March 13, 2020, Roche Diagnostics obtained approval by the Food and Drug Administration for a test that could be conducted within 3.5 hours on large numbers, allowing a single machine to conduct approximately 4,128 tests over a 24-hour period.
On March 19, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) granted emergency use authorization (EUA) to Abbott Laboratories to conduct testing on Abbotte's m2000 system; the Food and Drug Administration had previously granted permission similar to Hology, Lab Corp, and Thermo Fisher-Scientific.
On March 21, 2020, Sifeed obtained a similar emergency use authorization from the Food and Drug Administration in favor of a test that takes about 45 minutes.
The Food and Drug Administration has approved a test that uses the technique of amplifiing isothermal DNA inflated instead of PCR.
Because this doesn’t require a series of successive temperature cycles, this method can show positive results in less than five minutes and negative results in 13 minutes.
There are currently about 18,000 of these machines in the United States and Abbott expects to increase manufacturing to conduct 50,000 tests per day. Taiwan is also developing a test that uses a monoclonal antibody that specifically binds to the nuclear capspy protein (N-protein) of the novel coronavirus, in the hope that it can achieve results within 15 to 20 minutes such as a rapid influenza test.
A review of studies in March 2020 concluded that "the chest X-rays have little diagnostic value in the early stages, while CT [computed tomography] results may be visible even before the onset of symptoms."
The usual characteristics that appear in computated tomography include two-sided multi-lobed fronted glass cataracts with surround, asymmetrical and rear distribution.
Spreading underpleura develops irregular patches and intensifies as the disease develops.
A study in Wuhan that compares PCR with CT about the origins of the current pandemic suggests that computed tomography is more sensitive than BCR, although it’s less accurate, with many of its imaging properties interfering with pneumonia and other pathological stages.
In March 2020, the American College of Radiation recommends "not to use CT scans to detect COVID-19 or consider it a preliminary examination to diagnose the disease." In March 2020, CDC recommends using PCR for an initial examination.
Part of the infection's immune response involves the production of antibodies, including IgM and IgG.
It can be used to detect infection of individuals within a period starting from 7 days or after the appearance of symptoms, to determine the ability of immunity, and in the case of population monitoring.
High-performing automated systems in many clinical laboratories will be able to perform these analyzes, but their availability will depend on the production rate of each system.
For central laboratories, a single sample of peripheral blood cells is usually used, although sequential speciments can be used to monitor the immune response.
Foring a point-of-clinical care test, a single blood sample is usually drawn through a skin puncture.
Contrary to PCR methods, it is not necessary to carry out a pre-examination step. On March 26, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) identified 29 entities that have provided the agency’s requirement notification and are able to distribute their antibody tests.
On April 7, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) adopted only one test under emergency use authorization. In late March 2020, Euromun Medical Laboratory Diagnostics and Epitope Diagnostics received European accreditations for their testing kits, which can detect IgG and IgA antibodies in blood samples.
The test produces several hundred samples within hours and is therefore much faster than the traditional PCR analysis of viral RNA.
Antibodies can often be detected 14 days after the onset of infection. In early April, the United Kingdom discovered that the antibodies test kits they had purchased were not good enough to use.
Hong Kong has devised a plan under which suspected patients can stay at home, ‘giving the emergency department a sample tube to patients’, spitting into the tube, and bringing it back back to get the test result shortly after. The NHS has announced that it is experimenting with a plan to test suspected cases at home, thus excluding the risk of transmission from infected people when they come to the hospital or the need to sterilize the ambulance used. When conducting a COVID-19 test for suspected cases without leaving the vehicle, the healthcare professional withdraws the sample taking appropriate precautions.
In-vehicle testing centers have enabled South Korea to conduct some of the fastest and most comprehensive tests in the world. In Germany, the National Association of Physicians of Legal Health Insurance said on 2 March that it was able to conduct about 12,000 outpatient tests per day and 10,700 people underwent testing last week.
The health insurance bears the costs if a doctor orders the test.
According to the head of the Robert Koch Institute, Germany has a total capacity of about 160,000 tests per week.
On March 19, in-car virus tests were made available in several major cities.
On March 26, 2020, the total number of tests conducted in Germany was unknown, as only the results of positive cases were announced.
A preliminary laboratory study revealed that starting from calendar week 12/2020, at least 483,295 samples had been tested until the end of 12/2020, and 33,491 samples (6.9%) tested positive for SARS-CoV-2. In Israel, researchers at Technion and Rumbam Hospital devised a method to examine samples taken from 64 patients at the same time and tested, by collecting samples and conducting further tests only when the sample is positive. In Wuhan, BGI opened a 2,000-square-meter emergency screening laboratory called “Huo-Yan” (火眼 or “Fire Eye” in English) on February 5, 2020, which can examine more than 10,000 samples per day.
With Wang Jian, the founder of BGI, overseeing the construction work and taking five days to do so, modeling operations have shown that cases in Hubei would have increased by 47% and related quarantine costs would have doubled if that testing capability had not been completed.
The Wuhan laboratory immediately followed the establishment of Huo-Yan laboratories in Shenzhen, Tianjin, Beijing, Shanghai and in 12 cities in total throughout China.
By March 4, 2020, total daily productivity was 50,000 tests per day. Origami Assays had released open-source, multi-transmitter designs that could test up to 1122 patient samples to detect COVID-19 using only 93 tests. These balanced designs can be used in small laboratories without the need for automated fluid processors.
In March, the lack and insufficiency of detectors caused a dilemma when a large number of tests were conducted in the European Union, the United Kingdom and the United States.
This prompted some specialists to explore sampling protocols that include heating samples at 98 °C (208 °F) for 5 minutes to release the RNA genome for further testing. On March 31, it was announced that the UAE was at the top of the world in terms of coronavirus testing of its population, and was on track to raise the level of testing to reach most of the population.
It was a combination of activating in-vehicle testing capability, and the purchase of a high-productivity laboratory that suits residents from Group 42 and BGI (depending on their Huo-Yan emergency detection laboratories in China).
The lab was set up within 14 days, allowing tens of thousands of RT-PCR tests to be conducted daily and is the world’s first laboratory of this size to operate outside of China.
Different testing methods have been devised that target different parts of coronavirus genetic traits in China, France, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan and the United States.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has adopted the German plan to manufacture kits sent to low-income countries that do not have the resources to innovate their own kits.
The German plan was announced on January 17, 2020; the protocol developed by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention wasn't available until January 28, delaying tests available in the United States.
In contrast, experts stated that the abundance of tests in South Korea has helped reduce the spread of the emerging coronavirus.
The South Korean government has greatly boosted testing capacity in private sector laboratories over several years.
On 16 March, the World Health Organization (WHO) called for an intensification of testing programs as the best solution to limit the progress of the COVID-19 pandemic.The increased demand for testing as a result of the widespread spread of the virus has caused hundreds of thousands of tests to be accumulated in private U.S. laboratories, and supplies of swabs and chemical detectors have also squeezed.
In March 2020, China reported problems with the accuracy of its test kits.
In the United States, ‘defects’ were found ‘defections’ in the test kits created by CDC; the government then removed bureaucratic barriers that prevented testing in particular. Spain purchased test kits from the Chinese company Shenzhen Bioeasy Biotechnology Ltd, but found that the results were inaccurate.
The company explained that the incorrect results may be due to the failure to collect the samples or not to use the kits properly.
The Spanish ministry stated that it would undo the purchase of kits that gave incorrect results, and would replace them with other test kits from Shenzhen Bioeasy. 80% of the test kits purchased by the Czech Republic from China gave false results. Slovakia bought 1.2 million test kits from China that turned out to be inaccurate.
Prime Minister Matović has proposed dumping it into the Danube River. Professor Ç Kara of the Turkish Ministry of Health reported that test kits purchased by Turkey from China have a "high error rate" and have "prevented their use". The United Kingdom also purchased from China 3.5 million test kits but in early April 2020 announced that they were unusable.
The tests, followed by quarantining those who tested positive and tracking those who came into contact with SARS-CoV-2, yielded positive results.
Researchers working in the Italian city of Vapu, which witnessed the first death from COVID-19 in Italy, conducted tests on the entire population of about 3,400 people twice, which took about ten days at a time.
Nearly half of the people who tested positive did not have symptoms, and all the detected cases were isolated.
As a result of the ban on movement between towns, the new infections were completely eliminated.
Singapore’s Coronavirus Pandemic 2020 has seened a significant decline in prevalence compared to other developed countries, but without severe restrictions such as forcibly closing restaurants and retail establishments through strict contact tracing, restrictions on internal travel, testing and quarantine.
Singapore has already started urging residents to stay indoors on March 28, but schools reopened on March 23 after the holiday.
Several other countries have also taken control of the pandemic by strictly tracking contacts and imposing restrictions on domestic travel, testing and quarantining, but with less stringent shut-down decisions, such as in Iceland and South Korea.
A statistical study has revealed that countries that have conducted more tests, compared to the number of deaths, have much lower mortality rates, perhaps because these countries are better able to detect those who suffer only mild symptoms or do not have any symptoms.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that countries that do not have the ability to conduct tests or have local laboratories with limited experience on COVID-19 send the first five positive samples and the first twent negative samples of COVID-19 to one of the 16 WHO-accredited laboratories to confirm the testing.
Of the 16 accredited laboratories, 7 are in Asia, 5 are in Europe, 2 are in Africa, 1 in North America, and 1 are in Australia.
In the following diagram, the ��‘positive percentage of tests’ column is influenced by the country’s policy of conducting testing.
The country that conducts the tests for people transferred to the hospital only will have more positive cases than the country that conducts the test for all its residents, whether they show symptoms or not, considering the equality of other factors.
Hand washing (or hand washing), also known as hand hygiene, is the process of cleaning hands for the purpose of removing dirt, grease, microorganisms, or other unwanted substances.
Continuously washing with soap at certain ‘necessary times’ during the day prevents the spread of many diseases, such as diarrhea and cholera, which are transmitted through the oral anal road.
People can also develop respiratory diseases such as influenza or a cold, for example, if they don’t wash their hands before touching their eyes, noses or mouths (i.e. mucous membranes).
The five necessary moments during the day when washing hands with soap is important: before and after defecating, after cleaning the baby's butt or changing the diapers, before feeding the baby, before eating, before preparing food or dealing with meat, fish or raw poultry.
If water and soap are not available, your hands can be cleaned with ash. The World Health Organization recommends washing your hands:
Before preparing the food, during it, and after.
Before caring for a sick person and after him.
After changing the diapers of a baby or cleaning it after using it for the toilet.
After brushing your nose, coughing or sneezing.
Having touched the animals, their fodder, or wasted.
The medical hygiene of the hands refers to the hygiene practices associated with medical procedures.
Washing hands before administering medications or providing medical care can prevent or limit the spread of the disease.
The main medical purpose of handwashing is to disinfect the hands of pathogens (bacteria, viruses, or other microorganisms that can cause disease) and chemicals that can cause damage or diseases.
This is especially important for people dealing with food or working in the medical field, but it’s also an important procedure for the general public.
Hand washing is characterized by many health benefits, including reducing the spread of influenza, coronavirus, and other infectious diseases, preventing infectious diseases that cause diarrhea, and reducing cases of respiratory infections.
Reducing infant mortality rates at birth at home.
A 2013 study showed that improved hand-washing practices may lead to slight improvements in height growth in children under the age of five.
In developing countries, child mortality rates related to respiratory and diarrhea diseases can be reduced by introducing simple behavioral changes, such as washing hands with soap.
This simple procedure can reduce the mortality rate from these diseases by almost 50%.
Measures that encourage handwashing from diarrhoeal seizures can be reduced by almost a third, which is similar to providing clean water in low-income areas.
48% of reduced diarrhoeal seizures can be associated with handwashing with soap. Handwashing with soap is the single most effective and least expensive way to prevent diarrhea and acute respiratory infections (ARE) when applying spontaneous behavior in homes, schools, and communities around the world.
Pneumonia, one of the main acute respiratory infections, is the leading cause of death among children under the age of five, killing about 1.8 million children annually.
Diarrhea and pneumonia together cause the death of about 3.5 million children annually.
According to UNICEF, converting hand-washing with soap before eating and after using the toilet into an inherent habit can save more lives than any vaccine or medical intervention, reducing deaths from diarrhea by almost half, and deaths from acute respiratory infections by a quarter.
Hand washing is usually combined with other hygiene measures as part of water, sanitation and hygiene (water, sanitation and hygiene).
Handwashing also protects against herpes transmitted through direct physical contact.
Handwashing has a minor detrimental effect is that frequent hand washing can lead to damage to the skin due to its dryness.
A Danish study in 2012 revealed that over-washing hands can lead to a skin condition of peeling and itching known as hand eczema or hand skin inflammation, which is especially common among health care workers.
Hand-washing is also often seen too much as a symptom of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD).
There are five necessary moments during the day when washing hands with soap is important to reduce the transmission of diseases through the oral anal route: after using the bathroom (urinating, defecating), after cleaning the child's butt (changing diapers), before feeding the baby, before eating, and before/after preparing food or dealing with raw meat, fish or poultry.
The correct handwashing method should be practiced in other cases in order to prevent the transmission of the disease, including before or after the treatment of cutting or wound, after sneezing, coughing or cleaning your nose, and after touching or handling animal droppings, and after touching the garbage.
In many countries, the rate of washing hands with soap is low.
A study on handwashing in 54 countries in 2015 found that on average 38.7% of households wash their hands with soap. A 2014 study showed that Saudi Arabia has the highest rate of 97 percent, the U.S. approaching the middle at 77 percent, and in China with the lowest at 23 percent. There are now several behavior change methodologies to make more use of hand-washing behavior with soap at the necessary times.
The “Basic Health Care Program” implemented by the Philippine Department of Education is an example of taking widespread action to promote children’s health and education.
Treatment of worms twice a year, in addition to washing hands daily with soap, and cleaning teeth daily with fluoride, is at the heart of this national program.
It has also been successfully implemented in Indonesia.
Adding soap or detergents to water promotes the removal of microorganisms from the skin.
The main effect of soap and detergents is to reduce impediments of decay, increasing solubility.
Water alone disinfectorizer is ineffective for the skin because fats and proteins, which are the components of organic soil, do not decompose easily in water.
However, the flow of the right amount of water helps disinfection.
Hard soap, due to its reusable nature, may carry bacteria acquired from previous uses.
A few studies that have observed bacterial transmission of contaminated solid soap have concluded that transmission is unlikely because the bacteria are washed with foam.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention still states that “liquid soap is preferred from touchless devices to pump soap.”
Antibacterial soap has been heavily promoted to health-conscious consumers.
To date, there is no evidence that the use of recommended disinfectants or sterilizers is chosen to eliminate antibiotic-resistant organisms in nature.
However, antibacterial soaps contain common antibacterial agents such as triclosan, which does not affect a large number of strains of living organisms.
Therefore, if antibacterial soap doesn’t affect antibiotic-resistant strains, it may not be as effective as it does.
In addition to the superficial stress reliever and skin protection factor, sophisticated formulations may contain acids (acetic acid, ascorbic acid, lactic acid) as an acid organizer, antimicrobial active benzoic acid and other skin moisturizers (aloe vera, vitamins, menthol, plant extracts).A comprehensive analysis by the University of Oregon's School of Public Hygiene indicated that ordinary soap is as effective as consumer-consumers-containing antibacterial soap that contains trichlosan in preventing diseases and removing bacteria from the hands.
Hot water suitable for washing your hands isn’t hot enough to kill bacteria.
Bacteria grow much faster at body temperature (37 °C).
However, warm water and soap are more effective than cold water and soap in removing natural oils that retain dust and bacteria.
Contrary to popular belief, scientific studies have shown that the use of warm water has no effect in reducing the microbial load on the hands.
A disinfectant or hand sanitizer is a material that is not waterproof for cleaning hands.
In the late 1990s and the early 21st century, non-water-based alcohol-based hand detergents that are sterilized by scrub (also known as an alcohol-fixed hand lotion, disinfecting hand lotion, or hand sanitizers) began to gain popularity.
Most of them rely on isopropyl alcohol or ethanol combined with an incrementing agent such as carbomire (acrylic acid polymer) in a gel, a moisturizer such as glycerin in a liquid, or foaming for ease of usage and reducing the dehydration effect caused by alcohol.
Adding diluted hydrogen peroxide increases antimicrobial activity. Hand sanitizers containing at least 60 to 95% of alcohol are effectively bactericidal.
Alcoholic sterilizers by rubbing eliminate bacteria, multi-drug-resistant bacteria (staphylococcus aureus are resistant to methicillin and vancomycin-resistant intestinals), tuberculosis, and certain viruses (including HIV, herpes, respiratory syncytial virus, nasal viruses, hoarsis, influenza, hepatitis), and fungi.
Alcoholic sterilizers that contain 70% alcohol by scrubbing spend 99.97% (a logarithmic decrease of 3.5, similar to a decrease of decibel by 35) of bacteria on the hands 30 seconds later and 99.99% to 99.999% (a logarithmic reduction of between 4 and 5) of the bacteria on the hands 1 minute after application. Hand sanitizers are more effective against bacteria and less effective against certain viruses.
Alcoholic hand sanitizers are almost completely ineffective against norovirus (or norwallk), which is the most common cause of gastroenteritis caused by the infection. Enough of a manual disinfectant or rubbing alcohol should be used to wet or completely cover both hands.
Rub the front and back of the hands and between all fingers and limbs for about 30 seconds until the liquid, foam, or gel dries.
Your fingertips should also be washed thoroughly, rubbing them in both comforts. The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends hand washing by rubbing the hand sanitizer, especially when the hands are clearly dirty.
The increased use of these detergents is due to their ease of use and rapid activity in eliminating microorganisms; however, they should not be a substitute for proper hand washing unless water and soap are not available.
Frequent use of alcoholic hand sanitizers can cause the skin to dry out unless emollients, skin moisturizers, or both are added to the formula.
The effect of alcohol in dry skin can be reduced or eliminated by adding glycerin, other emollients, or both to the composition.
Clinical trials have proven that alcoholic hand sanitizers that contain emollients cause less irritation and dehydration of the skin than antimicrobial soap or detergents.
Allergic contact dermatitis, contact urticaria syndrome, hypersensitivity to alcohol, or additives found in alcoholic handwaships are rare.
Decreased susceptibility to the incidence of irritating contact dermatitis has become an attraction compared to washing hands with soap and water.
Despite the efficacy of detergents that are not water-based, they don’t disinfect the hands of organic matter, but they simply sterilize them.
That’s why hand sanitizers aren’t as effective as soap and water in preventing the spread of many pathogens, as pathogens remain on the hands.
The efficacy of an alcohol-free hand sanitizer depends heavily on the ingredients and composition, and historically its effect has been noticeably lower than the alcohol-containing that relies on rubbing alcohol.
Recently, it has been proven that formulations using benzalconium chloride have a cumulative and continuous effect as an antimicrobials after their use, unlike alcohol, which has been shown to have decreased effectiveness after repeated use, possibly due to gradual inverse skin reactions.
Many individuals in low-income communities cannot afford to buy soap and use ashes or dirt instead.
Ash or dirt may be more effective than water alone, but it may be less effective than soap.
One concern is that if dirt or ash is contaminated with microorganisms, they may increase the spread of the disease rather than reduce it.
Ashes, such as soap, are also disinfectants because they form an alkaline solution when they come into contact with water.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has recommended using ash or sand as an alternative to soap when unavailable.
The correct handwashing method recommended by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control (CDC) for the prevention of disease transmission includes the following steps:
Wet the hands with warm or cold running water.
It is recommended to use running water because the existing basins may be polluted, and at the same time the water temperature does not seem to make a difference.
Make foam on the hands by rubbing them with plenty of soap, including the back of the hands, between the fingers and under the nails.
Soap removes germs from the skin, and studies show that people tend to wash their hands thoroughly when using soap instead of just water.
Scrub for at least 20 seconds.
Scrubating creates friction, which helps remove germs from the skin, and rubbing for longer periods removes more germs.
Rinse thoroughly under running water.
Rinsing the hands inside the pelvis may re-contaminate them.
Dry with a clean towel or let it dry in the air.
Wet and wet hands are easily contaminated again. The most common parts that are overlooked are the thumb, wrist, areas between the fingers, and under the nails.
Synthesic nails and cracked nail polish may contain microorganisms.
Moisturizing lotion is often recommended to prevent dry hands, as dryness of the skin can damage it, which may increase the risk of transmission.
Various low-cost options can be provided to facilitate handwashing in developing countries, where tap water, soap, or both are not available, such as pouring water from a hanging container or knocking with suitable holes and/or using ashes if necessary. In places with limited water supplies (such as schools or rural areas in developing countries), there are solutions to rationalize water consumption, such as Tap Tepe and other low-cost options.
Teppe Faucet is a simple technique that uses a jug attached to a rope, a foot-powered controller to pour a small amount of water over the hands and a piece of soap.
Effective drying of hands is an essential part of hand hygiene, but there is some debate about the most effective form of drying in public toilets.
A growing number of research suggests that paper towels are healthier than electric hand dryers found in many toilets.
In 2008, the University of Westminster in London conducted a study sponsored by the European Textile Symposium on the manufacture of paper towels to compare the levels of hygiene provided by paper towels, warm air hand dryers, and newer jet air hand dryers.
Having washed and drying hands with a warm air dryer, the total number of bacteria was found to increase on average on the soles of the fingers by 194% and on the palm of the hand by 254%.
Drying using an air jet dryer has increased the average total number of bacteria on the soles of the fingers by 42% and on the palm of the hand by 15%.
Having washed and dried hands with a paper towel, on average, the total number of bacteria on the inside of the fingers decreased by up to 76% and on the palm of the hand by 77%, and scientists conducted tests to determine whether there is a possibility of transmission to other toilet users and toilet environment as a result of each type of drying method.
The jet air dryer, which pushes air out of the unit at speeds of 180 m/s (650 km/h; 400 mph), is able to inflate microorganisms from the hands and the device, and may infeculate other toilet users and the bathroom environment at a distance of up to 2 meters.
The use of a warm air dryer spreads microorganisms 0.25 meters away from the dryer.
Paper towels did not show any significant spread of microorganisms. In 2005, a study by TÜV Produkt und Umwelt evaluated different hand drying methods.
The following changes in the numbers of bacteria were observed after drying the hands:
There are many manufacturers of different hand dryers, and the hand dryers have been bottled with paper towels.
Washing hands with hand sterilization wipes is an alternative while traveling because soap and water are unavailiable.
An alcoholic hand sanitizer should contain at least 60% alcohol.
Medical handwashing has become mandatory for a long time after Hungarian physician Agnats Semelves discovered its effectiveness (in 1846) in the prevention of diseases in the hospital environment.
There are electronic devices that provide notes to remind hospital staff to wash their hands if they are forgotten.
One study found a reduction in infection rates using it.
Medical hand washing is for at least 15 seconds, using abundant amounts of soap, water or gel to make foam and rub each part of the hands.
The hands should be rubbed together and the fingers are tangled.
If there is excrement under the fingernails, you can use a brush with coarse hair to remove it.
Because germs may remain in the water on your hands, it’s important to rinse them thoroughly and dry them with a clean towel.
After drying, a paper towel should be used to close the water (and any exit door is opened if necessary).
This avoids contaminating the hands again from those surfaces.
The purpose of handwashing in the place of health care is the removal of pathogenic microorganisms ("bacteriums") and avoiding transmission.
According to the New England Journal of Medicine, not washing hands remains at unacceptable levels in most medical environments, with large numbers of doctors routinely forgetting washing their hands before touching patients, transporting microorganisms.
One study showed that proper handwashing and other simple procedures can reduce the rate of catheter-associated bloodstream infections by 66 percent, and the World Health Organization has published a newspaper outlining the method of handwashing and rubbing in the health care sectors.
The organization’s Hand Hygiene Guidance document project can also be found available for public comment.
Whitby and others conducted a related review.
Commercial devices can measure and verify hand hygiene, if necessary to prove compliance with regulations.
The World Health Organization has “five moments” for handwashing:
After exposure to blood/bodily fluids
Before the sterilization mission,
After caring for patients. Adding disinfectant chemicals to soap ("medical" or "antimicrobial") gives a pesticide element to the hand-washing agent.
This extermination element may be desirable prior to surgery or in places where antibiotic-resistant organisms are highly prevalent. When someone’s hands are “rubbed” for surgery, a faucet is required that can be turned on and off without touching it with the hands, some chlorhexidine or iodine lotions, sterilized towels for drying hands after washing, a sterile brush for scrubbing, and another sterilized tool for cleaning under the nails.
All the jewels have to be removed.
This procedure requires washing your hands and forearms up to the elbow, usually from 2 to 6 minutes.
The duration of a long scrub (10 minutes) is not necessary.
When rinsing, you should prevent the reflux of water from the forearms to the hands.
After washing your hands, dry the hands with a sterile cloth and wear a surgical gown.
To limit the spread of germs, it’s best to wash your hands or use a hand sanitizer before and after caring for a sick person.
To to control staphylococcus infection in hospitals, the greatest benefit of manual disinfection has been found to come from the first 20% of laundry, and it has gained very little additional benefit when the frequency of cleaning of the hands increased to more than 35%.
Washing with regular soap leads to more than three times the rate of bacterial infectious diseases transmitted to food compared to antibacterial soap washing. Comparing hand rubbing with an alcohol solution with hand washing with antibacterial soap for an average of 30 seconds, it was found that rubbing alcohol reduces bacterial contamination by 26% more than antibacterial soap.
However, water and soap are more effective than alcoholic handwashes to reduce H1N1 influenza virus type A and difficulent calcified spores. Measures to improve hand hygiene in health care settings may include educating employees about handwashing, increasing the availability of alcoholic handwash, and written and verbal reminders for employees.
More research is needed to find out which of these measures is most effective in different health care settings.
In developing countries, it is recognized that handwashing with soap is an essential tool at an affordable cost for good health, as well as good nutrition.
However, a lack of reliable water supplies, soap, or handwashing facilities in homes, schools, and workplaces makes it difficult to come up with generic handwashing behaviors.
For example, in most rural areas of Africa you rarely find hand-washing taps near every private or public toilet, although there are cheap options to build hand-washing stations.
However, low hand washing rates can be due to inherent habits rather than a lack of soap or water.
Promoting and advocating handwashing with soap can influence political decisions, raise awareness of the benefits of handwashing, and change the behavior of the population in the long term.
For this to work effectively, monitoring and evaluation is essential.
A systematic review of 70 studies found that community-based approaches are effective in increasing handwashing in lower-middle-income countries, while social marketing campaigns are less effective. One example of promoting handwashing in schools is UNICEF’s “three-star approach” designed to encourage schools to take simple and inexpensive steps to ensure that students wash their hands with soap, among other health requirements.
When minimum standards are achieved, schools can move from one star until they eventually reach three stars.
The construction of handwashing stations can be part of handwashing promotion campaigns conducted to reduce illness and child mortality.
World Handwashing Day is yet another example of awareness-raising campaigns that are trying to achieve behaviour change. Due to the 2019-20 coronavirus pandemic, UNICEF has encouraged the adoption of a hand-washing emoji.
Few studies have looked at the overall cost-effectiveness of handwashing in developing countries and its relationship to disability-adjusted life years.
However, one review indicates that enhancing handwashing with soap is significantly more cost-effective than other water and sanitation procedures.
The importance of handwashing for human health was recognized for the first time – especially for people in critical circumstances such as mothers who had just been born or wounded soldiers in hospitals – in the middle of the 19th century from two pioneers of hand hygiene: the Hungarian doctor Ignates Semelves, who worked in Vienna, Austria, and the Englishman Florence Nightingale, the “founder of modern nursing”.
Most people at the time still believed that the infection was caused by unpleasant odors called miyazmaz.
In the 1980s, food-borne outbreaks and health care-related infections prompted the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to promote more effective hand hygiene as an important way to prevent the spread of infection.
Swine flu outbreaks in 2009 and the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020 raised awareness in many countries of the importance of handwashing with soap to protect themselves from these infectious diseases.
For example, posters containing ‘correct handwashing methods’ have been hung next to hand-washining basins in public toilets and in the toilets of office and airport terminals in Germany.
‘Washing your hands from’ means a revelation of one’s unwillingness to take responsibility for something or to participate in complicity.
It originates from a paragraph of the Biblical in Matthew where Puntician Pilate washed his hands from the decision to crucify Jesus Christ, but it has become a phrase that has a much broader use in some English societies.
In Shakespeare’s Macbeth, Mrs. Macbeth begins by washing her hands excessively in an attempt to purify a fantasy spot that represents her sense of guilt for the crimes she has committed and the crimes she has induced her husband.
It has also been found that people, after pointing out or contemplating unethical acts, tend to wash their hands more than others, and tend to appreciate hand-washing tools more.
Moreover, those who are allowed to wash their hands after this study are less likely to participate in other “cleansing” compensatory actions, such as volunteering.
Religies describe hand washing for both healthy and symbolic purposes. Symbolic handwashing, using water without soap for handwashing, is part of hand washing rituals that have emerged in many religions, including Bahá’í faith, Hinduism, tévilla, hand washing in Judaism, ablution in Christianity and ablution in Islam.
Hinduism, Judaism and Islam require handwashing after using the toilet.
Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikhism, Judaism and Islam require washing hands before and after each meal.
Covid-19 risk control controls in the workplace
Controls on COVID-19 risk controls in the workplace are the application of occupational safety and health methodologies for risk control controls for the prevention of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).
Correct workplace risk control controls are based on the workplace and job task, based on an assessment of the risks of exposure, the risk of disease in the community, and the risk factors that threaten working individuals who may be exposed to COVID-19.
According to the U.S. Department of Occupational Safety and Health (OSHA), low-risk work involves minimal communication with the public and other co-workers, a situation in which basic infection prevention measures are recommended, including handwashing and encouraging workers to stay at home if they are sick, taking into account crippling and sneezing etiquette, and maintaining standard practices of cleaning and disinfecting the working environment.
Functions with moderate exposure risks include those that require repetitious or close contact with unknown individuals or suspected of having COVID-19, but may become infected due to ongoing transmission in the community or international travel.
This includes workers who have communicated with the general public, such as in schools, high labor-intensive working environments, and some large-sized retailers.
Risk controls for this group, in addition to basic infection prevention measures, include ventilation using high-efficiency air filters, sneezing prevention barriers, and the use of personal protective equipment available in the case of dealing with someone with COVID-19.
The Occupational Safety and Health Administration considers health-care workers and custodians who are exposed to a person known to be infected with COVID-19 or suspected to be exposed to high levels of risk of exposure, which increases the very high risk of exposure if workers carry out aerosol generation procedures, or the collection or handling of samples from a person known or suspected of COVID-19.
Appropriate risk control controls for these workers include engineering controls such as negative pressure ventilation chambers and personal protective equipment suitable for a function.
COVID-19 outbreaks can have multiple effects in the workplace.
Workers may be absent from work due to illness, the need to take care of others, or for fear of possible exposure.
Trade patterns may change, in terms of both the quality of the goods ordered, and the ways to get them (such as off-the-peak shopping or through delivery services or shopping without leaving the car).
Finally, shipments of goods from geographical areas severely affected by COVID-19 may be halted. An infectivoring disease preparedness and response plan can be used to guide preventive measures.
Plans address the risk levels associated with different workplaces and functional tasks, including sources of exposure, risk factors arising from the home and community settings, and risk factors for individual workers such as old age or chronic medical conditions.
It also determines the controls needed to address those risks, and contingency plans for situations that may arise as a result of an outbreak.
Preparedness and response plans for infectious diseases may be subject to national and subnational recommendations.
Epidemic response goals include reducing transmission between employees, protecting those most at risk of adverse health complications, maintaining business processes, and minimizing negative impacts on other entities in their supply chains.
The severity of illness in the society in which the business is located affects the responses taken.
Risk control hierarchy is a framework widely used in occupational safety and health to classify risk controls by effectiveness.
Because the risks of COVID-19 cannot be eliminated, the most effective controls are engineering controls, followed by administrative controls, and finally personal protective equipment.
Engineering controls involve isolating employees from work-related risks without relying on workers’ behavior, and can be the most cost-effective implementation solution.
Administrative controls are changes in labor policy or procedures that require action by the worker or employer.
Personal protective equipment (PPE) is less effective than engineering and administrative controls, but it can help prevent certain exposures.
All types of personal protective equipment must be selected based on the worker’s risk, the correct suitability as appropriate (e.g. respirators), wear continuously and correctly, regularly inspected, maintained and replaced, as necessary, removed, cleaned and stored, or disposed properly to avoid contamination.
According to the U.S. Department of Occupational Safety and Health (OSHA), jobs that involve lower risk exposure involve minimal communication with the public and other co-workers.
Basic infection prevention measures recommended for all workplaces include washing hands frequently and completely, encouraging workers to stay at home if they are sick, observing cough and sneezing etiquette including covering the mouth and nose when coughing and sneezing, providing paper towels and garbage containers, preparing to work remotely or organizing shifts successively if necessary, dissuading workers from using other people’s tools and equipment, and maintaining routine cleaning and disinfection of the work environment.
The immediate identification and isolation of individuals likely to be contagious is a crucial step in protecting workers, customers, visitors, and others in the workplace.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that employees with symptoms of severe respiratory illnesses remain at home until the fever, signs of fever, and any other symptoms disappear for at least 24 hours without the use of antifebrillators or other medications that improve symptoms, that sick leave policies be flexible, allow employees to stay at home to care for a sick family member, and that employees are familiar with these policies.
According to the Occupational Safety and Health Administration, jobs with an average risk of exposure include those that require repetitive or close communication within a distance of six feet (1.8 m) from individuals who are not known or suspected of having COVID-19, but may develop SARS-CoV-2 due to ongoing transmission in the community around the workplace, or due to a person's recensid international travel to a site where COVID-19 transmission is prevalent.
This includes workers who come into contact with the general public, such as in schools, high-intensity labor work environments, and some large-sized retail places. Engineering controls and high-risk groups include the installation of high-efficiency air filters, increased ventilation rates, the installation of physical barriers such as transparent plastic sneezing protectors, the installation of windows to serve customers who shop without leaving the car. Administrative controls for this and high-risk groups include encouraging sick workers to stay at home, replacing face-to-face meetings with virtual communications, establishing successively organized work shifts, stopping unnecessary travel to sites where there areasing cases of COVID-19, developing emergency communication plans, and providing labor-inclusitary and personalized labor-remandatory measures.
Employees of this risk group rarely need to use respirators.
If the person becomes sick while on a plane, appropriate protective controls for the protection of employees and other passengers include the removal of the sick passenger from others by a distance of 6 feet, the assignment of one member of the aircraft team to serve the sick person, and provide the sick person with a face mask or ask the sick person to cover his mouth and nose with paper napkins when coughing or sneezing.
The crew of the aircraft must wear disposable medical gloves when dealing with a sick traveler or touching contaminated body fluids or surfaces, and possibly using additional personal protective equipment if the sick traveler has fever, persistent cough or difficulty breathing.
Gloves and other disposable items should be disposed of in a biological hazard prevention bag, and contaminated surfaces must be cleaned and sterilized afterwards. For commercial shipping, including cruise ships and other passenger vessels, risk control controls include postponing travel upon illness, self-insulation and informing the medical center on board immediately if a person develops fever or other symptoms while on board.
Ideally, medical follow-up should take place in an isolated person's compartment. For schools and childcare facilities, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends a short-term closure of cleaning or disinfection if an infected person is present in the school building regardless of the spread in the community.
When there is a limit to transmission in the community that ranges from limited to average, social distancing strategies such as the cancellation of field trips, gatherings, and other large gatherings such as physical breeding classes, or band lessons or eating meals in the cafeteria, increasing the distances between offices, organizing successive attendance and departure times, reducing unnecessary visitors, and using a separate health office for children with symptoms similar to the symptoms of influenza.
When there is a significant transmission of infection in the local community, in addition to social distancing strategies, school extensions can be taken into account. For law enforcement officers who carry out daily routine activities, direct health risks are considered low on the part of the CDC.
Law enforcement officials who must communicate with individuals confirmed or suspected of having COVID-19 are advised to follow the same guidelines as emergency medical technicians, including appropriate personal protective equipment.
In the event of direct contact during arrest, employees should clean and disinfect work belts and tools before reusing them using a spray or household cleaning mop, and follow standard operating procedures to deal with pollution and dispose of personal protective equipment used and to collect and wash clothes.
The Department of Occupational Safety and Health considers that some health care workers and custodians fall into groups with high or very high levels of exposure.
Jobs with a high level of exposure include health-care providers, support, laboratories, and medical transportation who are exposed to patients known or suspected of having COVID-19.
These become a very high level of risk exposure if workers perform aerosol-generating procedures, as well as collecting or handling samples from confirmed or suspected COVID-19 patients.
Procedures for aerosynthesis include intubation, cough induction procedures, bronchoscopy, certain dental-related procedures and tests, or invasive sampling.
The functions of high-risk mortuary include workers who are involved in the preparation of the bodies of persons confirmed or suspected of having COVID-19 at the time of their death; these become extremely vulnerable if they perform an autopsy. Additional engineering controls of these endangered groups include isolation rooms for patients known or suspected of having COVID-19, including when implementing aerosoptic procedures.
Specialized negative pressure ventilation may be appropriate in some health care settings and conservion.
Specims must be treated by following level 3 biosafety precautions.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends isolating patients arriving in separate waiting areas based on whether or not they are suspected of having COVID-19. In addition to other personal protective equipment, the Department of Occupational Safety and Health recommends the use of respirators for those who work at a distance of 6 feet from patients known or suspected of having SARS-CoV-2, and those who perform aerosol generation procedures.
In the U.S., N95-approved N95 face mask respirators, certified by the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Hygiene, should be used in the context of a comprehensive written respiratory protection program, which includes synthesis and suitability testing, training, and medical examinations.
Other types of respirators can provide greater protection and improve worker comfort. The World Health Organization (WHO) does not recommend using aprons, because COVID-19 is a respiratory disease rather than being transmitted through body fluids.
The World Health Organization (WHO) only recommends using a surgical mask for screening staff at the point of entry.
For those who collect, care for, or transport respiratory samples from COVID-19 patients without any aerosol procedures, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommends the use of a surgical mask, goggles, face shields, swarbs and gloves.
If an aerosopic procedure is performed, the face mask is replaced with an N95 or FFP2 breathing mask.
Considering that the global supply of personal protective equipment is inadequate, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommends reducing the need for personal protective equipment through telemedicine, and physical barriers such as transparent windows, allowing only those involved in direct care to enter a room with a patient with COVID-19, using only personal protective equipment necessary for the specific task, continuing to use the breathing mask itself without removing it while caringing for many patients with the same diagnosis, monitoring and coordinating the supply chain of PPE, and dissuading the use of masks for asymptomatic individuals.
Catherine Maher, Executive Director of the Wikimedia Foundation
To: All employees of the Wikimedia Foundation
Topic Line: [Covid 19] Relieving the burden and preparing for the future
Date/time: March 14, 2020, 00:24 UTC
Licensing: CC0: No Rights Reserved
We found ourselves in the midst of exceptional circumstances this month.
The COVID-19 pandemic has clearly demonstrated the global interdependence of humanity and our responsibilities to others.
Our history has never seen similar challenges, but we know with certainty that the best response relies on a kind of global empathy, collaboration, and community-building that are entrenched elements at the heart of this institution.
The intense friendship and care we have seen among all of our colleagues via emails, calls and conversations is an amazing practical demonstration of the amazing human emotions that fortunately surround the way we do business.
I am very grateful and very proud to be a true colleague.
Last week, someone sent me a letter expressing their appreciation for our work.
They reminded me of how important the world’s ability to head for Wikipedia right now, and the powerful symbolism evidenced by the survival of this critical resource available online and accessible to all individuals.
Your efforts have made this possible, whether it’s through the continued operation of the sites, our colleagues getting paid, or providing safety for our communities.
The world is in dire need of the information available on Wikipedia than ever before.
It’s a moment where the positive impact in the world isn’t just about our efforts, but the way we do it.
Because of the importance of this task and your role in it, we will make significant adjustments in how we work together starting next week.
Modifications to mode of operation and timelines
As Rubén mentioned earlier, Team C met yesterday to discuss our method and schedule for the coming days and months.
In that discussion, we considered what we thought could be an appropriate response to what we’re facing, and the ideal way to sustain the organization’s business during this period.
We very enthusiastically wanted to get rid of stress and stress, and to support our long-term mission.
If you want to reduce burdens and tasks, that's okay.
For all employees, contractors and contract workers:
The expected daily working period is 4 hours per day, or 20 hours per week until further notice.
If you’re able to work longer than normal business hours, you may add your efforts to the task.
However, it’s currently unpredictable of the world, and whether you want to take care of your loved ones, get goods from groceries or go to the doctor, your safety is our top priority.
We don't track your times.
If you're sick, don't work.
It should be understood to allow this matter without expressing it, but we certainly mention it.
You don’t need to get sick or paid leave, just inform your manager and help your team review calendars and schedules to make sure key areas of work are covered.
(If your doctor diagnoses COVID-19 with a positive diagnosis, please tell Brian in the Department of Training and Certification processes so that the training and certification department can assist by providing support and ensuring that your condition gets the appropriate care from the administration.)
Fellows working per hour receive their full wages.
We’ve already mentioned that, and we renew this pledge of our commitments to our contractors and our fellow hourlies.
Each employee will get paid based on the usual working hours under normal circumstances.
This includes whether an employee is ill or unable to work.
If you want to work, we support you.
Many people use work to cope with the pervasive pressures in the world around us.
What we can do will be amazingly productive, especially during difficult times like this.
It’s about caring for oneself.
We only ask you to communicate with your manager so that he or she can find out what to expect and adjust the procedures to adapt accordingly.
Some business is necessary.
There are also some things that we have to keep going.
Site reliability engineering teams, HR processes, trust, honesty and fundraising (and others) are doing critical work, which may require more support.
We will begin a process in collaboration with all departments to assess existing goals and shift our focus to supporting the elements necessary to achieve our mission.
There’s a lot of work that we all have to do, and we’re just going to focus on the most important projects.
Going slower now won't hurt us later.
We don’t plan to “double working time to catch up” after the pandemic.
It’s unexpected that we’ll be working overtime to meet deadlines that have now become unrealistic.
We accept the fact that these circumstances have changed, and we will work to set new goals and timetables once this becomes appropriate.
What Happens in Annual Planning (APP)?
To cope with the new reality and the expectations of daily working hours, we intend to adjust the delivery schedule for our 2020-2021 annual plan.
We intend to propose an extension of our 2019-2020 plan to allow more time for budget design to allow employees to prioritize critical work, self-care and caring for loved ones who need or want to work within the next few weeks.
This timeline extension eases current planning workloads and pressure across the entire organization.
We will submit our proposal to the Board of Directors next week and will inform delegates and teams of updates on the next steps as soon as we receive confirmation.
We thank the annual planning team for their pioneering role in this matter.
Desk Case, Exposure and Cleaning
We learned last week that one of our colleagues working in San Francisco may have been infected with COVID-19.
However, out of extreme caution and prohibition, we hired a virus-resistant cleaning team to disinfect all surfaces in the San Francisco office.
The team has used a hospital-grade antiviral solution to disinfect all surfaces, as well as the hallway and elevator units that lead to our floor.
The building has implemented its care duty protocol using products that support tenants’ safety.
We are relieved that the office is conveniently prepared until the time we decide to return to work.
Our office in Washington, D.C., is located in a building belonging to the Wework Company, which shared with us its COVID-19 protocol and with all members of the staff at our headquarters in Washington, D.C.
Beginning last week, our office in Washington, D.C., has moved to a completely remote location under joint guidance with our office in San Francisco.
As some of our colleagues at our New York City headquarters know, we’ve had a discussion about renting a place in Brooklyn.
Those discussions are still ongoing, but they may be delayed for some time.
Some of our colleagues are working remotely for the first time.
Our colleagues who have been working remotely for a long time know that it may be an adjustment, and they’ve wanted to give you some advice:
The meeting time should be limited to a maximum of one hour or a gradual increase of up to two hours.
If it takes longer sessions, you should think about how to divide them over several days.
The meeting should be clearly defined, an agenda should be prepared, and reading materials should be sent in advance.
Video should be made the default solution, using tools such as Google Docs and Zoom to facilitate collaboration and direct communication.
Easily facilitate each meeting, and someone should monitor the conversation to find out the questions and track the list of speakers, and another person to help take notes (or carry out a collaborative notes).
An e-mail should be sent to technical support when comfortable headphones are needed.
The health and safety compensation program can be used to obtain the cost of snacks.
You can join #remoties channel in Slack app to talk to colleagues about distributed work
The HR process team is looking for user-friendly, meeting-based mentoring to support an increase incremented business distributed across our organization.
Over the past week, we have asked all recipients of community grants to cancel all public events funded by Wikimedia, such as those of editors of online website Editathon, until the World Health Organization declares the pandemic over.
We have told them that we understand that our request for cancellations and other restrictions may make it impossible to complete agreed grant activities, and that no one will be fined for delaying or modifying these objectives.
Next week, we’ll issue additional follow-up guidance related to Wikimedia and other regional community conferences with specific themes.
The public sentiment that prevails in the global community is floundering between sadness due to turbulence and satisfaction with clarity and the ability to focus on their communities, Wikimedia and others.
Going forward with these actions, the crisis response team is designing a page in the Meta Wiki to provide space for the community to monitor the impact and follow up on all communication with it.
Stay up-to-date on COVID-19-related topics
We'll send an invitation to your calendars next Thursday, at 14:00 UTC, 07:00 PST for the staff meeting.
We will use this time to share additional updates, answer questions, and spend time communicating between us.
We are going through this experience together and are always ready to help as much as possible.
In the meantime, we can continue to obtain information from this e-mail message, and all other necessary COVID-19-related information available at Office Wiki.
The crisis response team will keep these pages up-to-date and keep all the information in one place.
We are also working to maintain regular contacts with staff residing in the severely affected States.
If you have any questions about travel, events, key business areas, coverage challenges, or anything else you need assistance for, please do not hesitate to inform us and cooperate with the Crisis Response Team.
We are always prepared to provide support and communication as necessary.
If you have a confidential or sensitive matter, please send an email to Brian Goden, Director of Global Human Resources Operations.
None of these changes shall be deemed to be a relinquishment of the business entrusted to us or our obligations.
Instead, it’s a realization at the moment, that our work and commitments need to adapt in a way we’ve never seen in the past.
We believe these steps are necessary to support each other, so that we can continue to work and provide our employees with the support they need, and provide the world with the service they rely on.
The work that we have planned to achieve will await us when it comes.
Now, it’s time for everyone to help each other and create space for the important work that comes over the coming weeks and months.
We need all of you to achieve this, so we need all of you to take care of yourselves and your families to be at your best when your efforts are needed.
Now please wash your hands and don't touch your face!
Katherine, Crisis Response Team Manager (Amanda K, Amy V, Bryan J, Doreen D, Gregory V, Jamie V, Joel L., Lynette L., Ryan M, and Tony S), and other members of the leadership team (Grant Eye, Heather W, Jammie VI, Janine U, Lisa S, Robin A, Ryan M, and Toby Inn).
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) binds to the outer surface (cellular membranes) of cells in the lungs, arteries, heart and kidneys, as well as the intestines.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 inhibits the action of angiotensin converted enzyme (ACE) by reducing the amount of angiotensin 2 and increasing angiotensin (1–7); making it a promising drug in the treatment of cardiovascular disease. It is also an introduction to some types of coronaviruses into cells.
The human version of the enzyme is often referred to as hACE2.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is defined as a metal enzyme that contains zinc and is located on the surface of endothelial cells and other cells.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme protein 2 contains a peptidase M2 range that has an amino tip, and the carboxylic polycrystalline-enable end chain is a renal amino acid.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is a type 1 single-scroll membrane protein, which has active enzyme receptors found on the surface of lung cells and other tissues.
The part beyond the cell range of angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 separates from the range through the membrane by an enzyme called chidase, resulting in a soluble protein released into the bloodstream and then released through the urine.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is found in most organs of the body: it binds to the cellular membrane of type 2 alveolar cells in the lung, the intestinal cells of the intestines of the intestines, the endothelial cells of the arteries and veins, as well as in the cells of the smooth muscles of the arterial wall in most organs.
Angiotensin-converted RNA is present in the cerebral cortex, chart, hypothalamus, and brainstem.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 core function is to act as angiotensin-converting enzyme balancer.
Angiotensin converted enzyme stimulates the conversion of angiotensin 1 into angiotensin 2 astringent for blood vessels.
In contrast, angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 separates the carboxylic end containing amino acid phenylanine from angiotensine 2 (aspartite-arginine-valine-tyrosine-isolein-hestidine-phrolin-phenylalanine) and analyzes it into angiotensin (1-7), (hystedine-aspartite-arginine-valene-tyrosine-tyrosine-tyrosine-tyrosine-istocin-isolein-istolecin-hestolicine-histodine-hystedine-hestidine-hestidine-hestadine-prolin-phenylanine).
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is separated into a number of other peptides, including [Des-Argenine9]-Pradriquinin, Abeline, Neurotensin, Dinorphene A, and Gerlin.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 also regulates the membrane passage of a neutral transporter of SLC6A19 neutral amino acids, and also has a role in Hartneb's disease.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2, as a transmembranean protein, is the main input into the cells of certain coronaviruses, which include the human coronavirus NL63, SARS-CoV (the virus that causes SARS), and SARS-CoV-2 (the virus that causes COVID-19).
In particular, the association of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 with the enzymatic range of angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 on the cell surface leads to cytological rectification and transmission of the virus and the enzyme to the inner particles located in the cells.
This insertion process needs to configurate the S protein by transmembraneous protease, the host's serine 2, whose inhibitor is still being tested as a possible treatment. This makes some assume that lowering the levels of angiotensin-contalysin-converting enzyme 2 in the cells may help resist infection.
However, many specialized associations and self-regulating bodies recommend continuing to use the usual angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers as a treatment.
A methodological study and meta-analysis published on July 11, 2012, showed that "the use of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors was accompanied by a significant decrease in the risk of pneumonia by 34% compared to the reference group."
Moreover, “the risk of pneumonia has decreased among those who have received angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors treatment, and who have had a higher risk of pneumonia, especially those suffering from a stroke or heart failure.
The use of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors has led to a reduction in pneumonia deaths, although the results have been less robust compared to the overall risk of pneumonia.”
Recombinant angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (rhACE2) is thought to be a new treatment for acute lung injuries. It has been found to improve pulmonary blood distribution, as well as oxygen saturation in pigs suffering from acute shortness of breath syndrome induced by polysaccharides.
Recombinant angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is about 10 hours, while it begins to work after 30 minutes, as well as continuing its effectiveness (time period) up to 24 hours.
There are many indications to suggest that recombinant angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 may be a promising treatment for those with conventional angiotensin resonnail inhibitors (RAS inhibitors), and in diseases associated with a rise in angiotensin 2 in the blood. Clinical trials are under evaluation, on the effect of recombinant angiotensin-converting enzyme infusion in the treatment of acute respiratory dysmania syndrome.
Covid-19 apps are mobile software apps specifically designed to help track people in contact with the 2019-20 coronavirus pandemic, which means the process of identifying people (“contacts”) who may have come into contact with an infected person.
Numerous applications have been designed or proposed with official government support in some regions and jurisdictions.
Many frameworks have been developed to design applications to track contacts.
Many have voiced concerns about privacy, particularly about systems that rely on geolocation tracking for app users.
Lest-intrusive alternatives to privacy involve using bluetooth signals to record a user’s proximity to other cell phones.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple together announced that they might integrate a function that supports these Bluetooth-based apps, directly into their Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the Chinese government, in cooperation with Alipay, has published an app that allows citizens to check whether or not they came into contact with people with COVID-19.
It is an application used by individuals in more than 200 Chinese cities. In Singapore, individuals use an application called TraceTogether.
A local information technology association has designed the app, released it as an open-source application and is due to be handed over to the government. North Macedonia has launched StopKorona, a Bluetooth-based application that tracks exposure to contact with potentially infected individuals and provide rapid response to health care authorities.
The Ministry of Communications and Technology, in cooperation with the Ministry of Health, has designed the application.
On April 14, 2020, the app was still awaiting adoption of Apple’s Google Play Store and App Store.
On April 12, the government stated that the contact tracing app had been in advanced development and might become available for distribution within weeks. Ireland and France plan to launch a similar app, "StopCovid").
Australia and New Zealand are both considering designing apps based on Singapore’s TraceTogether app, as well as the BlueTres protocol.Russia intends to introduce an app that uses the geographic perimeter of patients diagnosed with COVID-19 from Moscow, which is designed to make sure they don’t leave home.
Ross Anderson, a professor of security engineering at the University of Cambridge, has mentioned a number of potential practical problems that surround application-based systems, including false positives and a potential inefficiencies if application use is limited to only a small segment of the population.
To address concerns about the spread of misleading or harmful coronavirus apps, Apple has imposed restrictions on the types of organizations that can add their coronavirus-linked apps to its app store, restricting only to “official” or reputable organizations.
Google and Amazon have implemented similar restrictions.
Privacy advocates have voiced concerns about the consequences of mass surveillance resulting from the use of coronavirus apps, particularly with regard to whether the surveillance infrastructure designed to deal with the coronavirus pandemic will go away once the threat lapses.
Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations have issued a statement calling for restrictions on this type of surveillance.
The organizations also announced eight conditions for government projects:
Observations should be “lawful, necessary and proportionate”;
Monitoring and control extensions should include clauses relating to determining the time of their discontinuation;
The use of data should be limited to COVID-19 purposes;
The security of data and the confidentiality of the identity of the owners should be protected on the basis of the evidence;
Digital surveillance should avoid exacerbating discrimination and marginalization;
Any sharing of data with third parties to the law should be identified;
There should be protections against abuse and the protection of the rights of citizens to address abuse;
‘Active engagement’ is required from all ‘stakeholders involved’, including public health experts and marginalized groups. The German Chaos Computer Club (CCCC) and Reporters Without Borders (Reporter ohne Grenzen) (RSF) have also issued checklists.
The proposed Google/Apple plan aims to address the problem of continuous monitoring by removing the tracking mechanism from device operating systems as soon as it’s needed.
Some countries have used network-based location tracking rather than using apps, eliminating both the need to download the app and avoiding tracking.
In Israel, officials have adopted network-based tracking.
But network-based solutions that have access to the initial data of the sites involve potentially significant privacy issues.
However, not all systems with centralized servers need access to personal location data; a number of privacy preservation systems have been established that use central servers for interconnections only (see section below).
In South Korea, officials used a non-application-based system to carry out contact tracing operations.
Instead of using a dedicated application, the system collects tracking information from various sources, including mobile tracking data and card transaction data, and includes this data to send notifications via text messages to individuals with potential infections.
In addition to using this information to alert potential contacts, the government has made site information publicly available as well, which it allowed due to wide-ranging changes to information privacy laws following the outbreak of Middle East Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus in that country.
This information is available to the public through a number of applications and websites. Countries, including Germany, have considered the use of both centralized and privacy-preserving systems.
On April 6, 2020, details about this order have not yet been released.
On April 7, 2020, more than a dozen groups of experts have worked on accessing privacy-friendly solutions, such as low-energy Bluetooth (LPLE) to record a user’s proximity to other cell phones.
However, PEBP-PT has been a coordinating effort that contains both centralized and decentralized approaches, not an individual protocol. Decentralization-based protocols include privacy-conserving decentralization (DPBT/DP-3T), temporary contact numbers (TCN, formerly known as contact event numbers, CEN), privacy sensitive protocols, mobile contact tracing mechanisms (BACT), and more.
In these protocols, personal identification data does not leave the device, and all matches occur on the device.
MIT Media Lab’s privacy group has been developing SafePaths, a platform to use privacy-preservating technologies when collecting site data or intersecting paths and using them to track the spread of COVID-19.
It draws on the research learned from the report “Evil Face of Apps: Preserving Personal Privacy in the Pandemic” released in March 2020. Enigma MPC’s SafeTrace platform, a privacy technology developer that also originated in MIT Media Lab, is another similar effort.
SafeTrace uses secure device technologies to allow users to share sensitive location and health data with users and other administrators without compromising data privacy.
On April 5, 2020, the Global Contact Number Tracking Coalition was founded by groups focused on what was fundamentally a similar approach and significantly intertwined protocols, whose goal was to reduce distraction and enable global interoperability between tracking and alerting applications, and is a key feature of achieving widespread usage.
On April 9, 2020, the Singaporean government announced that it had made the Blue Tres protocol, which is used by the official government application, open source.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, the two companies that control the Android and iOS mobile operating systems, announced an initiative to track contacts, claiming that they would maintain privacy based on the combination of low-energy Bluetooth technology and privacy-preserving encryption.
They also published the specifications of the main technologies used in the system.
According with Apple and Google, the goal is to put the system in three phases:
Introducing tools to enable governments to create official privacy-preserving applications to track the Coronavirus
Integrating this function directly into the Android and iOS system as Google and Apple plan to resolve usage issues and continuous monitoring by distributing systems first via operating system updates and then by removing them in the same way once the threat ends.
A drug relocation (also known as reuse, reallocatization, retargetion, target change or therapeutic transformation of the drug) is the re-use of an approved drug for the treatment of a different disease or medical condition than what was originally designed for treatment.
This is a method of scientific research that researchers are currently following to develop safe and effective treatments for COVID-19.
Other research trends include the development of the COVID-19 vaccine and the transfer of plasma convulsives. SARS-CoV-2 contains approximately 66 drug-treatable proteins, each of which has multiple binding sites.
Analyzing these binding sites provides a reasonable plan for the development of an effective antiviral drug that eliminates COVID-19 proteins.
Among the most important SARS-CoV-2 target proteins are the door-like protease, RNA polymerase based on RNA, helicase, S-protein, and repo phosphatase for adenosine diophosphate.
Hussain A. and others have studied several candidate compounds that have subsequently undergone improved and analyzed for the similarity of their structure to highly similarly approved drugs in order to accelerate the development of a powerful drug against SARS-CoV-2 in their pre-clinical study to be recommended in the design of a clinical study.
Chloroquine is a malaria treatment drug that doctors also use to treat certain autoimmune diseases.
On March 18, the World Health Organization announced that chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine associated with it could be among the four drugs studied as part of a clinical solidarity trial.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo has announced that New York State's trials of chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine may begin on March 24. On March 28, the Food and Drug Administration authorized the use of hydroxychloroquine sulfate and chloroquine phosphate under an emergency use license (UA).
The treatment was not approved by the Food and Drug Administration’s clinical trial process and was licensed under an emergency use license only as an experimental treatment for emergency use to treat patients who were hospitalized but cannot receive treatment in the clinical trial.
The CDC has announced that it has not yet been determined to "use hydroxychloroquine to prevent, treat, dosing or period of infection with SARS-CoV-2."
Doctors said that they use the drug when “there is no other option.”
A Turkish research team in Istanbul is currently conducting a small study on the use of chloriquene in addition to zinc, vitamin A, vitamin C and vitamin D.
Major studies are conducted at the University of Duke and the University of Oxford.
New York University’s Langone School of Medicine is conducting an experiment on the safety and efficacy of hydroxychloroquine protective use.
Chinese clinical trials in Wuhan and Shenzhen have claimed that Favipiravir was “clearly effective.”
Out of 35 patients in Schengen, test results were passive in an average of 4 days, while the illness period was 11 days in 45 patients who did not receive it.
In a study conducted in Wuhan on 240 patients with pneumonitis, half of them were given favipiravir and the other half received omiphenovir.
The Italian Medicines Agency has alerted people to the fact that the existing evidence supporting the property is incomplete and preliminary.
On 2 April, Germany announced that it would purchase the drug from Japan to be included in its reserve stock, and would use the military to deliver the drug to university hospitals, where the drug would be used to treat COVID-19 patients.
Shinzo Abe held preliminary talks with the Trump administration about buying the drug.The drug may be less influential in the treatment of more severe cases as the virus has already multiplied.
It may not be safe for pregnant women or women who want to become pregnant.
One study that used lopinavir/ritonavir (Calytra), a combination of lubinavir and retonavir, reported that the results “did not detect any benefit.”
The drugs were designed to inhibit HIV to prevent it from replicating by binding it to protease.
A team of researchers at the University of Colorado is trying to modify the drugs to reach a compound associated with SARS-CoV-2.The scientific community is witnessing a state of criticism about directing resources to redefinite the purposes of drugs specifically designed to treat HIV/AIDS.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has added Lubinavir/Retonavir to the International Solidarity Experience.
Gilead Sciences has developed remedisiver as a treatment for Ebola virus disease and Marburg virus infection. Gilead Sciences has subsequently discovered that remedicivir is characterized by antiviral activity in the laboratory against many filamentous viruses, mucous viruses and coronaviruses.
One of the problems with antiretroviral therapy is the development of resistance through mutations that can lead to more serious diseases and their transmission.
There are many current multiple clinical trials, including trials conducted by the University of Cleveland hospitals, one revolving around those with moderate disease, and the other for patients with the most severe disease.
There are three clinical trials of antiviral vitamin C in patients who have been hospitalized and are suffering from an acute illness due to COVID-19, as well as two controlled trials of placebo (China, Canada) and an uncontrollable trial (Italy).
The state of New York began experiments on the effect of antibiotic azithromycin on March 24, 2020.
Japan’s National Center for Public Health and Medicine (NCGM) plans to carry out a clinical trial on Teijin’s Alvesco (Cyclesonide), an inhaled corticosteroid for asthma, to treat patients with the novel coronavirus before symptoms appear.
A Phase II trial is currently underway using an angiotensin-converted anytenesin-converting an angiotensin-conversing enzyme for 200 patients who are required to join the trial from acutely hospitalized cases in Denmark, Germany, and Austria to determine the effectiveness of the treatment.
Researchers from the Montreal Heart Institute in Canada are currently studying the role of colchicine in reducing inflammation and pulmonary complications in patients with mild symptoms of COVID-19.
The study, named after Kolkorona, involves 6,000 adults aged 40 and over who were diagnosed with COVID-19 and experienced mild symptoms without having to be hospitalized.
Pregnant women who are breastfed or who do not have an effective contraceptive are not eligible.
Many anticoagulants are also being tested in Italy.
Low molecular weight heparin is currently widely used to treat patients, which requires the Italian Medicines Agency to release guidance on its use.
A multi-centered study was announced in Italy on 300 patients to investigate the use of sodium inoxaparine in doses for disease prevention and therapeutic doses on April 14.
Because SARS-CoV-2 is a virus, the focus has focused on the reuse of approved antivirals designed to deal with previous outbreaks such as the Mars, SARS, and West Nile virus.
Specialists recommended the use of ribavirin to treat COVID-19 according to the 7th version of the Chinese directives
Omiphenovir: Specialists recommended the use of Omifinovir to treat COVID-19 according to the 7th version of the Chinese directives
Some of the antibiotics that specialists have identified as potentially reusable as COVID-19 therapies include:
Tusilizumab (Interleukin 6 antirecipator): certified by China.
Experiments in Italy and China as well. See Tusilizumab#COVID-19.
The COVID-19 Vaccine is a Presumptive Vaccine Against Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19).
Although no vaccines have been completed for clinical trials, several attempts are underway to develop this vaccine.
In late February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) said it did not expect a vaccine against SARS-CoV-2, the causative virus, to be available in less than 18 months.
There were five candidate vaccines in the first phase of safety studies in April.
COVID-19 was identified in December 2019.
A major outbreak spread around the world in 2020, leading to significant investments and research activity to develop a vaccine.
Many organizations use published genomes to develop potential vaccines against SARS-CoV-2.
The requirements of the Vaccine Development Alliance’s Epidemic Preparedness Innovations Initiative, announced in April, are speed, manufacturing capacity, large-scale deployment and global reach.
In April, scientists of the Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations reported that 10 different technical platforms were being researched and developed during early 2020 to create an effective COVID-19 vaccine.
The platform’s main objectives that have progressed to the first phase of safety studies include:
DNA (DNB and RNA) (Developer of Phase I and Vaccine: Moderna, mRNA-1273)
Viral vector (stage 1 developer, candidate vaccine: Moderna, type 5 adenovirus vector)
According to scientists of the Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations in April, there are a total of 115 vaccine candidates in the early stages of development, with 78 active projects confirmed (79, according to the Milkin Institute), and 37 other projects have been announced, but with little public information (presumed to be in the planning stage or being designed).
The Phase I-II trial conducts an initial test of safety and resuscitation, which is usually randomized, and is controlled by inatention, in multiple locations, with more accurate and effective doses.
Phase III trials typically involve more participants, including the reference group, and a vaccine effectiveness test for disease prevention, while observing the negative effects of the optimum dose.
Of the 79 candidate vaccines under active development (confirmed as of early April 2020), 74 are not yet under human assessment (still being researched "before-clinical").
About January 24, 2020 in Australia, the University of Queensland announced that it was investigating the possibility that a molecular synapse vaccine that would genetically modify viral proteins in order to trigger an immunological reaction.
Approximately on January 24, 2020 in Canada, the Center for International Vaccines (VIDO-InterVac) at the University of Saskatchewan announced the start of work on a vaccine, targeting the start of tests in humans in 2021.
Vaccine development projects were announced at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention on January 26, 2020, and at the University of Hong Kong on January 28.
Around January 29, 2020, Janssen pharmaceutical companies, led by Haneke Schöitmaker, announced that they had begun work on the development of a vaccine.
Janssen is co-developing an oral vaccine with its partner in biotechnology, Vaxart.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced a manufacturing partnership with Vaxart to develop the vaccine.
On February 8, 2020, the OncoGen laboratory in Romania published a study on the design of a vaccine with technology similar to those used in the treatment of cancer with the new antigen vaccine.
On March 25, the head of the research institute announced that they had finished manufacturing the vaccine and that they had begun testing.
On February 27, 2020, NuGenerex Immuno-Oncology, a subsidiary of Generex, announced that it had started a vaccine project to synthesize the Ii-Key peptide vaccine against COVID-19.
They wanted to produce a candidate vaccine that could be tested in humans “within 90 days.”
On March 5, 2020, the University of Washington in St. Louis announced projects to develop a vaccine.
On March 5, 2020, the U.S. Army Medical and Materiel Research Command at Fort Derek and the Walter Reed Military Research Institute in Silver Spring, both in Western Maryland, announced that they were working on a vaccine.
Approximately on March 10, 2020, Emergent Biosolutions announced that it had formed a team with Novavax Inc.
In the development and manufacture of a vaccine.
The partners also announced plans for pre-clinical and Phase I clinical trials by July 2020.
On March 12, 2020, India’s Ministry of Health announced that it was working with 11 isolated cases, and that even as the pace of work was accelerated, it would take at least one and a half to almost two years to develop a vaccine.
On March 12, 2020, Medicago, a biotechnology company in Quebec City of Quebec, reported that it was developing a coronavirus-litted particle with partial funding from the Canadian Institutes of Health Research.
Candidated vaccine is undergoing laboratory research, with plans to be tested in humans in July or August 2020.
Earlier that week, the Guardian reported that U.S. President Donald Trump had offered CureVac “significant sums of money” for exclusive access to the COVID-19 vaccine, which the German government has protested.
On March 17, 2020, U.S. pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced a partnership with Germany’s BioNTech to co-develop a vaccine based on messenger RNA.
BNT162-candiminated MRNA vaccine is currently undergoing pre-clinical testing with clinical trials expected to begin in April 2020.
In Italy on March 17, 2020, Takis Biotech, an Italian biotechnology company, announced that it would receive pre-clinical test results in April 2020 and its final candidate vaccine could start testing in humans by autumn.
In France on March 19, 2020, the Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations (CABI) announced an investment of US$4.9 million in the COVID-19 Vaccine Research Consortium involving the Pasteur Institute, Themis Bioscience (Vienna, Austria), and the University of Pittsburgh, proposing the overall investment of the Pandemic Preparedness Innovation Alliance in the development of the COVID-19 vaccine to US$29 million.
The COVID-19 COVID-19 Vaccine Preparedness Alliance partners are Moderna, Curevac, Novavax, University of Hong Kong, University of Oxford and the University of Queensland.
On March 20, 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had begun animal testing for six different candidate vaccines.
Researchers at Imperial College of London announced on March 20, 2020 that they are developing a self-amplifying RNA vaccine for COVID-19.
The candidate vaccine was developed within 14 days of receiving the sequence from China.
In late March, the Canadian government announced $275 million in funding for 96 research projects on COVID-19 medicinal measures, including several candidate vaccines in Canadian companies and universities, such as Medicago Initiatives and the University of Saskatchewan.
At about the same time, the Canadian government announced 192 million Canadian dollars specifically for the development of the COVID-19 vaccine, with plans to create a national “vaccine bank” of several new vaccines that could be used in the event of another coronavirus outbreak.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at the University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine reported tests for PittCoVacc, a potential COVID-19 vaccine in mice, where they reported that "SARS-CoV-2 subunit vaccines given to microscopic needles provoked strong responses to specific antigestic antibodies [in mice] that were evident starting from 2 weeks after vaccination."
In Canada on April 16, 2020, the University of Waterloo’s School of Pharmacy announced the design of a candidate vaccine based of hypoxic DNA as a possible nasal spray.
Using bacteriophages, oxygen-deprivated RNA will be designed for replication within human bacteria to produce harmless particles similar to the virus, which may stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies for SARS-CoV-2.
In March 2020, the U.S. government, industry, and three universities pooled resources to access IBM supercomputers, along with cloud computing resources from Hewlett Packard Enterprise, Amazon, Microsoft, and Google.
Some vaccines have opposite effects, and are also called unspecified effects.
This means that she can have benefits that go beyond the disease she’s fighting.
Another randomized trial in Australia seeks to register 4,170 healthcare workers.
Vaccines being developed are unlikely to be safe or effective.
Early research to assess the effectiveness of the vaccine using COVID-19-specific animal models, such as angiotensin-converted mice, and other laboratory animals, and non-human primates, point to the need for Tier-3 biosafety containment measures to deal with live viruses, and international coordination to ensure standardized safety procedures.
SARS and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome vaccines have been tested on non-human animal models.
As of 2020, there is no SARS preventive treatment or vaccine that has been shown to be both safe and effective in humans.
According to papers published in 2005 and 2006, the identification and development of new vaccines and drugs for SARS has been a priority for governments and public health agencies around the world.
When March spread, it was thought that existing research on SARS might provide a useful model for the development of vaccines and therapies against Mars-CoV infection.
As of March 2020, there was one Middle East Respiratory Syndrome vaccine (based on DRNA) that had completed the first phase of clinical trials in humans, and three other vaccines under development, all of which were virus-oriented vaccines (ChAdOx1-MERS, BVRS-GamVac), and one MVA (MVA-MERS-S).
Social media postings sparked a conspiracy theory alleging that the virus causing COVID-19 was known and that the vaccine was already available.
Patents cited by many social media posts refer to existing patents for genetic sequencing and vaccines for other strains of coronaviruses such as SARS-causing coronavirus.
Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) is a contagion caused by Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Common symptoms include fever, cough, and shortness of breath.
Other symptoms may include fatigue, muscle pain, diarrhea, sore throat, loss of smell, and abdominal pain.
The period between exposure to symptoms is usually about five days, but it can be between two and fourteen days.
Although most cases show mild symptoms, some develop into viral pneumonia and multiple organ failure.
On April 17, 2020, more than 2.24 million people were reported infected in 210 countries and territories, resulting in more than 153,000 deaths.
More than 568,000 people have recovered. The virus is mainly prevalent among people during mixing, often by scattered sprinkled sprinkling, sneezing, or talking.
When the spray is scattered during exhalation, it usually falls to the ground or on surfaces rather than contagious over long distances.
People may also be injured when touching a contaminated surface and then touching their eyes, noses or mouths.
The virus can survive on surfaces for up to 72 hours.
It's more contagious in the first three days of symptoms, although it may spread before the onset of symptoms and in the later stages of the disease. The standard method of diagnosis is a polymerase chain reaction of instant retraction (RRT-PCR) through a nasopharyngeal swab.
Masks are recommended for those who are suspected of being infected with the virus and their caregivers.
Recommendations for the use of masks by the general public vary, with some authorities recommending not using them, some seeing otherwise, and others making it imperative.
At the moment, there is no specific vaccine or antiviral treatment for COVID-19.
Transmission has been reported at the local level in most countries in all six WHO regions.
People with the virus may have no symptoms or show flu-like symptoms, such as fever, cough, fatigue and shortness of breath.
Immediate medical attention is recommended if there are emergency symptoms of difficulty breathing, persistent pain or pressure in the chest area, disorder, difficulty waking, or cyanosis of the face or lips.
In less common cases, symptoms of the upper respiratory tract such as sneezing, runny nose or sore throat may be observed.
Symptoms related to the digestive tract such as nausea, vomiting and diarrhea have been observed in varying proportions.
Initially, some cases in China suffered from chest tightness and palpitations only.
In some, the disease may develop into pneumonia, multiple organ failure, and death.
It's called incubation.
The incubation period for COVID-19 is usually five to six days, but it can range from two to 14 days.
97.5% of those who show symptoms will do this within 11.5 days of the infection.Reports indicate that symptoms may not appear in all infected people.
It has not yet been fully recognized the role of carriers without showing symptoms in transmission; however, preliminary evidence suggests that they may contribute to the spread of the disease.
The percentage of people infected without symptoms is currently unknown and is under study, and the Korea Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (KCCDC) reported that 20% of the total confirmed cases did not show symptoms during their stay in the hospital.
China’s National Health Commission began listing asymptomatic cases into the daily toll on April 1; out of 166 cases that day, 130 (78 percent) of these cases didn’t show symptoms on testing.
Phlegm and saliva may carry large proportions of the virus.
Speaking loudly produces more spray than from talking normally.
A study in Singapore has shown that coughing without covering the mouth may lead to a sprinkled sprinkled to a distance of up to 4.5 meters (15 feet).
Although the virus is not usually transmitted by air, the National Academy of Sciences has indicated that it can be transmitted through bioastrophy, and examining samples from the air complexes in the hallway outside individual rooms has yielded positive results for viral RNA.
Some medical procedures, such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), may release respiratory secretions in the form of a spray, and then the virus spreads through the air.
Although there are concerns that it may spread through feces, it is believed that the risk is limited. The virus is more contagious when people experience symptoms; while it may spread before symptoms appear, the risk is lower.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) says that despite some ambiguity about how easily the disease spreads, one person can enumerate from two to three others in general. The virus remains stuck on surfaces from hours to days.
Specifically, the virus has been found to be present for one day on cardboard, for up to three days on polypropylene and stainless steel (AISI 304), and up to four hours on 99 percent copper.
However, this varies based on humidity and temperature.
Soaps and disinfectants are also effective, provided that they are used correctly, as the soap dissolves the protective fatty membrane of the virus and stops its work, in addition to ridding the skin and other surfaces from it.
Other solutions, such as benzalconium chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate (surgical disinfectant), are available to be less efficacy. In a study in Hong Kong, saliva samples were taken after an average of two days of hospitalization.
In five out of six patients, the first sample showed the highest viral pregnancies, and on the second day of the test, the sixth patient had the highest viral pregnancy.
Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a novel coronavirus associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome, where three people with pneumonia were first isolated in the Wuhan acute respiratory disease group.
The emerging SARS-CoV-2 virus shares its characteristics with the relevant coronaviruses found in nature.
Outside the body, household soap eliminates the virus, as it destroys its protective membrane. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-Cove.
The lungs are the two organs most affected by COVID-19 because the virus enters the host cells by angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2), which is the most abundant in type II alveoli cells found in the lung.
The virus uses a special superficial glycoprotein called "spike" (the fulfi clipart) to connect to ACE2 and enter the host cell.
12% of infected people hospitalized in Wuhan, China, have had severe heart attacks and are more prevalent in critical conditions.
Cardiovascular-related symptomatic rates are high, due to systemic inflammatory response and disorders of the immune system during the development of the disease, but acute heart muscle injury may also be associated with ACE2 receptors in the heart.
AC2 receptors have a very important role in the heart and participate in the performance of its functions.
Thrombosis (31%) and intravenous thrombosis (25%) in patients in the intensive care unit with COVID-19 have increased, which may be associated with poor prediction of the outcome of the condition. The autopsies of individuals who have died of COVID-19 have been found to have diffused alveolar damage (DAD), and inflammatory infiltration that contains lymphocytic cells inside the lung.
Although SARS-CoV-2 is moving towards the epithelial cells expressing ACE2 in the respiratory tract, patients with severe COVID-19 have symptoms of systemic hyperinflammation.
In particular, it has been shown that T cells secreting GM-CSF pathogenic diseases are associated with fetching the only cells that excrete interlokin 6 that causes inflammation and worsening lung disease in COVID-19 patients.
It has also been reported that there is a lymphatic infiltration at the autopsy.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has published several testing protocols for the disease.
Intractor polymerase retractive reaction (RRT-PCR) is the standard method of testing.
Testing is usually done using respiratory samples taken from a nasopharyngeal swab; however, a nasal swab or phlegm sample can also be used.
Results are generally available within a couple of hours to two days.
Blood tests can be used, but this requires taking two blood samples within two weeks and the results have little direct value.
Chinese scientists have been able to isolate a strain of coronavirus and spread genetic sequencing so that laboratories around the world can independently develop polymerase chain reaction (PCR) tests to detect infection with the virus.
On April 4, 2020, antibodies tests (which may detect active infections and whether a person has been infected in the past) were under development, but have not yet been widely used.
The Chinese experience of testing has shown that accuracy ranges only from 60 to 70 percent.
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration approved the first Clinical Care Point test on March 21, 2020 for use at the end of that month. Diagnostic guidelines issued by Zhongnan Hospital at the University of Wuhan proposed methods to detect infections based on clinical characteristics and epidemiological risks.
Multi-lobined two-sided flaps with peripheral, asymmetrial and posterior diffusion are common features at the onset of infection.
A spherosis may be manifested under the side and shape of a stone tranquility (thickness of the lobular septum with uneven alveolar fillers) and merging with the progression of the disease.
Few data is available on microscopic lesions and pathophysiology of COVID-19.
The main pathological conclusions of autopsies:
Microscopic examination: pleusitis, pericarditis, enlarged lung and pulmonary edema
Four types of the severity of viral pneumonia can be observed:
Mild pneumonia: pulmonary edema, pneumonial hyperplasia, untypical large alveolic cells, intramitia with lymphatic infiltration and the formation of giant multi-core cells
Acute pneumonia: diffused alveolar damage (DAD) with diffused alveolar secretions.
Diffused alveoric damage causes acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and acute hypoxia.
Pneumonia treatment: regulation of secretions in alveolar cavities and interstitial pulmonary fibrosis
Blomb: diffuse invascular thrombosis (DIC); white blood cell reaction
Preventive measures to reduce the odds include staying at home, avoiding crowded places, washing your hands with soap and water frequently for at least 20 seconds, practicing good respiratory hygiene, and avoiding touching your eye, nose or mouth with unwashed hands.
The CDC recommends covering the mouth and nose with a handkerchief when coughing or sneezing, and it is recommended to use the inside of the elbow if a handkerchief is not available.
It is recommended to clean the hands well after any cough or sneezing.
The CDC has recommended the use of canvas face coverings in public places, partly aimed at reducing asymptomatic transmission of infections. Social distancing strategies aim to reduce the mixing of people with large groups by closing schools and workplaces, restricting travel and canceling large public gatherings.
Spacing guidelines also include that people stay away from each other by at least 6 feet (1.8 m).
There is no drug known to be effective in preventing COVID-19. Because a vaccine is not expected until 2021 at the earliest, the main part of COVID-19 control is trying to reduce the peak of the epidemic, which is known as “flatting the curve.”
The CDC also recommends that people wash their hands frequently with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when their hands are clearly dirty, before eating, and after cleaning the nose, coughing or sneezing.
It is also recommended to use at least 60% alcohol-containing hand sanitizer, when only soap and water are unavailable. For areas where commercial hand sanitizers are not readily available, the World Health Organization (WHO) offers two combinations for local production.
Antimicrobial activity originates in these two structures from ethanol or isoprobanol.
Hydrogen peroxide is used to help eliminate bacterial spores in alcohol; it is “not an effective substance for disinfecting the hands.”
Glycerol is added as a moisturizer.
People receive supportive care, which may include fluid therapy, oxygen support, and aid for other affected vital organs.
The CDC recommends that people who are suspected of being infected with the virus wear a simple face mask.
Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) is used to address the problem of respiratory failure, but its benefits are still being studied.
It is recommended for personal hygiene and reliance on a healthy lifestyle and diet to improve immunity.
Supportive therapies may benefit people with mild symptoms during the early stage of infection. The World Health Organization and the China National Health Commission have published recommendations to care for those who have been hospitalized for COVID-19.
Intensive caregiologists and pulmonologists in the U.S. have compiled treatment recommendations from a variety of bodies into a free resource, a critical care book available online.
As of April 2020, there was no specific treatment for COVID-19.
As for symptoms, some specialist physicians recommend using paracetamol (acetaminophen) instead of ibuprofen as the first treatment.
Precautions should be taken to minimize the risk of transmission of the virus, especially in health care settings when performing actions that may result in spray, such as intubation or manual breathing.
For healthcare professionals who care for people with COVID-19, CDC recommends placing the person in an air-transmitted isolation room as well as using standard precautions, mixing precautions and airborne disease precautions.
Recommended tools: personal protective equipment aprons, a respirator or face mask, two-eyed condoms and medical gloves. Respirators (rather than face masks) are preferred.
N95 respirators have been approved for industrial facilities, but the Food and Drug Administration has authorized the use of masks under an emergency use license.
They are designed to protect against airborne particles such as dust, but their effectiveness against a particular biological agent is uncertain in the case of uses for purposes other than specified.
When masks are not available, the CDC recommends using face protectors or homemade masks as the last resort.
Most COVID-19 cases aren’t deteriorating to the extent that it requires artificial respiration or alternatives, but some cases do.
The type of respiratory support provided to people with COVID-19-related respiratory failure for those in hospitals is being actively studied, with some evidence that intubation may be avoided, using high-flowing nasal ducts or bilateral positive pressure of the airway.
It is not known whether either of these options is of the same benefit to those with a critical condition or not.
Some doctors prefer to continue using invasive ventilation when available because this technique limits the spread of aerosol particles compared to a high-flow nasal cannula. Critical conditions abound among the elderly (over the age of 60, especially those over the age of 80).
Many developed countries don't have enough hospital beds per capita, restricting the health system's ability to cope with the sudden rise in the number of dangerous COVID-19 cases that require hospitalization.
One study in China found that 5 percent of cases were admitted to intensive care units, 2.3 percent needed artificial respiratory support, and 1.4 percent died.
In China, nearly 30% of those hospitalized due to COVID-19 are eventually admitted to intensive care units.
Ventilation becomes more complex with the development of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) in people with COVID-19 and the supply of oxygen becomes more difficult.
Respirators need to be characterized by pressure control modes and high positive expiratory pressure to maximize the delivery of oxygen while minimizing the risk of lung injury associated with the respirator and pneumothorax.
A high positive end-of-expiratory pressure may not be available in older respirators.
The search for potential therapies began in January 2020, and several antiviral drugs are in clinical trials.
It seems that Remedicivir is the most missionary of it.
Although the new drugs may take until 2021 to develop, many of the drugs that are being tested have already been approved for other uses or are already in an advanced stage of testing.
Antiviral drugs can be tested on those with a critical condition.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has recommended volunteers to participate in experiments on the efficacy and safety of potential therapies. The Food and Drug Administration has granted a temporary license to use convalescent plasma as an experimental treatment in cases where a person's life is seriously or directly threatened.
It has not undergone the necessary clinical studies to prove that it is safe and effective against the disease.
In February 2020, China launched a mobile application to deal with the outbreak.
Users are required to enter the name and ID number.
The application is able to detect ‘contacts’ using surveillance data and thus the risk of potential infection.
Each user can also verify the status of three other users.
If a potential risk is discovered, the app not only recommends self-quarantine, but also alerts local health officials.Big data analytics for mobile data, facial recognition technology, mobile phone tracking, and artificial intelligence are used to track infected people in South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore.
In March 2020, the Israeli government enabled the security services to track mobile phone data of those presumed to be infected with coronavirus.
The measure has been taken to impose quarantines and protect those who are likely to come into contact with injured citizens.
Also in March 2020, Deutsche Telekom shared aggregated phone location data with the German federal government agency, the Robert Koch Institute, for the purposes of research and preventing the spread of the virus.
Russia has used facial recognition technology to detect quarantine violators.
Italy’s regional health commissioner, Giulio Gallera, said mobile phone companies had told him that “40 percent of people are still moving everywhere.”
The German government conducted a 48-hour hackathon over the weekend in which more than 42,000 people participated.
Estonia’s President, Ms. Kirsty Kaljulaide, has also made a global call to present innovative solutions to prevent the spread of the coronavirus.
Individuals may feel distressed by quarantines, travel restrictions, side effects of treatment or fear of injury itself.
Rory O'Connor said: ‘Increasing social isolation, loneliness, anxiety about health, stress and economic stagnation is a torrential storm that harms people’s mental health and well-being.
The disease may take a mild cycle that involves minor symptoms or be asymptomatic, similar to other common upper respiratory diseases such as a cold.
Minor cases usually recover within two weeks, while severe or critical cases take three to six weeks to recover.
Pregnant women may be more susceptible to severe COVID-19 based on data for other similar viruses, such as SARS and MERS, but there is still a lack of data on COVID-19.
Covid-19 in those most severely affected may quickly develop into acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), leading to respiratory failure, septic shock, or multiple organ failure.
Complications associated with COVID-19 are sepsis, abnormal blood clotting, and damage to the heart, kidney and liver.
6% of people with COVID-19 who were hospitalized were diagnosed with blood clot abnormalities, specifically an increase in prothrombin time, while kidney dysfunction was observed in 4%.
Approximately 20-30% of people with COVID-19 showed an increase in levels of liver enzymes (amine vectors).
According to the same report, the average period between the appearance of symptoms and death was ten days, including five days in the hospital.
However, the average period for those transferred to the intensive care unit between hospitalization and death was seven days.
In a study of early cases, the average period from initial symptoms to death was 14 days, and at the total level of cases from six days to 41 days.
In a study conducted by China's National Health Committee (NHC), the mortality rate for men was 2.8%, while the mortality rate for women was 1.7%.
Histopic anatomical anatomical examinations of lung samples after death show diffused alveolar damage with cellular fibromoxide secretions in both lungs.
Viral cytopathy changes have been observed in alveolar cells.
The lung image shows acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
Heart damage due to elevated troponin levels or cardiac arrest was observed in 11.8% of deaths reported by China’s National Health Commission.
According to U.S. data released in March, 89% of those hospitalized had previous medical conditions.
Estimates of deaths resulting from the condition vary as a result of those differences between regions, but also due to methodological difficulties.
An impermissibred inventory of simple cases may lead to an appreciation of the mortality rate above the truth.
However, the fact that previous cases have caused deaths may indicate that the current death rate is lower than the reality.
Smokers are 1.4 times more likely to experience severe symptoms of COVID-19 and 2.4 times more likely to need intensive care and more likely to die than non-smokers.
The Hong Kong Hospital Authority has revealed a reduction in lung capacity by 20% to 30% in some people recovering from the disease, and lung tests have indicated damage.
It may also cause post-intensive intensive care syndrome after recovery.
As of March 2020, it was not known whether the previous infection caused those recovering from the disease to be effective and long-lasting immunity.
Immunity acquisition is seen as potential, based on the behavior of other coronaviruses, but it has been announced that there have been cases of COVID-19 recovered and then contracted again later.
It is believed that these conditions are exacerbated if the infection persists more than when repeated.
It is believed that the virus is normal and has animal origin, given the spread of infection.
The actual origin is unknown, but by December 2019, the spread of the infection was largely due to human-to-human transmission.
A study of the first 41 confirmed cases of COVID-19, published in January 2020 in The Lancet, revealed that December 1, 2019 is the first date for symptoms.
According to official publications issued by the World Health Organization (WHO), 8 December 2019 is the first date for symptoms.
Numerous actions are usually taken to determine the number of deaths.
These figures vary by region and over time and are influenced by the size of screenings, the quality of the health care system, treatment options, the time taken since the outbreak began and the characteristics of the population such as age, gender and public health.
In late 2019, the World Health Organization (WHO) allocated laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 deaths, and code 07.2 for COVID-19-diagnosed COVID-2 deaths clinically or epidemiologically without laboratory-confirmated infection. The mortality rate of deaths is the number of deaths divided by the number of diagnosed cases over a given period of time.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, the global casualty mortality rate is 6.9% (153,822/2,240,191) as of April 17, 2020.
The number varies by region. Other measures include the CFR mortality rate, which expresses the percentage of those who have been diagnosed and have died due to a disease, and an infection mortality rate (IFR), which expresses the percentage of those infected (those who have been detected and who have not been diagnosed) and those who have died from an illness.
These stats don’t have a time frame, and they follow a specific population from injury to the fate of the case.
Although antibodies may not be produced by the bodies of all infected people, their presence may provide information about the number of infected.
In Italy’s epicenter of the outbreak, Castileone di Ada, a small village of 4,600 people, 80 people (1.7%) have already died.
In Ganglet, the disease spread due to carnival festivals, and spread to young people, causing a relatively lower mortality rate, and perhaps not all COVID-19 deaths were officially classified as the result.
Moreover, Germany’s health system has not been exhausted.
In the Netherlands, about 3 percent probably have antibodies, according to an assessment of blood donors.
69 (0.004% of the population) have been confirmed dead due to COVID-19.
The impact of the pandemic and the resulting mortality rate varies between men and women.
The mortality rate is higher in men in studies conducted in China and Italy.
The maximum risk for men is at the age of 50, with the difference between men and women fading at the age of only 90.
In China, the mortality rate was 2.8 percent for men and 1.7 percent for women.
The exact causes of these sexual differences are unknown, but genetic and behavioral factors may be caused.
Immunological variations based on gender, lack of prevalence of smoking among women and men's simultaneous conditions such as hypertension at a younger age may have contributed to male mortality.
In Europe, 57% of those infected were men and 72% of those who died from COVID-19 were men.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government does not track gender-disaggregated data for COVID-19 infections.
Research has shown that viral diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza and SARS affect men and women differently.
The highest percentage of health workers, especially nurses, are women, and have an increased risk of HIV infection.
The World Health Organization (WHO) announced on February 11, 2020 that the official name of the disease is "Covid-19".
The Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO), Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, explained that Ko is an acronym for Corona, and in an abbreviation of a disease and 19 refers to the year of the discovery of the outbreak for the first time: 31 December 2019.
The name was chosen to avoid referring to a specific geographical location (e.g. China), animal species, or group of people, in line with international naming recommendations aimed at preventing stigmatization. The virus that causes COVID-19 is called Coronavirus 2 associated with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
In addition, the World Health Organization (WHO) uses the terms "COVID-19" and "COVID-19" in public statements.
The disease and the virus are commonly referred to as "coronavirus".
During the first outbreak in Wuhan, China, the virus and disease were commonly referred to as "coronavirus" and "Coronavirus Wuhan".
In January 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) recommended the use of the names Coronavirus-2019 and the acute respiratory disease associated with the novel coronavirus-2019 as temporary names for the virus and the disease in accordance with the 2015 guidelines that prohibit the use of sites in the names of diseases and viruses.
The official names COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were released on February 11, 2020.
Due to limited capabilities in standard supply chains, digital industrial companies export healthcare materials such as nasal wipes and respirator parts.
One example, when an Italian hospital urgently needed a respirator valve, and the supplier was unable to deliver on time, a local startup reversed its geometry and released the required 100 valves in one night.
After the first outbreak of COVID-19, conspiracy theories, misinformation and misinformation emerged regarding the origin of the disease, its scope, prevention, treatment and other aspects and quickly spread on the Internet.
It seems that humans are able to transmit the virus to some other animals.
The study failed to find evidence of virus replication in pigs, ducks, and chickens.
There are no approved drugs or vaccines to treat the disease.
Government institutions, academic groups, and industry researchers conduct international research on COVID-19 vaccines and pharmaceuticals.
In March, the World Health Organization (WHO) launched a ‘solidarity trial’ to assess the therapeutic effects of four existing antiviral compounds that are promising.
There's no vaccine available, but different bodies are actively developing candidate vaccines.
Previous studies on SARS-Coff are used because it resembles SARS-CoV-2 in the use of ACE2 receptors to enter human cells.
Three pollinating strategies are being studied.
First, the researchers aim to create a complete vaccine for the virus.
The use of such a virus, whether inactive or dead, aims to trigger an immediate immune response in the human body against a new infection with COVID-19.
The second strategy, the partial units vaccine, aims to create a vaccine that makes the immune system sensitive to some partial units of the virus.
In the case of SARS-CoV-2, this research focuses on the Spike S protein that helps the virus break into ACE2 receptors.
The third strategy is DNA vaccines (chromosar DNA or RNA vaccines, which is a new technology to innovate a vaccine).
The experimental vaccines resulting from any of these strategies should be tested for their safety and efficacy. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of the vaccine began on four volunteers in Seattle.
The vaccine contains a harmless genetic code copied from the virus that causes the disease. Antibody-dependent rebuke has been cited as a potential challenge facing the creation of the SARS-CoV-2 vaccine, but this is controversial.
There are more than 300 active clinical trials underway as of April 2020.
Seven trials of already approved treatments for malaria have been evaluated, including four studies related to hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Proposed reusing antiviral drugs in the treatment of the new disease make up most of China’s research, with nine Phase III trials on remedicifer across several countries whose results are scheduled to be reported by the end of April.
A dynamic review of the clinical development of potential COVID-19 vaccines and drugs has been carried out as of April 2020. Several existing antiviral drugs for the treatment of COVID-19 are being evaluated, including remedicifer, chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine, lobinavir/retonavir and lobinavir integrated with beta interferon.
There is preliminary evidence of the effectiveness of remedicifer, as of March 2020.
Clinical improvement has been observed in patients who have been treated with remedicivir for compassionate use.
Chloroquine, formerly used for the treatment of malaria, underwent study in China in February 2020 and has preliminary results.
However, there are calls to conduct a peer-to-peer review.
Korean and Chinese health authorities recommend chloroquine use.
However, the Wuhan Virus Research Institute notes that although a dose of one gram has been recommended, double this dose is very dangerous and may cause death.
On March 28, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration issued an emergency use of chloroquine and chloroquine hydroxychloroquine at the discretion of physicians treating those suffering from COVID-19. Version 7 of the Chinese guidelines also includes interferon, ribavirin, or omiphenovir for use for the treatment of COVID-19.
Preliminary data indicates that high doses of ribavirin are necessary to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in the laboratory.
It was recommended that further study of the effect of nitazoxanide on living organisms after proving its inhibition of the low concentration of SARS-CoV-2 was demonstrated. Studies have shown that the initial configuration of the Spike protein by transmembraneous serin protease (TMPRSS2) is necessary to enter SARS-CoV-2 by interacting with the ACE2 receptor.
Chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine studies with or without azithromycin have significant deficiencies that have prevented the medical community from accepting these treatments without further study.Oseltamivir SARS-CoV-2 is not inhibited in the laboratory and has no known role in the treatment of COVID-19.
Cytokine storm may be a complication in the later stages of severe COVID-19.
There is evidence that hydroxychloroquine may have anti-cytokine storm properties.China’s National Health Commission (NHEC) has added tusilizumab to guidelines for treatment after a small study has been completed.
He undergoes a non-randominatory stage test at the national level in Italy after having shown positive results in people with severe medical conditions.
In addition to being used in a serum-serum ferritin test to identify cytokine storms, it aims to cope with such developments, which are believed to be the cause of death in some infected people.
The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has adopted an interleukin-6 receptor receptorant T-cell therapy, based on retrospective case studies to treat cytokine secretion syndrome for non-responsive steroids that have a different cause in 2017.
To date, there is no well-disciplined trespring evidence that tosilizumab is an effective treatment for cytokine secretion syndrome.
The method of transporting purified and concentrated antibodies produced by COVID-19 immunosupplies to those who need them is subject to being studied as non-vaccinative method of passive vaccination.
This strategy was tested on SARS and its results were inconclusive.
Neutralization of the virus represents the expected working mechanism by which passive antibodies therapy can act as an intermediary of defense against SARS-CoV-2.
However, other mechanisms such as antibody-dependent cellular toxicity or phagocytophage, or both, may be used.
Other forms of passive antibodies, such as the use of manufactured monoclonal antibodies, are under development.
The production of the serum, which consists of the liquid part of the recovered patients’ blood and contains antibodies specific to this virus, can be increased to spread it faster.
